var e=JSON.parse("[[1501,\"JavaScript is carried out in the browser's main thread, which is also responsible for the rendering. Lengthy synchronous operations block that thread, freezing the updating of the interface. The browser uses the event loop for the scheduling of the tasks: it carries out the JavaScript, then computes the styles and the layout and paints a frame. So as not to get in the way of the rendering, heavy tasks have to be split into parts or moved out into Web Workers.\",\"The interaction of JavaScript with the browser's rendering is a key aspect of the performance of web applications. Both processes (the execution of the JS and the updating of the screen) take place in one thread, called the main thread. This means that they cannot work simultaneously.\\nThe event loop and the stages of the rendering\\nThe browser manages the tasks by means of the event loop. After the execution of a block of JavaScript code the browser may carry out the stages of the rendering if that is necessary. The main stages:\\n\\n- Style: The recomputation of the computed styles.\\n- Layout (or Reflow): The computation of the geometry and the position of the elements.\\n- Paint: The filling of the pixels (the rasterisation).\\n- Composite: The compositing of the layers for the display.\\nThe browser tries to carry out the rendering at a rate of 60 frames per second. If the execution of the JavaScript takes more than ~16 ms, a frame will be skipped, which will lead to the \\\"stuttering\\\" of the interface.\\nA practical example and the problem\\nLet us look at code that synchronously handles a large array:\\n\\nWhile that loop is being carried out, the browser cannot update an animation, handle clicks or repaint the page.\\nThe ways of optimising\\nTo avoid the blocking, people use:\\n\\n- The splitting into parts (chunking) by means of `setTimeout` or `requestIdleCallback`.\\n- Web Workers for the moving of the computations out into a separate thread.\\n- The minimisation of the operations that cause the recomputation of the layout (layout thrashing). For example, the reading and the writing of geometric properties in one loop forces the browser to recompute the layout many times.\\nConclusion: An understanding of this interaction is critical for the creation of smooth interfaces. JavaScript that is carried out for a long time has to yield control to the main thread so that the browser can render the frames in a timely way. For background or complex computations it is preferable to use Web Workers.\",\"// This function blocks the main thread\\nfunction processLargeArray(data) {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {\\n    // The heavy computations\\n    data[i] = Math.sqrt(data[i]) * Math.random();\\n  }\\n  return data;\\n}\\n\\n// The call will freeze the interface for the duration of the execution\\nconst result = processLargeArray(new Array(1000000).fill(10));\\nconsole.log('Done'); // By this moment the UI had not been updated\"],[1502,\"Cookies are passed through HTTP headers. When the server wants to set a cookie in the user's browser, it sends a response with the `Set-Cookie` header. The browser saves that key-value pair. On subsequent requests to the same domain the browser automatically adds every cookie saved for that domain to the request's `Cookie` header. In this way the server can identify the user and maintain the state of the session.\",\"Cookies are small fragments of data that the server sends to the user's browser for holding. The main goal is the preservation of state between stateless HTTP requests, which lets sessions, personalisation and tracking be implemented.\\nThe mechanism of the passing\\nThe passing takes place in two stages through the standard HTTP headers:\\n\\n- The setting of the cookie by the server: When the server wants to save data on the client side (the identifier of a session, for example), it includes a `Set-Cookie` header in the HTTP response.\\n- The sending of the cookie by the browser: For every subsequent request to the domains and paths specified in the cookie's parameters, the browser automatically adds the data saved to the request's `Cookie` header.\\nAn example of code (Node.js/Express)\\nLet us look at a simple server that sets a cookie on the first visit and reads it on a repeat.\\n\\nThe key parameters of a cookie\\n\\n- Domain and Path: They determine which requests the cookie will be attached to.\\n- Expires/Max-Age: They set the cookie's lifetime.\\n- Secure: The cookie will be sent only over HTTPS.\\n- HttpOnly: It protects against access through JavaScript (it prevents XSS attacks).\\n- SameSite: It controls the sending of the cookie on cross-site requests (protection against CSRF).\\nThis mechanism is widely applied for authentication (session cookies), for the holding of the user's preferences (the theme, the language) and for analytics.\\nConclusion: Cookies are a fundamental mechanism for the maintenance of state on the web. They are worth applying for the holding of small volumes of data on the client that have to be sent to the server automatically — for the management of the user's sessions, for example. For the holding of large volumes of data or of information that does not have to be sent with every request, it is better to use Web Storage (localStorage/sessionStorage).\",\"const express = require('express');\\nconst app = express();\\n\\n// The route for the setting of the cookie\\napp.get('/set-cookie', (req, res) => {\\n    // We set a cookie with the name 'user' and the value 'id12345'\\n    // We add the parameters: Max-Age (the lifetime) and HttpOnly (the protection)\\n    res.setHeader('Set-Cookie', 'user=id12345; Max-Age=3600; HttpOnly');\\n    res.send('Cookie has been set!');\\n});\\n\\n// The route for the reading of the cookie from the incoming request\\napp.get('/get-cookie', (req, res) => {\\n    // The cookies from the request's header are available in req.headers.cookie\\n    const cookies = req.headers.cookie; // For example: 'user=id12345; other=value'\\n    res.send(`Received cookies: ${cookies}`);\\n});\\n\\napp.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on port 3000'));\"],[1503,\"Cookies are small strings of data that the server sends to the browser and that the browser saves and sends back with subsequent requests. A cookie can be set by assigning a string to the `document.cookie` property. Every cookie for the current domain can be read by turning to that same `document.cookie` property. It matters to remember the cookies' parameters, such as the term of validity (expires/max-age), the path and the HttpOnly flag, which forbids access from JavaScript.\",\"Cookies are a mechanism for the holding of small fragments of data on the client side, which the browser automatically sends to the server with every HTTP request. They are widely used for the authentication of users, the tracking of sessions, the holding of preferences (the theme or the language, for example) and targeted advertising.\\nThe setting of a cookie\\nTo set a cookie from JavaScript, you assign a string to the `document.cookie` property. That string has to contain the name, the value and additional attributes, separated by semicolons. Important: the assignment does not overwrite every existing cookie but adds or updates only the one specified.\\n\\nThe reading of cookies\\nEvery cookie for the current domain is available as one string through `document.cookie`. To obtain the value of a specific cookie, that string has to be parsed.\\n\\nThe key attributes and the application\\n\\n- expires and max-age: they determine the cookie's lifetime. Without them the cookie will be a session one and will be deleted when the browser is closed.\\n- path: it specifies the path on the server for which the cookie will be sent. By default it is the current path.\\n- domain: it lets you specify the domain for which the cookie is valid.\\n- Secure: the cookie will be sent only over the protected HTTPS protocol.\\n- HttpOnly: this attribute, set by the server, forbids access to the cookie from JavaScript, which protects against XSS attacks.\\nCookies can also be set by the server by means of the `Set-Cookie` HTTP header in the response, which is the main way for authentication.\\nConclusion: Use cookies when you have to hold data that has to be sent to the server automatically with every request — authentication tokens or the identifiers of a session, for example. For the holding of large volumes of data that do not have to be sent to the server, it is preferable to use `localStorage` or `sessionStorage`.\",\"// The setting of a simple cookie\\ndocument.cookie = \\\"username=JohnDoe\\\";\\n\\n// The setting of a cookie with parameters: the term of validity, the path and the Secure flag\\nconst expiryDate = new Date();\\nexpiryDate.setDate(expiryDate.getDate() + 7); // A cookie for 7 days\\ndocument.cookie = `sessionId=abc123; expires=${expiryDate.toUTCString()}; path=/; Secure`;\\n\\n// The obtaining of every cookie in the form of a string\\nconst allCookies = document.cookie; // For example: \\\"username=JohnDoe; sessionId=abc123\\\"\\n\\n// A function for the obtaining of a value by its name\\nfunction getCookie(name) {\\n  const cookies = document.cookie.split('; ');\\n  for (let cookie of cookies) {\\n    const [key, value] = cookie.split('=');\\n    if (key === name) return value;\\n  }\\n  return null;\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(getCookie('username')); // Will print: JohnDoe\"],[1504,\"The handler passed into Promise.then is a microtask. It is placed in the queue of microtasks after the promise has passed into the 'fulfilled' or the 'rejected' state. The Event Loop carries out every microtask from that queue after the current synchronous task (the macrotask) has finished and before it takes the next macrotask (an event handler or a setTimeout, for example). This guarantees that microtasks have priority over macrotasks.\",\"To understand when `Promise.then` is carried out, you have to get to grips with two key concepts of JavaScript: the Event Loop and the queues of tasks. JavaScript is single-threaded, and the Event Loop manages the order of the execution of the code, using the queue of macrotasks (events, `setTimeout`, for example) and the queue of microtasks (for promises).\\n\\nThe priority of the queues\\n\\nWhen a promise is resolved (fulfilled or rejected), every one of its `.then`, `.catch` or `.finally` handlers is placed in the queue of microtasks. The Event Loop works in a cycle: it carries out the current synchronous task (the macrotask) to the end, then checks the queue of microtasks. If there are tasks in it, it carries out all of them, one after another, until the queue empties. Only after that does the Event Loop take the next macrotask from its queue.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nLet us look at code that clearly shows the priority of microtasks:\\n\\nThe output will be:\\n\\nThe explanation:\\n\\n- First every synchronous line is carried out (1 and 2).\\n\\n- The `setTimeout` callback is placed in the queue of macrotasks.\\n\\n- The promise is resolved immediately, and its `.then` handler lands in the queue of microtasks.\\n\\n- After the synchronous code the Event Loop handles every microtask (it prints 3).\\n\\n- Only then does it take the macrotask from the `setTimeout` (it prints 4).\\n\\nWhere this is applied\\n\\nThis mechanism is critically important for the predictability of asynchronous code. It guarantees that the handling of promises (the updating of state after a fetch request, for example) takes place as soon as possible, before any postponed macrotasks. This is used in the modern frameworks (React, Vue) for the scheduling of the updates of the UI and in libraries for the management of asynchronous flows.\\n\\nConclusion: The `Promise.then` handler is carried out as a microtask right after the current synchronous code has finished and before the next macrotask, which ensures a high priority and a deterministic order of the execution of asynchronous operations.\",\"console.log('1. The synchronous start');\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log('4. The macrotask from the setTimeout');\\n}, 0);\\n\\nPromise.resolve()\\n  .then(() => {\\n    console.log('3. The microtask from the Promise.then');\\n  });\\n\\nconsole.log('2. The synchronous end');\\n\\n1. The synchronous start\\n2. The synchronous end\\n3. The microtask from the Promise.then\\n4. The macrotask from the setTimeout\"],[1505,\"The value of this depends on the way the function is called. On an ordinary call of a function this refers to the global object (in non-strict mode) or to undefined (in strict mode). On a call as a method of an object this points to that object. The methods call, apply and bind let you set the context explicitly. Arrow functions have no this of their own and use the context of the surrounding lexical scope.\",\"In JavaScript the `this` keyword (the context of the execution) is a special variable, which is determined at the moment of the function's call rather than at the moment of its declaration. Its value depends on how exactly the function was called.\\n\\nThe main rules of the determination of this\\n\\n- An ordinary call of a function: In non-strict mode (`'use strict'` is not used) `this` refers to the global object (in the browser `window`, in Node.js `global`). In strict mode the value of `this` will be `undefined`.\\n\\n- A call as a method of an object: If the function is called as a property of an object (through a dot or square brackets), then `this` refers to that object.\\n\\n- The explicit specification of the context: The methods `call`, `apply` and `bind` let you set explicitly what `this` will point to inside the function.\\n\\n- Arrow functions: They have no `this` of their own. Instead they capture the value of `this` from the surrounding lexical scope (from an outer ordinary function or from the global context, for example).\\n\\nPractical examples\\n\\nLet us look at several examples demonstrating those rules.\\n\\nThese examples show how it matters to understand the mechanism of the working of `this`, especially in work with event handlers, callbacks and asynchronous code, in which the context can easily be lost.\\n\\nConclusion: An understanding of the `this` context is critically important for the writing of correct JavaScript code. Use the `call`/`apply`/`bind` methods for the explicit management of the context, and arrow functions when you have to preserve the context from an outer scope (in callbacks or in the methods of classes, for example).\",\"'use strict';\\n\\nconst obj = {\\n  name: 'Test',\\n  regularFunc: function() {\\n    console.log(this?.name); // this points to obj\\n  },\\n  arrowFunc: () => {\\n    console.log(this?.name); // this has been captured from the global scope\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nfunction standaloneFunc() {\\n  console.log(this); // In strict mode - undefined\\n}\\n\\n// 1. The call as a method of the object\\nobj.regularFunc(); // Will print: 'Test'\\n\\n// 2. The call of the arrow function as a method\\nobj.arrowFunc(); // Will print: undefined (since the global this.name is not defined)\\n\\n// 3. An ordinary call of a function\\nstandaloneFunc(); // Will print: undefined\\n\\n// 4. The explicit specification of the context by means of call\\nconst anotherObj = { name: 'Another' };\\nobj.regularFunc.call(anotherObj); // Will print: 'Another'\\n\\n// 5. The loss of the context on the assignment of the method to a variable\\nconst extractedFunc = obj.regularFunc;\\nextractedFunc(); // Will print: undefined (the function has been called without the object's context)\\n\\n// 6. The fixing of the context by means of bind\\nconst boundFunc = obj.regularFunc.bind(obj);\\nboundFunc(); // Will print: 'Test'\"],[1506,\"In JavaScript the number of arguments expected by a function (its \\\"length\\\") can be obtained through the `functionName.length` property. That property returns the number of parameters specified in the function's declaration, up to the first default parameter or rest parameter (`...rest`). To obtain the number of the arguments actually passed inside the function, the pseudo-array `arguments.length` is used. The `length` property is useful for introspection and for the creation of adaptive utilities.\",\"In JavaScript a function is an object and has a built-in `length` property, which indicates the number of parameters expected at its declaration. That value, known as the function's arity, is useful for metaprogramming — in the creation of decorators, in currying or in the validation of calls, for example.\\n\\nHow the length property works\\n\\nThe `length` property returns the number of parameters specified in the function's signature up to the first parameter with a default value or the rest parameter (`...rest`). For example:\\n\\nThe actual number of the arguments passed\\n\\nInside a function you can find out how many arguments were actually passed at the call, using the pseudo-array `arguments` (in ordinary functions) or the `...rest` parameter (in arrow and modern functions). For example:\\n\\nThe practical application\\n\\nThe determination of a function's length is used in various scenarios:\\n\\n- Currying and partial application: Libraries of the Lodash kind check the `length` in order to know when the function has been fully applied.\\n\\n- Validation: You can check whether the minimally necessary number of arguments has been passed.\\n\\n- Decorators and higher-order components: For the adaptive wrapping of functions that may take a different number of parameters.\\n\\nConclusion: Use `function.length` when you have to find out the expected number of a function's parameters for metaprogramming or introspection, and `arguments.length` or `...rest` for work with the arguments actually passed inside the function. This is especially useful in the creation of libraries, utilities or frameworks in which adaptivity to the signatures of functions is required.\",\"function sum(a, b, c) {\\n    return a + b + c;\\n}\\nconsole.log(sum.length); // 3\\n\\nfunction greet(name, greeting = 'Hello') {\\n    return `${greeting}, ${name}!`;\\n}\\nconsole.log(greet.length); // 1 (the parameters after the default value are not counted)\\n\\nfunction logAll(...args) {\\n    console.log(args);\\n}\\nconsole.log(logAll.length); // 0 (the rest parameter is not taken into account)\\n\\nfunction example(x, y) {\\n    console.log('Expected:', example.length); // 2\\n    console.log('Passed:', arguments.length); // It may be 0, 1, 2 or more\\n}\\nexample(10); // Expected: 2, Passed: 1\\n\\nconst arrow = (...params) => {\\n    console.log('Arguments passed:', params.length);\\n};\\narrow(1, 2, 3); // Arguments passed: 3\\n\\n// An example: a decorator for the logging of the arity\\nfunction logArity(fn) {\\n    console.log(`The function ${fn.name} expects ${fn.length} argument(s)`);\\n    return fn;\\n}\\n\\nconst add = logArity((a, b) => a + b);\\n// In the console: \\\"The function add expects 2 argument(s)\\\"\"],[1507,\"The Virtual DOM is a concept rather than an exclusive part of React. It is a pattern of programming in which a lightweight representation of the real DOM is created in memory. Other libraries, such as Vue.js and Inferno, also use that idea for the optimisation of the updates of the interface. The essence is to compare the changes in the virtual tree and apply only the necessary edits to the real DOM, which speeds the rendering up. You can even implement your own simplified version of a Virtual DOM for teaching purposes.\",\"The Virtual DOM (VDOM) is an architectural pattern rather than a technology tied exclusively to React. It solves the fundamental problem of performance in frequent manipulations with the DOM in web applications. Direct work with the DOM through JavaScript (`document.createElement`, for example) is an expensive operation, especially in mass updates. The VDOM proposes creating and manipulating lightweight JavaScript objects, which represent the structure of the real DOM. Then a special algorithm (reconciliation) determines the minimal set of changes between two states of the VDOM and applies only those to the real browser DOM.\\nWhere else is the Virtual DOM used?\\nMany modern UI libraries and frameworks have adopted this concept:\\n\\n- Vue.js uses its own reactive Virtual DOM for the tracking of the dependencies and for efficient updates.\\n- Inferno and Preact position themselves as lighter and faster alternatives to React, also built on a VDOM.\\n- Mithril and Snabbdom are libraries offering a minimalist and modular implementation of a VDOM.\\nAn example of an independent implementation\\nThe simplest implementation helps you understand the essence. Let us look at the creation of a VDOM element and a function for its rendering:\\n\\nOf course, in the real libraries the implementation includes a complex algorithm of comparison (diffing) for the updating of only the parts that have changed rather than the repainting of the whole tree.\\nConclusion: The concept of a Virtual DOM is worth applying when you are developing a dynamic web application with intensive updates of the interface and want to abstract away from manual, potentially inefficient manipulations with the DOM. It is useful not only in React but also as a general pattern for the raising of a UI's performance.\",\"// The function for the creation of a VDOM node (an object)\\nfunction createElement(tag, props, ...children) {\\n    return {\\n        tag,\\n        props: props || {},\\n        children: children.flat() // The children may be strings or other VDOM nodes\\n    };\\n}\\n\\n// The function for the turning of a VDOM object into a real DOM element\\nfunction render(vnode) {\\n    if (typeof vnode === 'string') {\\n        return document.createTextNode(vnode);\\n    }\\n    const element = document.createElement(vnode.tag);\\n    // The setting of the attributes (the props)\\n    Object.keys(vnode.props).forEach(key => {\\n        element.setAttribute(key, vnode.props[key]);\\n    });\\n    // The recursive rendering of the children\\n    vnode.children.forEach(child => {\\n        element.appendChild(render(child));\\n    });\\n    return element;\\n}\\n\\n// The use: we create a VDOM tree\\nconst vdomTree = createElement('div', { id: 'app' },\\n    createElement('h1', null, 'Hello, VDOM!'),\\n    createElement('p', { class: 'text' }, 'This is an example.')\\n);\\n// We convert it into real DOM and add it to the page\\ndocument.body.appendChild(render(vdomTree));\"],[1508,\"On an attempt to convert an object into a string JavaScript looks for the toString() method on the object itself or in its chain of prototypes. If toString() returns a primitive (usually a string), it is used. If not, the valueOf() method is checked. If both methods are absent or return something other than a primitive, an error will be thrown. By default Object.prototype.toString() returns a string of the form [object Object].\",\"In JavaScript the conversion of an object into a string takes place in the context of an explicit (String(obj)) or an implicit coercion of the type — in the concatenation with a string (obj + ''), for example, or in the passing into alert(). The mechanism follows the general rules of the abstract operation ToPrimitive with the hint 'string'.\\nThe algorithm of the conversion\\nWhen the engine has to obtain a primitive value from an object, it carries out the following steps:\\n\\n- If the object has an `@@toPrimitive` method (the symbol method `[Symbol.toPrimitive]`), it is called with the hint 'string' and its result is used.\\n- Otherwise, if the hint is 'string', the `toString()` method is called first. If it returns a primitive value (not an object), the conversion has finished.\\n- If `toString()` is absent or returns an object, the `valueOf()` method is called. If it returns a primitive, it is used.\\n- If both `toString()` and `valueOf()` are absent or return objects, a `TypeError` is generated.\\nPractical examples\\nLet us look at custom behaviour:\\n\\nAn example with the overriding of `toString` and `valueOf`:\\n\\nThe standard `Object.prototype.toString()` method can be used for the determination of an object's type:\\n\\nWhere is this applied?\\nAn understanding of this mechanism is critical for:\\n\\n- Debugging: so that objects are printed to the console or to the logs in a readable format.\\n- Serialisation: a custom `toString` can return JSON or a formatted string.\\n- Work with operators: the prediction of the result of expressions of the `obj + 'text'` kind.\\n- The creation of an API in which objects have to behave like primitives in particular contexts.\\nConclusion: Override `toString()` (or `[Symbol.toPrimitive]`) in your own objects when you have to control their string representation for logging, for the output to the user or for the integration with string operations.\",\"const obj = {\\n  name: 'Test',\\n  // Priority 1: Symbol.toPrimitive\\n  [Symbol.toPrimitive](hint) {\\n    if (hint === 'string') {\\n      return `Object: ${this.name}`;\\n    }\\n    return 42; // for the numeric context\\n  }\\n};\\nconsole.log(String(obj)); // 'Object: Test'\\nconsole.log(obj + '');    // 'Object: Test' (implicit)\\n\\nconst user = {\\n  age: 30,\\n  toString() {\\n    return `User age: ${this.age}`;\\n  },\\n  valueOf() {\\n    return this.age;\\n  }\\n};\\n// In the string context toString is used:\\nalert(user); // 'User age: 30'\\n// In the numeric context (a comparison, for example) valueOf is used:\\nconsole.log(user > 20); // true (30 > 20)\\n\\nconsole.log(Object.prototype.toString.call([]));     // [object Array]\\nconsole.log(Object.prototype.toString.call(null));   // [object Null]\\nconsole.log(Object.prototype.toString.call(new Date())); // [object Date]\"],[1509,\"If an error arises inside a Promise, it will be automatically intercepted and the promise will pass into the 'rejected' state. This can be handled by means of the .catch() method or a try/catch block with the use of async/await. Without handling, the error will become an 'unhandled promise rejection', which can lead to the fall of the application in Node.js or to unexpected behaviour in the browser. Always handle the errors of promises, for the robustness of the code.\",\"A Promise in JavaScript is an object representing the result of an asynchronous operation, which may be carried out successfully (fulfilled) or with an error (rejected). The key aspect is the automatic handling of exceptions inside the promise's executor function.\\n\\nHow errors are converted into the rejection of the promise\\n\\nWhen you create a new promise, the executor function passed is carried out immediately. If in the course of its execution a synchronous error arises (the call of an undefined function or a throw new Error(), for example), the promise's mechanism automatically intercepts that error and converts it into a rejection. This means that the promise will not finish successfully but will pass into the rejected state with that error as the reason.\\n\\nThe handling of errors: the .catch() method and async/await\\n\\nFor the handling of a rejected promise a chain of methods is used:\\n\\n- .catch(): it is attached to the promise and is called if any promise in the chain above was rejected.\\n\\n- .finally(): it is carried out in any case, after the resolution or the rejection.\\n\\n- In asynchronous functions you can use a try/catch around the await.\\n\\nExamples of code\\n\\nAn example with an explicit throw and the handling through .catch():\\n\\nAn example with an asynchronous error inside a .then() and the handling at the end of the chain:\\n\\nAn example of the use of async/await with a try/catch:\\n\\nUnhandled rejections\\n\\nIf an error in a promise is handled by no .catch(), an 'unhandledrejection' event arises (in the browser) or the process may terminate (in Node.js, depending on the version). Always finish chains of promises with the handling of the errors.\\n\\nConclusion: Errors inside promises are automatically converted into rejections, which requires their explicit handling through .catch() or a try/catch. This is a fundamental mechanism for the writing of reliable asynchronous code, preventing the unexpected falls of applications.\",\"const riskyPromise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n  // The synchronous error will be intercepted\\n  throw new Error('Something went wrong!');\\n  // This code will never be carried out\\n  resolve('Success');\\n});\\n\\nriskyPromise\\n  .then(result => console.log('The result:', result))\\n  .catch(error => console.error('An error has been caught:', error.message));\\n// The output: An error has been caught: Something went wrong!\\n\\nPromise.resolve('the beginning')\\n  .then(data => {\\n    // An asynchronous error here will also lead to a rejection\\n    return JSON.parse('invalid JSON');\\n  })\\n  .then(data => console.log('This then will not be carried out'))\\n  .catch(error => console.error('An error of the parsing:', error.message));\\n\\nasync function fetchData() {\\n  try {\\n    const result = await someAsyncOperation();\\n    console.log(result);\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error('An error in the asynchronous function:', error);\\n  }\\n}\"],[1510,\"requestAnimationFrame is a browser API that places a callback in a special queue connected with the cycle of the rendering. The Event Loop manages the execution of the tasks from the different queues: the macrotasks, the microtasks and the tasks connected with the painting. The requestAnimationFrame callback is carried out immediately before the stage of the updating of the styles and the layout (Layout/Paint) in the cycle of the rendering, which ensures a smooth animation synchronised with the screen's refresh rate.\",\"To understand the connection of `requestAnimationFrame` (rAF) with the Event Loop, you first have to remember that the Event Loop is the mechanism that continuously handles the tasks from the different queues. The main phases of the Event Loop include the execution of the macrotasks (`setTimeout`, for example), of the microtasks (promises, for example) and of the tasks connected with the rendering.\\nThe place of rAF in the event loop\\nThe callback passed into `requestAnimationFrame` does not land in the general queue of macrotasks. Instead the browser places it in a special queue of animation frames. That queue is handled at the stage of \\\"Update the rendering\\\" inside the Event Loop. That stage takes place only when the browser is preparing to repaint the screen, usually synchronised with the monitor's refresh rate (60 Hz, for example).\\nThe order of the execution\\nThe typical cycle for a frame with an animation looks like this:\\n\\n- Every microtask is carried out (the handlers of promises, for example).\\n- One macrotask is carried out (a `setTimeout` callback, for example).\\n- The phase of the updating of the render arrives. Here, if the time has come for the painting of a frame:\\n- Every callback scheduled through `requestAnimationFrame` is carried out.\\n- The computations of the styles, the layout and the paint take place.\\n\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at code that animates the movement of an element. The use of rAF guarantees that the update of the position will take place immediately before the browser's paint, which minimises the \\\"jank\\\".\\n\\nIn this example the `animate` function will be called by the browser at the optimal time for the animation. If we had used `setInterval`, the calls could have taken place at unsuitable moments (when the browser is busy with other tasks, for example), which would have led to skipped frames or to an uneven animation.\\nWhere this is applied\\n`requestAnimationFrame` is the standard way of creating smooth animations in web applications. It is used in animation libraries, in game engines on Canvas or WebGL, and also for the carrying out of \\\"heavy\\\" computations that can be split into frames so as not to block the main thread for long.\\nConclusion: Use `requestAnimationFrame` when you need a smooth, performant animation or visual update synchronised with the screen's refresh rate. This lets the browser integrate your code into its own cycle of rendering, avoiding superfluous computations and ensuring a better user experience.\",\"const element = document.getElementById('box');\\nlet position = 0;\\n\\nfunction animate() {\\n  // We update the element's position\\n  position += 2;\\n  element.style.transform = `translateX(${position}px)`;\\n\\n  // We request the next frame of the animation\\n  if (position < 200) {\\n    requestAnimationFrame(animate);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// We start the animation\\nrequestAnimationFrame(animate);\"],[1511,\"Without requestAnimationFrame the animation will be carried out at arbitrary moments in time, not synchronised with the refresh rate of the browser's screen. This can lead to \\\"jank\\\", to skipped frames and to a raised consumption of the processor's resources. The animation may work too fast or too slowly depending on the system's load. requestAnimationFrame guarantees that the function of the update is called before every repaint of the screen, ensuring the smoothness.\",\"requestAnimationFrame (rAF) is a browser API that lets you schedule the execution of a function before the next repaint of the screen. Its main goal is to synchronise the execution of the animation code with the refresh rate of the device's display (usually 60 Hz, that is, 60 frames per second).\\n\\nWhat happens without requestAnimationFrame?\\n\\nIf you use an ordinary `setInterval` or a recursive `setTimeout` for the cycle of the animation, several problems arise:\\n\\n- The absence of synchronisation: The timers are not tied to the browser's cycle of rendering. Your function may be called in the middle of the phase of the rendering or when the browser is busy with other tasks (the handling of events or parsing, for example).\\n\\n- Dropped frames: If the function is called too often, the browser may not manage to paint the changes, which leads to \\\"jank\\\".\\n\\n- The load on the CPU in background tabs: The timers carry on working even when the tab is inactive, wasting the battery's charge and the processor's resources.\\n\\n- An unpredictable speed: On different devices with a different refresh rate of the screen (120 Hz, for example) the animation will work at a different speed.\\n\\nAn example of code: a comparison of the approaches\\n\\nHere is a simple example of the animation of the movement of an element. First the problematic version with `setInterval`:\\n\\nAnd now the correct version with `requestAnimationFrame`:\\n\\nWhere is requestAnimationFrame applied?\\n\\nThis API is used not only for the moving of DOM elements. It is critically important for:\\n\\n- Canvas animations and games\\n\\n- Complex visualisations and charts\\n\\n- Smooth transitions and parallax effects\\n\\n- Animation libraries of your own\\n\\nThe browser automatically optimises the rAF calls: it suspends them in inactive tabs, synchronises them with the VSync and combines several changes of the DOM into one repaint.\\n\\nConclusion: Always use requestAnimationFrame for any visual updates that have to take place smoothly and synchronously with the screen's refresh rate. This ensures optimal performance, saves energy and prevents visual artefacts. Timers (setInterval/setTimeout) are suitable only for tasks not connected with the rendering.\",\"// BAD: the use of setInterval\\nconst element = document.getElementById('box');\\nlet pos = 0;\\nconst intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n  pos += 5;\\n  element.style.left = pos + 'px';\\n  if (pos > 500) clearInterval(intervalId);\\n}, 16); // ~60 frames/sec (1000/16)\\n\\n// GOOD: the use of requestAnimationFrame\\nconst element = document.getElementById('box');\\nlet pos = 0;\\nfunction animate() {\\n  pos += 5;\\n  element.style.left = pos + 'px';\\n  if (pos < 500) {\\n    requestAnimationFrame(animate);\\n  }\\n}\\nrequestAnimationFrame(animate);\"],[1512,\"When the browser meets a `<script>` tag without those attributes, it stops the parsing of the HTML, loads the file of the script and carries it out, and only then carries on building the DOM. The content below the script is therefore not displayed until the script has finished its work, which can noticeably slow the appearance of the page down.\",\"When the browser loads an HTML page, it parses its contents in sequence, building the DOM tree. On meeting an ordinary `<script>` tag without the `async` or `defer` attributes, it immediately suspends the process of the parsing. This is critical behaviour, because the script may contain commands that change the DOM (`document.write`, for example), and the browser has to ensure a consistent state of the tree.\\n\\nThe sequence of the events\\n\\n- The browser finds the tag `<script src=\\\"app.js\\\">`.\\n\\n- The parsing of the HTML is suspended.\\n\\n- The browser begins the loading of the file of the script from the server (if a `src` is specified).\\n\\n- After the complete loading the script is carried out synchronously.\\n\\n- Only after the completion of the execution is the parsing of the HTML resumed.\\n\\nA practical example and the consequences\\n\\nLet us look at a simple HTML page:\\n\\nWhile the browser loads and carries out `heavy-script.js`, the user will see an empty screen. The page's content (the `<h1>`, the `<p>`) will not be painted until the script's work has finished. This leads to a bad user experience, especially with a slow connection.\\n\\nWhere this is applied and how to avoid the problems\\n\\nSuch an approach is necessary if it is critically important for the script to interact with the DOM elements situated immediately before it, or if the order of the execution of several scripts is strictly set. In modern development, however, for the optimisation of the performance it is recommended:\\n\\n- To use the `defer` attribute for scripts that do not have to be carried out immediately. They will load in parallel with the parsing but will be carried out only after the complete building of the DOM, before the `DOMContentLoaded` event.\\n\\n- To use the `async` attribute for independent scripts (counters of analytics, for example), which can be carried out at any moment after the loading without blocking the parsing.\\n\\n- To place non-critical scripts before the closing `</body>` tag, so that the main content manages to be displayed.\\n\\nConclusion: The ordinary loading of a script (without async/defer) blocks the parsing of the page, which can seriously worsen the perceived speed of the loading. Use it knowingly, only when a strict order of the execution and immediate access to the DOM are required. In most cases, for scripts that work with the DOM `defer` is preferable, and for independent ones `async`.\",\"<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n    <title>An example</title>\\n    <script src=\\\"heavy-script.js\\\"><\/script>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n    <h1>An important heading</h1>\\n    <p>This text will appear with a delay.</p>\\n</body>\\n</html>\"],[1513,\"In currying the intermediate arguments are held in the closure of the function returned. Currying converts a function of several arguments into a chain of functions, each of which takes one argument. Every call returns a new function, which \\\"remembers\\\" the arguments passed earlier through the lexical environment. This lets you create specialised functions on the fly, fixing part of the parameters.\",\"Currying is a technique in functional programming that converts a function of several arguments into a sequence of functions, each of which takes one argument. The key point is how the arguments passed at the intermediate steps are preserved.\\nThe mechanism of the holding through closures\\nWhen a curried function is called with an argument, it returns a new function. That new function has access to the argument (or arguments) passed at the previous step, thanks to the closure. A closure is the combination of a function and the lexical environment in which it was declared. That environment contains every local variable that was available at the moment of the function's creation, the parameters of the outer function included.\\nAn example of currying in JavaScript\\nLet us look at a simple function of addition and its curried version:\\n\\nIn this example the value `5` is held in the closure of the function that `curryAdd(5)` returns. Every call of `addFive` uses that preserved value.\\nA universal currying decorator\\nIn practice generalised implementations are used, working with any number of arguments. Here is an example for a function with three arguments:\\n\\nHere the intermediate arguments accumulate in the `args` array, which is preserved in the closure of every function returned.\\nWhere currying is applied\\n\\n- The creation of specialised functions: The fixing of part of the parameters (a logging function with a prefix, for example).\\n- Functional composition: The simplification of the combining of functions into pipelines.\\n- Postponed computations: The building of a computation step by step, when not all of the data is available at once.\\nSummary: Currying uses closures for the holding of the intermediate arguments, which lets you create flexible and reusable functions. Apply it when you have to create a multitude of similar functions with partially set parameters or to build chains of functional transformations.\",\"// An ordinary function\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n\\n// The curried version\\nfunction curryAdd(a) {\\n    // The argument 'a' is preserved in the closure\\n    return function(b) {\\n        // The inner function has access to 'a'\\n        return a + b;\\n    };\\n}\\n\\nconst addFive = curryAdd(5); // '5' is held in the closure of addFive\\nconsole.log(addFive(3)); // 8\\nconsole.log(addFive(10)); // 15\\n\\nfunction curry(fn) {\\n    return function curried(...args) {\\n        // If the arguments passed are enough, we call the original function\\n        if (args.length >= fn.length) {\\n            return fn.apply(this, args);\\n        } else {\\n            // Otherwise we return a new function, which will remember the current arguments\\n            return function(...args2) {\\n                // Every argument (the old and the new ones) is held in the closure\\n                return curried.apply(this, args.concat(args2));\\n            };\\n        }\\n    };\\n}\\n\\n// The use\\nfunction sum(a, b, c) {\\n    return a + b + c;\\n}\\n\\nconst curriedSum = curry(sum);\\nconst addTwo = curriedSum(2); // It remembers 2\\nconst addTwoAndThree = addTwo(3); // It remembers 2 and 3\\nconsole.log(addTwoAndThree(5)); // 10 (2+3+5)\"],[1514,\"The DOM (Document Object Model) is a programming interface for HTML and XML documents, which represents a document in the form of a tree of objects. Every element, attribute and textual fragment becomes a node of that tree. The browser creates the DOM on the loading of the page so that JavaScript can change the document's structure, style and content. Without the DOM scripts could not interact with the elements of a page.\",\"The DOM (Document Object Model) is not a programming language but a standard, cross-platform interface that lets programs and scripts dynamically obtain access to and update a document's content, structure and style. When the browser loads an HTML page, it parses it and builds a tree structure in memory out of the elements, the attributes and the text — the DOM tree.\\n\\nThe structure of the DOM tree\\n\\nThe tree consists of nodes. The main types of node:\\n\\n- The document: the root node, representing the whole document.\\n\\n- Element nodes: the nodes corresponding to the HTML tags (<div>, <p>, for example).\\n\\n- Text nodes: they contain the textual content inside the elements.\\n\\n- Attribute nodes: although they are part of the DOM specification, in most modern APIs they are not represented as separate nodes in the tree but are properties of the element nodes.\\n\\nThe nodes are connected by parent-child-sibling relations, forming a hierarchy.\\n\\nAn example of HTML and of the corresponding DOM\\n\\nLet us look at simple HTML:\\n\\nThe DOM tree for this document will look approximately like this (in a simplified form):\\n\\n- The document\\n\\n- ├── The element: <html>\\n\\n- │ ├── The element: <head>\\n\\n- │ │ └── The element: <title>\\n\\n- │ │ └── The text node: \\\"An example\\\"\\n\\n- │ └── The element: <body>\\n\\n- │ ├── The element: <h1>\\n\\n- │ │ └── The text node: \\\"A heading\\\"\\n\\n- │ └── The element: <p>\\n\\n- │ ├── The text node: \\\"A paragraph with \\\"\\n\\n- │ ├── The element: <strong>\\n\\n- │ │ └── The text node: \\\"bold\\\"\\n\\n- │ └── The text node: \\\" text.\\\"\\n\\nHow is the DOM used?\\n\\nJavaScript obtains access to the DOM through the global `document` object. By means of the methods of the DOM API (`getElementById`, `querySelector`, `createElement`, for example) you can find nodes, create new ones, change their properties and add them to or remove them from the tree. Any change of the DOM tree is immediately reflected in the page displayed (that process is called reflow and repaint).\\n\\nA practical example of code\\n\\nConclusion: An understanding of the DOM tree is critically important for any client-side development in JavaScript, since this is the main way of making a web page interactive and dynamic. The DOM serves as the bridge between the static HTML and a live interface governed by scripts.\",\"<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n    <title>An example</title>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n    <h1>A heading</h1>\\n    <p>A paragraph with <strong>bold</strong> text.</p>\\n</body>\\n</html>\\n\\n// We find the element by its id\\nconst header = document.getElementById('main-header');\\n// We change its textual content\\nheader.textContent = 'A new heading';\\n\\n// We create a new element\\nconst newParagraph = document.createElement('p');\\nnewParagraph.textContent = 'This paragraph has been added through JavaScript.';\\n\\n// We add it to the end of the document's body\\ndocument.body.appendChild(newParagraph);\\n\\n// We change the element's style\\nheader.style.color = 'blue';\"],[1515,\"The sleep function suspends the execution of the current thread or task for the number of seconds specified. This is useful for the creation of delays — in the imitation of long operations or the limitation of the frequency of requests, for example. In synchronous code sleep blocks the thread, which can be inefficient. In asynchronous environments non-blocking analogues are used, so as not to delay the other tasks.\",\"The sleep function is a standard mechanism in many programming languages, which suspends the execution of the current thread of execution for a given span of time. It is useful in situations in which a delay has to be created artificially — for the testing of timeouts, the limitation of the speed of requests to an API or the simulation of lengthy processes, for example.\\nHow sleep works\\nIn synchronous languages such as Python (the time module) or Java (Thread.sleep), the call of sleep blocks the current thread. This means that the thread cannot carry out other work during the waiting, which can lead to the inefficient use of resources, especially in multithreaded or server-side applications.\\nExamples of the use\\nLet us look at a simple example in Python with a synchronous sleep:\\n\\nIn asynchronous frameworks such as asyncio in Python, a non-blocking sleep is used, which lets other tasks be carried out during the waiting:\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Testing: For the imitation of network delays or of slow responses.\\n- The limitation of the speed: So as not to exceed the limits of requests to external services.\\n- The scheduling of tasks: In combination with loops for the periodic carrying out of operations.\\nIt matters to understand the difference between a blocking and a non-blocking sleep. In highly loaded applications it is preferable to use the asynchronous variants, in order to avoid the idling of threads.\\nConclusion: Use sleep for the creation of controlled delays, but in production code, especially in asynchronous or multithreaded systems, choose non-blocking analogues in order to preserve the performance.\",\"import time\\n\\nprint(\\\"The beginning of the work\\\")\\ntime.sleep(2)  # A suspension for 2 seconds\\nprint(\\\"2 seconds have elapsed\\\")\\n\\nimport asyncio\\n\\nasync def main():\\n    print(\\\"The beginning of the asynchronous task\\\")\\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)  # It does not block the thread\\n    print(\\\"1 second has elapsed\\\")\\n\\nasyncio.run(main())\"],[1516,\"On the addition of an eventListener the handler function is registered for the event specified on the element. On the occurrence of the event that function is carried out. The removal of the eventListener cancels that registration, and the function will no longer be called. It matters to remove listeners when they are no longer needed, especially for elements that are removed from the DOM, in order to avoid memory leaks.\",\"Event listeners are functions that \\\"listen\\\" to particular events (a click, a hover of the mouse, input from the keyboard, for example) on DOM elements and are carried out on their occurrence. The mechanism of the addition and the removal is key for the interactivity of web pages and for the management of resources.\\nThe addition with addEventListener\\nThe `addEventListener` method registers the handler function passed for an event on the target element. You can add several handlers for one and the same event on one element. The handler receives the event object (Event), which contains useful information.\\n\\nThe removal with removeEventListener\\nThe `removeEventListener` method removes a handler registered earlier. For a successful removal you have to pass the very same arguments: the type of the event and a reference to the very same function. Anonymous functions cannot be removed, so it is recommended that you use named functions.\\n\\nThe practical application and the important aspects\\n\\n- The management of memory: If an element is removed from the DOM but the listener on it remains, the function and the element may not be freed by the garbage collector (a memory leak). Always remove the listeners before the removal of an element.\\n- The options parameter: In modern JavaScript `addEventListener` takes a third parameter — an options object with settings of the `{ once: true }` kind (carry the handler out only once) or `{ passive: true }` (for the improvement of the performance of the scrolling).\\n- The phases of the event: The third parameter may be a boolean value `useCapture`, determining in which phase (the capturing or the bubbling one) the handler will be called.\\nConclusion: Use `removeEventListener` for the cleanup when the event handler is no longer needed (on the destruction of a component in an SPA framework, for example), in order to ensure the predictability of the application's behaviour and to avoid memory leaks.\",\"const button = document.getElementById('myButton');\\nfunction handleClick(event) {\\n    console.log('The button has been pressed!', event.target);\\n}\\n// We add the listener\\nbutton.addEventListener('click', handleClick);\\n\\n// We remove the listener\\nbutton.removeEventListener('click', handleClick);\\n// Now handleClick will no longer be called on a click.\"],[1517,\"Promise.all waits for every promise passed to it to be carried out. If at least one promise finishes with an error (rejected), then the whole Promise.all immediately finishes with that error, ignoring the results of the other promises (even if they finished successfully). This behaviour is called \\\"fail-fast\\\". To handle the errors without the interruption of the whole process you can use Promise.allSettled.\",\"Promise.all is a method that takes an iterable collection of promises and returns a single promise. That promise returned finishes successfully only when every promise passed finishes successfully. Its result is an array of values in the same order as the original promises.\\nThe behaviour on an error\\nThe key particular feature of Promise.all is its reaction to an error. If any of the promises passed finishes with an error (passes into the `rejected` state), then Promise.all immediately finishes with that same error. This happens without waiting for the results of the other promises. Even if the other promises have already finished successfully, their results are ignored.\\nAn example of code\\nLet us look at an example in which two promises finish successfully and one with an error.\\n\\nIn this example the `catch` will fire as soon as `promise2` is rejected. The results of `promise1` and `promise3` will not be available.\\nThe alternatives and the application\\nSuch behaviour is useful when several asynchronous operations depend critically on one another and an error in one makes the continuation of the handling of the others pointless. In the parallel loading of several obligatory resources for a page, for example.\\nIf you have to wait for every promise to finish independently of its result (for logging or the gathering of statistics, for example), you should use `Promise.allSettled`. It always waits for every promise to finish and returns an array of objects with a description of the result of each (with the status `fulfilled` or `rejected`).\\nConclusion: Use Promise.all when you need a \\\"fail-fast\\\" and every operation has to finish successfully for the further logic. If you have to handle the results of every operation, the unsuccessful ones included, choose Promise.allSettled.\",\"const promise1 = Promise.resolve('Success 1');\\nconst promise2 = Promise.reject(new Error('An error!'));\\nconst promise3 = Promise.resolve('Success 3');\\n\\nPromise.all([promise1, promise2, promise3])\\n  .then(results => {\\n    console.log('Everything is successful:', results); // This block will not be carried out\\n  })\\n  .catch(error => {\\n    console.error('An error has been caught:', error.message); // Will print: \\\"An error has been caught: An error!\\\"\\n  });\"],[1518,\"The async keyword before a function guarantees that it always returns a Promise. This is needed for the uniformity of the work with asynchronous code. Even if the function returns an ordinary value, it is automatically wrapped in a resolved Promise. If the function returns a Promise, it is that which will be the result. This lets you use await inside the function and build chains of asynchronous operations.\",\"The `async` keyword in JavaScript is syntactic sugar over promises, which makes asynchronous code more readable and more like synchronous code. When you declare a function with `async`, you tell the JavaScript engine that that function will work with asynchronous operations and that its execution may be suspended by means of `await`.\\n\\nHow async/await works\\n\\nAny function marked as `async` automatically returns a Promise object. This happens independently of what you return from the function's body:\\n\\n- If you return an ordinary value (a number or a string, for example), the engine wraps it in a resolved Promise (Promise.resolve(value)).\\n\\n- If you return an already existing Promise, it is that which becomes the function's result.\\n\\n- If an exception is thrown in the function, it is converted into a rejected Promise (Promise.reject(error)).\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nLet us look at a simple example demonstrating that behaviour:\\n\\nWhat is this needed for?\\n\\nSuch behaviour ensures the consistency. Since `await` can wait only for a Promise, a function containing an `await` has itself to return a Promise, so that it can be awaited in another async function. This creates a single model for every asynchronous operation and lets calls be combined and chained easily.\\n\\nSummary: async/await requires the return of a Promise from a function in order to ensure the compatibility and the chaining of asynchronous operations; this is a fundamental rule, which makes asynchronous code in JavaScript consistent and predictable.\",\"// An async function always returns a Promise\\nasync function getNumber() {\\n    return 42; // It is automatically wrapped in Promise.resolve(42)\\n}\\n\\n// The equivalent without async\\nfunction getNumberPromise() {\\n    return Promise.resolve(42);\\n}\\n\\n// Both functions can be used identically\\ngetNumber().then(value => console.log(value)); // 42\\ngetNumberPromise().then(value => console.log(value)); // 42\\n\\n// The use of await inside an async function\\nasync function fetchData() {\\n    const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data'); // await suspends the execution until the Promise is resolved\\n    const data = await response.json();\\n    return data; // The value returned is wrapped in a Promise\\n}\\n\\n// The call of fetchData returns a Promise, so we can use .then or await\\nfetchData().then(data => console.log(data));\"],[1519,\"The addEventListener method takes two main arguments: the type of the event and the handler function. The function is passed by reference and will be called on the occurrence of the event specified. You can pass both named and anonymous functions. It matters not to call the function at once but to pass its declaration, otherwise it will be carried out instantaneously rather than on the event.\",\"The `addEventListener` method is the standard way of subscribing to the events of DOM elements in JavaScript. It takes a minimum of two arguments: a string with the name of the event ('click', for example) and the handler function, which will be carried out when the event fires. The passing of the function takes place by reference, which lets you use one and the same handler for several elements or events.\\n\\nHow to pass a function\\n\\nThe key point is to pass the function itself rather than the result of its call. If you put brackets after the function's name, it will be carried out immediately, and the value it returns (often `undefined`) will land in `addEventListener`, which will not work.\\n\\nThe context and the event object\\n\\nOn the call of the handler the event object (`Event`) is passed to it, containing information about what has happened (the coordinates, the target element and so on). The context (`this`) inside an ordinary function passed into `addEventListener` will refer to the element on which the handler hangs. In arrow functions `this` is taken from the outer scope.\\n\\nThe practical application\\n\\nThis mechanism is used everywhere for the interactivity of web pages: the handling of clicks, of the typing of text, of the sending of forms, of the movement of the mouse. It lets you separate the logic of the behaviour from the markup (unlike attributes of the `onclick` kind in HTML). You can also add several handlers for one event for one element.\\n\\nConclusion: Pass the handler function into `addEventListener` by reference, without the call. Use this method for flexible and maintainable work with events in modern web applications.\",\"// Correct: we pass the function\\nbutton.addEventListener('click', handleClick);\\n\\n// Wrong: we call the function and pass its result\\nbutton.addEventListener('click', handleClick()); // An error!\\n\\n// An anonymous function also works\\nbutton.addEventListener('click', function() {\\n  console.log('A click!');\\n});\\n\\n// An arrow function\\nbutton.addEventListener('click', () => {\\n  console.log('A click from an arrow function!');\\n});\\n\\nbutton.addEventListener('click', function(event) {\\n  console.log(this); // the button element\\n  console.log(event.type); // 'click'\\n});\\n\\n// In an arrow function this comes from the outer context\\nbutton.addEventListener('click', (event) => {\\n  console.log(this); // window (if we are in the global scope)\\n});\"],[1520,\"In functional React components the access to the moments of the mounting and the unmounting is carried out by means of the useEffect hook. With an empty array of dependencies [] passed, the effect will be carried out only on the mounting. The function returned from the useEffect will be carried out on the unmounting of the component and serves for the cleanup. In class components the componentDidMount and componentWillUnmount methods are used for that.\",\"In React components pass through various stages of the lifecycle, and the key moments are their mounting (the first render into the DOM) and unmounting (the removal from the DOM). For the carrying out of operations at those moments, such as the subscription to events, requests to an API or the clearing of timers, special methods or hooks are used.\\nFunctional components (with hooks)\\nWith the appearance of the hooks, the standard way is the use of `useEffect`. Its logic is the following:\\n\\n- The effect is carried out after React has updated the DOM.\\n- If an empty array `[]` is passed as the second argument, the effect will be carried out only once — after the first render (the mounting).\\n- The function that the effect returns is the cleanup function. React will call it immediately before the unmounting of the component.\\n\\nClass components\\nIn class components special methods of the lifecycle are used for that:\\n\\n- `componentDidMount()` is called right after the mounting of the component.\\n- `componentWillUnmount()` is called immediately before the unmounting and the removal of the component.\\n\\nThis approach is applied for the management of the side effects that have to exist only while the component is displayed on the screen. The typical cases of use: subscriptions to external services, event listeners, timers and requests for data that can be cancelled.\\nSummary: Use the `useEffect` hook with an empty array of dependencies and a cleanup function returned in functional components for the handling of the mounting and the unmounting. This is necessary for the prevention of memory leaks and for the correct working of an application on the dynamic updating of the interface.\",\"import React, { useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // The code here is carried out on the mounting\\n    console.log('The component has been mounted');\\n    const timerId = setInterval(() => console.log('Tick'), 1000);\\n\\n    // The function returned is the cleanup for the unmounting\\n    return () => {\\n      console.log('The component will be unmounted');\\n      clearInterval(timerId); // The clearing of the timer\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty array of dependencies\\n\\n  return <div>An example</div>;\\n}\\n\\nimport React from 'react';\\n\\nclass MyClassComponent extends React.Component {\\n  componentDidMount() {\\n    console.log('The component has been mounted');\\n    this.timerId = setInterval(() => console.log('Tick'), 1000);\\n  }\\n\\n  componentWillUnmount() {\\n    console.log('The component will be unmounted');\\n    clearInterval(this.timerId);\\n  }\\n\\n  render() {\\n    return <div>An example of a class</div>;\\n  }\\n}\"],[1521,\"The clearing of side effects in React matters for the prevention of memory leaks and errors. Side effects such as subscriptions to events or timers carry on working after the removal of the component from the DOM if they are not stopped. The useEffect hook lets you return a cleanup function, which is carried out before the unmounting of the component or before the next effect. This ensures the correct freeing of the resources and the application's stability.\",\"In React components can create side effects, such as requests to an API, subscriptions to events, timers or the manual changing of the DOM. Those operations are often carried out inside the `useEffect` hook. If the component is removed from the DOM (unmounted) and the effect carries on working, that can lead to memory leaks, to errors in the logic (an attempt to update the state of a non-existent component, for example) or to an unnecessary load on the system.\\n\\nHow the cleanup works\\n\\nThe `useEffect` hook lets you return a cleanup function. That function is automatically called by React in two cases:\\n\\n- Before the execution of the same effect on the next render (if the dependencies have changed).\\n\\n- Before the unmounting of the component (when it is removed from the DOM).\\n\\nThis mechanism guarantees that the resources created by the effect will be freed in good time.\\n\\nExamples of code\\n\\nLet us look at an example with a subscription to the `resize` event of the browser's window:\\n\\nWithout the cleanup function, on every unmounting of the component the event listener would remain active, causing unnecessary updates and potential memory leaks.\\n\\nWhere this is applied\\n\\nThe cleanup of effects is critically important for:\\n\\n- The cancellation of network requests (by means of an `AbortController`, for example).\\n\\n- The stopping of intervals (`setInterval`) and timeouts (`setTimeout`).\\n\\n- The closing of WebSocket connections or of subscriptions to external stores (RxJS, for example).\\n\\n- The cancellation of any asynchronous operations that may try to update the state after the unmounting.\\n\\nConclusion: Always return a cleanup function in a `useEffect` if the effect creates resources that live longer than the component's lifecycle. This is an obligatory practice for the writing of reliable React applications, especially in SPAs, in which components are often mounted and unmounted.\",\"import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction WindowSize() {\\n  const [width, setWidth] = useState(window.innerWidth);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // The side effect: the subscription to the event\\n    const handleResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);\\n    window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);\\n\\n    // The cleanup function: the unsubscribing from the event\\n    return () => {\\n      window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty array of dependencies = the effect only on the mounting\\n\\n  return <div>The width of the window: {width}px</div>;\\n}\"],[1522,\"A custom hook for work with an eventListener lets you manage the subscription to DOM events centrally. It uses useEffect for the addition and the removal of the listener, and useRef for the preservation of a stable reference to the callback function. This prevents memory leaks and ensures the correct cleanup on the unmounting of the component. The hook takes the type of the event, the handler and an optional target element.\",\"Custom hooks in React are functions that encapsulate logic with state and side effects for reuse. A hook for work with an eventListener is a typical example, since the subscription to the browser's events is a side effect requiring a cleanup.\\nThe main logic of the hook\\nThe hook has to take three arguments: the type of the event ('click', for example), the handler (a callback function) and an optional element to which the listener will be bound (by default the window object). Inside the hook useEffect is used for the management of the subscription's lifecycle.\\nThe key aspects of the implementation\\n\\n- Use useRef for the preservation of the handler. This lets you avoid a repeated subscription on every render if the handler has changed.\\n- In the useEffect add the listener by means of addEventListener.\\n- The cleanup function returned from the useEffect has to remove the listener by means of removeEventListener.\\n- The dependencies of the useEffect have to include the type of the event and the target element, so that the hook reacts to their changes.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nThe application of the hook\\nThis hook can be used in any functional component for the subscription to events such as clicks, scrolling, a change of the window's size or keyboard events. For the tracking of the pressing of the Escape key, for example:\\n\\nConclusion: A custom useEventListener hook is useful for the centralisation of the logic of the work with DOM events, for the ensuring of the correct cleanup and for the raising of the reusability of the code in React applications.\",\"import { useEffect, useRef } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction useEventListener(eventType, handler, element = window) {\\n  // We save the handler in a ref, so that it does not cause the effect on every render\\n  const savedHandler = useRef();\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    savedHandler.current = handler;\\n  }, [handler]);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // We check whether the element supports addEventListener\\n    const isSupported = element && element.addEventListener;\\n    if (!isSupported) return;\\n\\n    // We create a wrapper, which calls the handler saved\\n    const eventListener = (event) => savedHandler.current(event);\\n\\n    // We add the listener\\n    element.addEventListener(eventType, eventListener);\\n\\n    // The cleanup function: we remove the listener\\n    return () => {\\n      element.removeEventListener(eventType, eventListener);\\n    };\\n  }, [eventType, element]); // The effect is restarted on a change of the type or the element\\n}\\n\\nexport default useEventListener;\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  const handleEscape = (event) => {\\n    if (event.key === 'Escape') {\\n      console.log('Escape pressed');\\n      // Close the modal window and so on\\n    }\\n  };\\n\\n  useEventListener('keydown', handleEscape);\\n\\n  return <div>Press Escape</div>;\\n}\"],[1523,\"Promise.race is a static method that takes an array (or an iterable object) of promises and returns a new promise. That new promise finishes (is resolved or rejected) with the same result as the first promise from the list passed to finish. This is useful for the implementation of timeouts or when you need the result from the fastest source of data.\",\"Promise.race is a powerful tool for the management of asynchronous operations in JavaScript, letting you react to the result of the fastest of several promises.\\nHow Promise.race works\\nThe method takes an iterable object (most often an array) of promises. It immediately returns a new promise. That new promise is \\\"tied\\\" to the fate of the first promise from the list passed that passes into the `fulfilled` or the `rejected` state. As soon as any of the original promises finishes, the result (the value or the reason for the error) is passed into the promise returned from `race`, and every other promise is ignored in the context of that call.\\nThe practical application\\n\\n- Timeouts for operations: You can create a \\\"race\\\" between a useful promise (a request to an API, for example) and a promise that is rejected after a particular time. If the request takes longer than the timeout, the user will receive an error of the time of the waiting.\\n- The choice of the fastest source: If you have several servers or CDNs providing one and the same data, you can send requests to all of them and use the result from whichever answers first.\\nAn example of code with a timeout\\n\\nIn this example, if `fetchData` finishes before 5 seconds, its result will land in the `then`. If, however, the request takes longer, the timeout will fire and the `catch` block will be carried out with the error \\\"Request timeout\\\".\\nThe important nuances\\n\\n- Promise.race does not cancel the other promises — they will carry on being carried out in the background, but their results will be ignored.\\n- If you pass an empty array, the promise returned will never finish, since there is nothing to \\\"compete\\\" with.\\n- This method is often used in a pair with `Promise.all` (which waits for all of them) and `Promise.any` (which waits for the first successful one).\\nConclusion: Use Promise.race when the time of the response is critical for you and you have to react to the fastest result, especially for the implementation of timeouts or the choice of the optimal source of data in competitive scenarios.\",\"// The useful asynchronous operation (a fetch, for example)\\nconst fetchData = fetch('https://api.example.com/data');\\n\\n// A promise that is rejected after 5 seconds\\nconst timeout = new Promise((_, reject) => {\\n    setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('Request timeout')), 5000);\\n});\\n\\n// The race between the request and the timeout\\nPromise.race([fetchData, timeout])\\n    .then(response => {\\n        console.log('The data has been received:', response);\\n    })\\n    .catch(error => {\\n        console.error('An error or a timeout:', error.message);\\n    });\"],[1524,\"For work with APIs Postman is most often used — a popular GUI client for the sending of requests, the creation of collections and the automation of tests. cURL, a console utility for fast requests, is also often applied. Tools of the Insomnia kind offer analogous functionality. For the documentation and the testing of an API on the basis of the OpenAPI specification Swagger UI is used. These tools help developers check the endpoints, debug the requests and share the configurations.\",\"Work with APIs is an inalienable part of modern development, whether it is the integration with external services, the testing of your own back end or the writing of documentation. For an effective interaction with an API, specialised tools are used, which simplify the sending of requests, the inspection of the responses and the automation of the checks.\\nThe main categories of tool\\n\\n- GUI clients (Postman, Insomnia): they provide a convenient interface for the creation, the saving and the organisation of HTTP requests. They let you configure the headers, the body of the request (JSON, form-data), the parameters of the authorisation and the variables of the environment.\\n- Console utilities (cURL, HTTPie): they are used for fast requests straight from the terminal, often in scripts or CI/CD pipelines.\\n- Tools of documentation (Swagger/OpenAPI): they let you describe an API in a standard format and generate interactive documentation, through which test requests can be sent at once.\\n- Specialised frameworks: for the automated testing of an API in code (Supertest for Node.js or pytest for Python, for example).\\nA practical example: the sending of a request\\nLet us look at how to send a POST request for the creation of a user by means of cURL and in Postman.\\n\\nPostman also lets you create collections of requests, write tests in JavaScript (the checking of the response's status code, for example), use variables and run the collections within CI/CD.\\nWhere they are applied\\n\\n- The development of a back end: the fast checking of new endpoints.\\n- Integration testing: automatic runs of API tests.\\n- Documenting: the generation of a current specification of the API.\\n- Debugging: the analysis of the server's complete response, the headers and the time of the execution included.\\nConclusion: Tools of the Postman and cURL kind are necessary for the everyday work with APIs; they substantially speed the development, the testing and the debugging up, ensuring the reliability of the interaction between services. The choice of the specific tool depends on the task: for one-off requests cURL is suitable, and for complex scenarios with collections and tests Postman or Insomnia.\",\"// An example of a cURL request\\ncurl -X POST https://api.example.com/users \\\\\\n  -H \\\"Content-Type: application/json\\\" \\\\\\n  -H \\\"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN\\\" \\\\\\n  -d '{\\\"name\\\": \\\"John\\\", \\\"email\\\": \\\"john@example.com\\\"}'\\n\\n// In Postman you fill in analogous fields in the interface:\\n// Method: POST, URL: https://api.example.com/users\\n// Headers: Content-Type: application/json, Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN\\n// Body (raw JSON): {\\\"name\\\": \\\"John\\\", \\\"email\\\": \\\"john@example.com\\\"}\"],[1525,\"The removeEventListener method may not remove a handler if the parameters passed to it do not coincide exactly with the ones that were used in the addition through addEventListener. Most often this happens when an anonymous function is passed as the handler, since every call creates a new function in memory. The coincidence of the phases of the event (capturing or bubbling) and of the other options, if they are specified, also matters.\",\"The `removeEventListener` method is intended for the removal of an event handler registered earlier by means of `addEventListener`. For a successful removal it is necessary that every parameter of the call of `removeEventListener` coincide exactly with the parameters of the original call of `addEventListener`.\\nThe main causes of failure\\n\\n- The use of anonymous functions: If an anonymous (or arrow) function was passed in the addition of the handler, it is impossible to preserve a reference to it. Every declaration of such a function creates a new object in memory, and `removeEventListener` will not find a match.\\n- A mismatch of the phase of the event: The third parameter of `addEventListener` may be an object of options or the boolean value `useCapture`, indicating the phase (capturing or bubbling). If on the removal that parameter does not coincide, the handler will not be removed.\\n- A mismatch of the other options: If in the addition an object of options was used (`{ once: true }`, for example), then on the removal you have to pass that same object or the equivalent boolean value for `capture`.\\n- Different contexts (this): With the use of `.bind()` a new function is created. It is precisely the bound function that has to be removed rather than the original.\\nExamples of code\\nLet us look at the typical mistake with an anonymous function:\\n\\nThe correct approach is to use a named function:\\n\\nAn example with the phase of the event (useCapture):\\n\\nWhere this matters\\nThe correct removal of handlers is critical for the prevention of memory leaks in long-lived applications (an SPA in React, for example), and also for the management of the behaviour of dynamic interfaces in which elements may be created and destroyed many times.\\nConclusion: Always preserve a reference to the handler function if you plan to remove it, and watch for the exact coincidence of every parameter in the calls of `addEventListener` and `removeEventListener`.\",\"// Wrong: the handler will not be removed\\nelement.addEventListener('click', function() {\\n  console.log('Clicked!');\\n});\\nelement.removeEventListener('click', function() {\\n  console.log('Clicked!');\\n}); // These are two DIFFERENT functions\\n\\n// Correct: the handler is successfully removed\\nfunction handleClick() {\\n  console.log('Clicked!');\\n}\\nelement.addEventListener('click', handleClick);\\n// ... later\\nelement.removeEventListener('click', handleClick);\\n\\nfunction handler() { /* ... */ }\\n// We add it in the capturing phase (the third parameter true)\\nelement.addEventListener('click', handler, true);\\n// This attempt at removal will not work, since the useCapture is not specified (false by default)\\nelement.removeEventListener('click', handler);\\n// But this will work:\\nelement.removeEventListener('click', handler, true);\"],[1526,\"In functional React components use the useEffect hook. The function returned from the useEffect is carried out on the unmounting of the component. In it call clearInterval, passing the identifier of the interval saved at its creation. In class components use the componentWillUnmount lifecycle method for the call of clearInterval. This is an obligatory practice for the prevention of memory leaks and errors.\",\"In React components can create side effects, such as timers, subscriptions to events or requests to an API. If those effects are not cleared on the removal of the component from the DOM (the unmounting), they will carry on being carried out in the background, which can lead to memory leaks, to errors (an attempt to update the state of a non-existent component, for example) and to unexpected behaviour of the application.\\nThe cleanup in functional components (with hooks)\\nFor the management of side effects in functional components the `useEffect` hook is used. The function passed into `useEffect` can return another function, which will be carried out before the next call of the effect or on the unmounting of the component. This is the place for the cleanup.\\n\\nThe cleanup in class components\\nIn class components the `componentDidMount` method is usually used for the creation of the interval, and the `componentWillUnmount` lifecycle method for the cleanup.\\n\\nWhy this matters and where it is applied\\nThis practice is critically important in any React application in which components are dynamically mounted and unmounted — for example:\\n\\n- Pages with timers or countdowns.\\n- Components that periodically poll a server (polling).\\n- Applications with routing (React Router), in which the components change on a navigation between pages.\\n- Any components using `setInterval`, `setTimeout` or WebSocket subscriptions.\\nSummary: Always clear the intervals and the timers in the cleanup function of the `useEffect` or in the `componentWillUnmount` method. This is an obligatory rule for the writing of clean, safe and efficient React code, which prevents serious errors in the application's working.\",\"import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TimerComponent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // We create the interval\\n    const intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n      setCount(prevCount => prevCount + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n\\n    // The cleanup function: it will be carried out on the unmounting\\n    return () => {\\n      clearInterval(intervalId);\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty array of dependencies = the effect is carried out only on the mounting\\n\\n  return <div>The count: {count}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nimport React from 'react';\\n\\nclass TimerComponent extends React.Component {\\n  constructor(props) {\\n    super(props);\\n    this.state = { count: 0 };\\n    this.intervalId = null;\\n  }\\n\\n  componentDidMount() {\\n    this.intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n      this.setState(prevState => ({ count: prevState.count + 1 }));\\n    }, 1000);\\n  }\\n\\n  componentWillUnmount() {\\n    // We clear the interval on the unmounting\\n    clearInterval(this.intervalId);\\n  }\\n\\n  render() {\\n    return <div>The count: {this.state.count}</div>;\\n  }\\n}\"],[1527,\"After Promise, the async/await syntax appeared in JavaScript, introduced in the ES2017 (ES8) standard. It lets you write asynchronous code that looks and behaves like synchronous code, which substantially improves the readability and simplifies the handling of errors. Instead of chains of .then() and .catch() you use the keyword async before the function and await before the call of a Promise. This does not replace Promise but is a layer over them, providing more convenient syntactic sugar.\",\"After the wide introduction of Promise in JavaScript (ES2015), the next logical step in the development of asynchronous programming was the appearance of the async/await syntax, which was standardised in ES2017 (ES8). This approach is built on top of Promise and does not replace them but provides a more convenient and more readable way of working with asynchronous operations.\\n\\nHow does async/await work?\\n\\nThe `async` keyword, placed before a function's declaration, indicates that that function always returns a Promise. If the function returns a non-Promise value, it is automatically wrapped in a resolved Promise. Inside such a function the `await` keyword can be used. It suspends the execution of the function until the Promise before which it stands is resolved (fulfilled) or rejected. The thread of the execution is thereby not blocked — the control returns to the event loop.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nLet us compare code with Promise and with async/await for the sequential carrying out of two asynchronous operations (the loading of a user's data and then of their posts, for example).\\n\\nWhere and how is it applied?\\n\\nAsync/await has become the de facto standard for the writing of asynchronous code in modern JavaScript applications, especially in Node.js servers and in front-end frameworks (React, Vue, Angular). It is ideally suited for:\\n\\n- Sequential asynchronous operations, in which the result of one step is needed for the next.\\n\\n- The simplification of complex chains or conditions in asynchronous code.\\n\\n- The handling of errors by means of the familiar `try/catch` block.\\n\\nIt matters to remember that `await` can be used only inside `async` functions. At the top level of modules in modern environments `await` is also supported.\\n\\nConclusion: Use async/await when you need clean, linear and easily readable code for work with asynchronous operations, especially when there are sequential dependencies or complex logic for the handling of errors. This is the main tool in the arsenal of a modern JavaScript developer.\",\"// The old style with Promise and .then()\\nfunction getUserAndPosts(userId) {\\n  return fetchUser(userId)\\n    .then(user => {\\n      return fetchPosts(user.id);\\n    })\\n    .then(posts => {\\n      console.log('Posts:', posts);\\n      return posts;\\n    })\\n    .catch(error => {\\n      console.error('Error:', error);\\n    });\\n}\\n\\n// The new style with async/await\\nasync function getUserAndPostsAsync(userId) {\\n  try {\\n    const user = await fetchUser(userId);\\n    const posts = await fetchPosts(user.id);\\n    console.log('Posts:', posts);\\n    return posts;\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error('Error:', error);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The presumed stub functions, returning a Promise\\nfunction fetchUser(id) { return Promise.resolve({ id, name: 'John' }); }\\nfunction fetchPosts(userId) { return Promise.resolve(['Post1', 'Post2']); }\"],[1528,\"The main alternatives to Promise.all are Promise.allSettled, Promise.race and Promise.any. Promise.allSettled waits for every promise to finish, independently of its status (success or error), and returns an array of results with information about each. Promise.race returns the result of the first promise to finish (successfully or with an error). Promise.any returns the result of the first promise to be carried out successfully, and if all of them finish with an error, it returns an AggregateError. These methods give more flexible control over asynchronous operations.\",\"In JavaScript, for work with several asynchronous operations in parallel `Promise.all` is often used, but it has a limitation: if at least one promise finishes with an error, the whole result will be rejected. For more flexible scenarios there are other static methods of the Promise class.\\nPromise.allSettled\\nThis method waits until every promise passed has finished (it does not matter whether successfully or with an error) and returns an array of objects with detailed results. Every object contains the status (`fulfilled` or `rejected`) and the value or the reason for the error. This is useful when you have to handle the results of every operation — in the sending of several independent requests to an API, for example, in which the failure of one should not block the handling of the others.\\n\\nPromise.race\\nThe method returns the result of the first promise to finish (whether a success or an error). This can be used for the implementation of timeouts or for the choice of the fastest source of data. For example, if you have two servers, you can start requests to both and take the response from whichever answers first.\\n\\nPromise.any\\nThis method resembles `Promise.race`, but it returns the result of the first promise to be carried out successfully. If every promise finishes with an error, it will be rejected with an AggregateError containing all of the errors. This is useful when you have several fallback sources and you need the first working one.\\n\\nBesides these methods, you can use a sequential execution with `async/await` in a loop or libraries of the `async.js` kind for complex flows, but the built-in methods cover most cases.\\nConclusion: Use `Promise.allSettled` when you need the results of every operation, `Promise.race` for timeouts or races, and `Promise.any` for the obtaining of the first successful result from several sources. This lets you manage the parallelism in asynchronous code flexibly.\",\"const promises = [\\n  Promise.resolve('Success 1'),\\n  Promise.reject('An error'),\\n  Promise.resolve('Success 2')\\n];\\nPromise.allSettled(promises).then(results => {\\n  results.forEach(result => {\\n    if (result.status === 'fulfilled') {\\n      console.log('Success:', result.value);\\n    } else {\\n      console.log('Error:', result.reason);\\n    }\\n  });\\n});\\n// The output: Success: Success 1, Error: An error, Success: Success 2\\n\\nconst fast = new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve('The fast one'), 100));\\nconst slow = new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve('The slow one'), 200));\\nPromise.race([fast, slow]).then(result => console.log(result)); // The output: The fast one\\n\\nconst p1 = Promise.reject('Error 1');\\nconst p2 = Promise.resolve('Success 1');\\nconst p3 = Promise.resolve('Success 2');\\nPromise.any([p1, p2, p3]).then(result => console.log(result)); // The output: Success 1\"],[1529,\"With the use of setInterval in a useEffect without a cleanup, the interval will carry on being carried out after the unmounting of the component, which can cause a memory leak and errors on a reference to the state of a non-existent component. It also matters to specify the dependencies in the useEffect's array of dependencies correctly, so that the interval is not recreated on every render. For work with the current state inside the setInterval you have to use refs or the functional form of the update of the state.\",\"The use of `setInterval` inside a `useEffect` in React requires particular attention to the component's lifecycle and to the management of the side effects. The main difficulty consists in the fact that the interval carries on being carried out in the background mode even if the component that created it has been removed from the screen (unmounted).\\n\\nThe main problems\\n\\n- A memory leak: If you do not stop the interval on the unmounting of the component, it will carry on working, occupying resources and potentially causing a memory leak.\\n\\n- A reference to state that is not current: The callback passed into `setInterval` \\\"captures\\\" the values of the variables (the state included) at the moment of the creation of the interval. This means that inside the interval you will work with the old (closed-over) values rather than with the current ones.\\n\\n- The uncontrolled creation of intervals: If you forget to specify the empty array of dependencies `[]` in the `useEffect`, the hook will be carried out on every render, creating new intervals, which will quickly lead to a fall in performance.\\n\\nThe correct pattern with a cleanup\\n\\nThe key rule is: always return a cleanup function from the `useEffect`. That function will be carried out before the unmounting of the component or before the next call of the effect.\\n\\nWork with the current state\\n\\nSo as to have access inside the interval to the latest value of the state, you can use a ref (`useRef`) or the functional form of the update of the state (`setState(prev => prev + 1)`). The functional form guarantees that you are working with the current previous value, which React passes into the update function.\\n\\nThis approach is applied for the implementation of periodic actions in an interface: the updating of data (a ticker of shares, for example), the creation of timers, the polling of a server or any other logic that has to be carried out with a given periodicity.\\n\\nConclusion: Always clear the intervals in the cleanup function of the `useEffect` and use functional updates of the state or refs for work with the current data. This pattern is necessary for the creation of reliable React components with timers, avoiding memory leaks and unexpected behaviour.\",\"useEffect(() => {\\n  const intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n    console.log('The timer has fired');\\n  }, 1000);\\n\\n  // The cleanup function\\n  return () => {\\n    clearInterval(intervalId);\\n  };\\n}, []); // An empty array of dependencies = the effect only on the mounting\\n\\nconst [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n    // We use a functional update for the obtaining of the current count\\n    setCount(prevCount => prevCount + 1);\\n  }, 1000);\\n\\n  return () => clearInterval(intervalId);\\n}, []);\"],[1530,\"The use of one and the same reference to a function matters for the prevention of superfluous re-renders in React and in other frameworks. If the function is created anew on every render, React perceives it as a new dependency, which can cause the firing of effects (useEffect) or the repainting of the child components. This leads to a lowering of the performance. For the preservation of the reference useCallback is used, or the function is moved out beyond the bounds of the component.\",\"In JavaScript functions are objects, and on every call that creates a new function (`() => {}` or `function() {}`, for example) a new object with a new reference appears in memory. This is fundamental behaviour of the language, which directly affects the working of modern UI frameworks, especially React.\\n\\nThe problem with new references\\n\\nLet us look at a typical React component:\\n\\nOn every render of `MyComponent` a new `handleClick` function is created. For a React component `ChildComponent` that uses `React.memo` or `shouldComponentUpdate`, the `onClick` prop will be a new object every time, which will lead to an unnecessary re-render of the child component even if its state has not changed.\\n\\nHow this affects the hooks\\n\\nThe `useEffect` hook relies on the array of dependencies. If you pass a function into that array and it is created anew on every render, the effect will be carried out every time, which is often a mistake.\\n\\nThe solution: the preservation of the reference\\n\\nFor the stability of the references people use:\\n\\n- useCallback: It caches the function between renders until the dependencies specified change.\\n\\n- The moving out beyond the bounds of the component: If the function does not depend on the props or the state, it can be declared outside the component.\\n\\n- useRef: You can preserve a stable reference to a function that may change.\\n\\nWhere this is applied\\n\\nThis principle is critically important in React for the optimisation of the performance of memoized components (`React.memo`, `PureComponent`), for the correct working of the hooks (`useEffect`, `useMemo`, `useCallback`) and in the passing of callbacks down the tree of components. It is also relevant in other contexts — in the unsubscribing from events, for example, in which you have to pass the same function as in the subscription.\\n\\nConclusion: The preservation of a reference to a function is a key optimisation for the prevention of superfluous computations and re-renders in reactive applications. It is worth applying whenever a function is passed as a prop into optimised child components or is used in an array of dependencies of hooks.\",\"function MyComponent() {\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    console.log('Clicked!');\\n  };\\n\\n  return <ChildComponent onClick={handleClick} />;\\n}\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  // It is carried out on every render, if fetchData is new\\n  fetchData();\\n}, [fetchData]); // fetchData is a new reference every time\\n\\nconst fetchData = () => {\\n  // The request for the data\\n};\\n\\n// The solution with useCallback\\nconst handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n  console.log('Clicked!');\\n}, []); // An empty array - the function is created once\\n\\n// The moving out beyond the bounds of the component\\nfunction stableLog() {\\n  console.log('Stable!');\\n}\\nfunction Component() {\\n  return <button onClick={stableLog}>Click</button>;\\n}\"],[1531,\"useRef returns a mutable object whose .current property can be changed. A change of .current does not cause a re-render of the component, because React does not track the changes inside the ref object for the purposes of the updating of the UI. Unlike useState, the call of setState marks the component as needing a re-render. useRef is intended for the holding of values that have to be preserved between renders but do not affect the visual output.\",\"The `useRef` hook in React creates a mutable object, which exists throughout the component's whole lifecycle. Its main purpose is to hold values that have to be preserved between renders but whose change should not start a repeated rendering of the component.\\n\\nHow useRef works\\n\\nOn the call of `useRef(initialValue)` React returns the object `{ current: initialValue }`. That object remains the same one (it preserves its referential identity) on every subsequent render of the component. You can read and write the value in the `.current` property. Since React does not track the changes of that object as a dependency for the effects (unlike state), the updating of `.current` does not lead to the scheduling of a new render.\\n\\nA comparison with useState\\n\\n- useState: A change of the state through the set function tells React that the component's data has changed and the UI may need updating. React marks the component as \\\"dirty\\\" and carries out a re-render.\\n\\n- useRef: A ref object is simply a container for an arbitrary value. React does not include it in the system of reactivity. A change of `.current` is a mutation of an ordinary JavaScript object, which does not start React's mechanisms of updating.\\n\\nThe practical application and an example\\n\\n`useRef` is often used for:\\n\\n- The holding of references to DOM elements (through the ref attribute).\\n\\n- The preservation of the values of timers (setInterval/setTimeout).\\n\\n- The caching of previous values or of computations that should not trigger a re-render.\\n\\n- The holding of mutable flags or of the instances of third-party libraries.\\n\\nIn the example `renderCount.current` is incremented on every render, but that does not create an infinite loop, since a change of a ref does not cause a new render. The timer is held in `intervalRef`, which lets it be cleared from any place in the component.\\n\\nConclusion\\n\\nUse `useRef` when you have to hold a mutable value that has to \\\"survive\\\" the component's renders but should not affect its visual representation. This is ideal for side effects, for the integration with an imperative API (the DOM, for example) or for the avoidance of superfluous re-renders when state is not required.\",\"import { useRef, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const renderCount = useRef(0); // It does not cause a re-render\\n  const intervalRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  renderCount.current += 1; // A mutation! Without a re-render.\\n\\n  const startTimer = () => {\\n    intervalRef.current = setInterval(() => {\\n      setCount(prev => prev + 1); // setCount will cause a re-render\\n    }, 1000);\\n  };\\n\\n  const stopTimer = () => {\\n    clearInterval(intervalRef.current);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <p>The component has been rendered {renderCount.current} times</p>\\n      <button onClick={startTimer}>Start</button>\\n      <button onClick={stopTimer}>Stop</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[1532,\"useEffect is carried out asynchronously after the browser has painted the changes on the screen. useLayoutEffect, however, is carried out synchronously right after the computation of the changes in the DOM but before their painting by the browser. This makes useLayoutEffect ideal for operations with the DOM that have to be finished before the visual update, in order to avoid flickering. In most cases useEffect is enough for requests for data or for subscriptions. Use useLayoutEffect only if the effect directly changes the styles or the layout and you see undesirable flickering.\",\"In React the hooks `useEffect` and `useLayoutEffect` let you carry out side effects in functional components, but the key difference consists in the time of their execution relative to the phase of the painting by the browser.\\n\\nuseEffect: An asynchronous execution\\n\\nThe `useEffect` hook schedules the execution of the function passed to it after the component has rendered and the browser has already painted the changes on the screen. This happens asynchronously and does not block the updating of the interface. It is ideally suited for most side effects, such as:\\n\\n- Requests for data to an API.\\n\\n- The establishment and the clearing of subscriptions (to events, for example).\\n\\n- The manual changing of state that does not require an instantaneous reflection in the DOM.\\n\\nuseLayoutEffect: A synchronous execution before the painting\\n\\nThe `useLayoutEffect` hook is carried out synchronously right after React has computed every change in the DOM (the \\\"commit\\\" phase) but before the browser paints those changes on the screen. This blocks the visual update until the effect has finished.\\n\\nIts main application is manipulations with the DOM or the styles that have to be applied before the user sees the result. Without that, visible flickering or a jump of the content may arise.\\n\\nWhen to use which?\\n\\n- Use `useEffect` by default for most tasks: requests for data, logging, subscriptions. This is safe and does not block the rendering.\\n\\n- Use `useLayoutEffect` only when the effect directly changes the appearance of the DOM (the styles, the position, the dimensions) and you observe visual artefacts (the element is displayed first in one place and then \\\"jumps\\\" to another, for example).\\n\\nConclusion: `useLayoutEffect` is a specialised tool for synchronous manipulations with the DOM, necessary for the prevention of visual flickering. In 90 % of cases the standard `useEffect` is enough, which does not block the painting and ensures better performance.\",\"// An example with useEffect: the measurement of an element after the painting\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  // This code will be carried out AFTER the user has seen the element\\n  const rect = elementRef.current.getBoundingClientRect();\\n  console.log('The dimensions after the painting:', rect);\\n}, [dependencies]);\\n\\n// An example with useLayoutEffect: a synchronous measurement and change of the style\\nuseLayoutEffect(() => {\\n  // This code will be carried out BEFORE the user sees the element\\n  const button = buttonRef.current;\\n  const rect = button.getBoundingClientRect();\\n  // We immediately change the style on the basis of the measurements\\n  if (rect.width > 200) {\\n    button.style.fontSize = '10px';\\n  }\\n}, [dependencies]);\"],[1533,\"The useLayoutEffect hook fires synchronously right after React has computed the changes in the DOM but before the browser displays (\\\"paints\\\") those changes on the screen. This lets you carry out operations that require the measurement of the DOM (the obtaining of an element's dimensions, for example) or the synchronous manipulation of the DOM before the painting, in order to avoid visual \\\"flickering\\\". Unlike it, useEffect fires asynchronously after the browser has carried out the painting.\",\"In React the hooks `useEffect` and `useLayoutEffect` serve for the carrying out of side effects in functional components, but their moment of execution in the lifecycle differs, which is critical for particular scenarios.\\n\\nThe sequence of the execution\\n\\nWhen React updates a component, the process can be represented in a simplified way like this:\\n\\n- The render of the component (the call of the component's function, the creation of the React elements).\\n\\n- The comparison of the new and the old Virtual DOM (Reconciliation).\\n\\n- The fixing of the changes in the real DOM (the Commit Phase).\\n\\n- The call of `useLayoutEffect` synchronously.\\n\\n- The painting by the browser (Paint) — the pixels appear on the screen.\\n\\n- The call of `useEffect` asynchronously, after the painting.\\n\\nThe practical application of useLayoutEffect\\n\\nThe main application of `useLayoutEffect` is operations that depend on the DOM's layout and that have to be finished before the user sees the updated interface. The typical examples:\\n\\n- The measurement of the dimensions or the position of a DOM element (by means of `getBoundingClientRect`, for example).\\n\\n- The synchronous changing of an element's styles (an animation or the positioning of a tooltip, for example), in order to avoid visual \\\"jerking\\\".\\n\\n- The programmatic scrolling to a particular element.\\n\\nAn example of code\\n\\nSuppose we have to measure an element's width after the render and update the state on its basis:\\n\\nThe important remarks\\n\\nSince `useLayoutEffect` is carried out synchronously and blocks the browser's painting, it should be used only when that is genuinely necessary for the prevention of visual artefacts. For most side effects (requests for data, subscriptions to events) the asynchronous `useEffect` is enough, which does not block the painting and ensures better performance.\\n\\nConclusion: Use `useLayoutEffect` in those rare cases in which you have to measure or change the DOM synchronously right after the component's update but before the browser displays the changes on the screen, in order to avoid visual flickering. For every other effect the standard `useEffect` is preferable.\",\"import { useLayoutEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction MeasurableComponent() {\\n  const ref = useRef(null);\\n  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useLayoutEffect(() => {\\n    // This code will be carried out BEFORE the painting by the browser.\\n    if (ref.current) {\\n      const measuredWidth = ref.current.getBoundingClientRect().width;\\n      setWidth(measuredWidth); // A synchronous update of the state\\n      // If we had used useEffect, the user might have\\n      // seen the element at its original size and then its change.\\n    }\\n  }, []); // The dependencies\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div ref={ref}>\\n      The width of this element: {width}px\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[1534,\"The memoization of functions in hooks such as useCallback and useMemo can lead to problems if it is applied without need. The main problems include the excessive complexity of the code, a false sense of optimisation when the function is cheap anyway, and potential errors caused by incorrect dependencies. Excessive memoization can also increase the consumption of memory, since the cached values are preserved until the dependencies change. It matters to memoize only expensive computations or functions passed into child components, in order to avoid superfluous re-renders.\",\"Memoization in React by means of the `useCallback` and `useMemo` hooks is a powerful tool for the optimisation of performance, but its incorrect use can lead to the opposite effect. Let us analyse the key problems that may arise.\\n\\n1. Excessive complexity and premature optimisation\\n\\nDevelopers often wrap every function or computation in a `useCallback`/`useMemo`, thinking that that will improve the performance. For simple operations, however, the overheads of the memoization (the comparison of the dependencies, the holding in memory) may exceed the benefit. This complicates the code without any real benefit.\\n\\n2. Incorrect dependencies\\n\\nOne of the most frequent mistakes is an incorrect array of dependencies. If you forget to include a variable that the function depends on, the memoized value will become out of date, which will lead to bugs. For example:\\n\\nHere `fetchData` will always refer to the original `userId`, which will cause errors on a change of the prop.\\n\\n3. Problems with memory and leaks\\n\\nMemoized values are held in memory until the dependencies change. If you memoize many large objects or functions, that can increase the consumption of memory. In the worst case, if the dependencies change often, the memoization becomes useless but the memory is spent all the same.\\n\\n4. A false sense of safety with the re-renders\\n\\n`useCallback` does not prevent the creation of the function — it returns a memoized reference. If you pass that function into a child component wrapped in `React.memo`, that will help you avoid superfluous re-renders. But if the child component is not memoized or the function is used differently, the optimisation will not work.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nLet us look at a component that filters a list:\\n\\nHere the memoization is useful, since the filtering may be expensive for large lists and we avoid repeated computations on every render if `users` and `searchTerm` have not changed.\\n\\nConclusion: The memoization of functions is worth applying knowingly: for expensive computations, for functions passed into memoized child components, or when the stability of the reference has to be preserved (in effects, for example). In the other cases it can add unnecessary complexity and even harm the performance.\",\"const MyComponent = ({ userId }) => {\\n  const fetchData = useCallback(() => {\\n    // userId is not included in the dependencies\\n    api.fetch(userId);\\n  }, []); // An empty array - the function will never be updated\\n  // ...\\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useState, useMemo } from 'react';\\n\\nconst UserList = ({ users, searchTerm }) => {\\n  const filteredUsers = useMemo(() => {\\n    console.log('Filtering...');\\n    return users.filter(user => \\n      user.name.toLowerCase().includes(searchTerm.toLowerCase())\\n    );\\n  }, [users, searchTerm]); // The correct dependencies\\n\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {filteredUsers.map(user => (\\n        <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// If the array of dependencies had been [users], then on a change of searchTerm\\n// the list would not have been updated, which is an error.\"],[1535,\"React repaints a component when its state or the properties (props) it receives change. It creates a new virtual DOM tree and compares it with the previous one (the process of reconciliation). Then React computes the minimal set of changes and applies them to the real DOM. This lets the interface be updated efficiently.\",\"An update (a re-render) in React is the process in which a component recreates its JSX representation in response to changes. The main triggers for that are a change of the component's internal state (through `setState` or the `useState` hook) or the receiving of new properties (props) from the parent component.\\n\\nHow the repainting is started\\n\\nWhen a function for the update of the state is called (`setCount`, for example), React marks the component as needing an update. This does not mean an instantaneous change of the DOM. React schedules the update and carries it out later, usually within one cycle of the event loop, which lets several updates be grouped for efficiency.\\n\\nThe process of reconciliation\\n\\nAfter the component has been marked for the update, React calls its rendering function (the render method or the body of the functional component). That function returns a new tree of React elements (the virtual DOM). Then React compares that new tree with the previous one, preserved in memory. That process is called reconciliation.\\n\\nThe key stages of the reconciliation\\n\\n- The diffing algorithm: React recursively compares the elements at every level of the tree. If the type of an element (a `<div>` or another component, for example) has changed, React destroys the old subtree and builds a new one.\\n\\n- The keys: In the rendering of lists the keys help React identify which elements have been changed, added or deleted. This is critically important for the performance.\\n\\n- The application of the changes: After the computation of the difference (the diff) React brings the minimally necessary changes into the browser's real DOM tree.\\n\\nAn example of code\\n\\nLet us look at a simple counter component:\\n\\nEvery click on the button calls `setCount`, which leads to a change of the state. React schedules the update of the `Counter` component, carries out its body anew, compares the result with the previous virtual DOM and updates only the text inside the `<p>` tag in the real DOM.\\n\\nThe optimisation of the repaintings\\n\\nNot every change of the state or of the props has to lead to the repainting of the whole subtree. For the optimisation the following are used:\\n\\n- `React.memo` for the memoization of functional components.\\n\\n- `useMemo` and `useCallback` for the memoization of values and functions, in order to prevent superfluous updates of the child components.\\n\\n- The correct use of the keys in lists.\\n\\nConclusion: The mechanism of the re-render in React, founded on the virtual DOM and on reconciliation, ensures a declarative and efficient way of updating a user interface. An understanding of this process is necessary for the writing of performant React applications and for the correct optimisation of components.\",\"import { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  // This function causes the re-render\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    setCount(count + 1); // The change of the state -> the scheduling of the update\\n  };\\n\\n  // The component's body is carried out on every re-render\\n  console.log('The component has been painted, count =', count);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>You have pressed {count} times</p>\\n      <button onClick={handleClick}>Press me</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[1536,\"The problem arises when a function is passed as a handler and its `this` context is lost, pointing to the global object or to `undefined`. To fix that you can use an arrow function, which has no `this` of its own and inherits it from the surrounding lexical context. You can also bind the context by means of the `bind` method, creating a new function with a rigidly set `this`. Another variant is to pass the function as a method of an object, but that is less flexible. These approaches guarantee that `this` inside the handler will refer to the object you need.\",\"In JavaScript the context of a function's execution, determined by the `this` keyword, can change depending on how the function is called. The problem especially often arises with the use of functions as event handlers — in React or in the native DOM, for example. When a function is passed as a callback, it may lose the binding to the original object, and `this` begins to point to the global object (in non-strict mode) or to `undefined` (in strict mode), which leads to errors.\\nThe main causes of the problem\\n\\n- The function is called not as a method of an object but as an independent function.\\n- The passing of a method as a callback — into `addEventListener` or `setTimeout`, for example.\\n- The use of ES6 classes, in which the methods are by default not bound to the instance.\\nThe ways of solving it\\nThere are several widespread approaches for the fixing of the context:\\n\\n- Arrow functions: They have no `this` of their own, so they use the value from the surrounding scope. This is the most modern and most convenient way in ES6+.\\n- The bind() method: It lets you create a new function with a bound context, which will not change on subsequent calls.\\n- The binding in the constructor: In classes you can bind the methods in the constructor, in order to avoid the loss of the context.\\n- The use of a class field as an arrow function: In ES6+ classes you can define a method as a class field with an arrow function, which automatically binds the context to the instance.\\nExamples of code\\nLet us look at an example with a class and an event handler:\\n\\nThe use of an arrow function as a class field:\\n\\nOr on the call it can be wrapped in an arrow function:\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nThis problem is relevant in any JavaScript code in which event handlers are used, especially in React components (both class ones and functional ones with hooks), in work with the DOM API, and also in asynchronous operations such as timers or requests to a server. An understanding of the context is necessary for the writing of stable and predictable code.\\nConclusion: The fixing of the problem with the `this` context matters for the correct working of handlers. Arrow functions are the most modern and most concise way, while `bind` remains a reliable solution for compatibility. The choice of the method depends on the style of the code and on the project's requirements, but it always matters to manage the context explicitly, in order to avoid errors that are hard to catch.\",\"class Button {\\n  constructor() {\\n    this.count = 0;\\n    // Way 1: The binding in the constructor\\n    this.handleClick = this.handleClick.bind(this);\\n  }\\n  \\n  handleClick() {\\n    this.count++;\\n    console.log(`Clicked ${this.count} times`);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst btn = new Button();\\n// Without the bind this will break, since this will be undefined\\ndocument.addEventListener('click', btn.handleClick);\\n\\nclass Button {\\n  count = 0;\\n  \\n  // Way 2: An arrow function as a class field\\n  handleClick = () => {\\n    this.count++;\\n    console.log(`Clicked ${this.count} times`);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst btn = new Button();\\n// The context is preserved, since handleClick is an arrow function\\ndocument.addEventListener('click', btn.handleClick);\\n\\nclass Button {\\n  count = 0;\\n  \\n  handleClick() {\\n    this.count++;\\n    console.log(`Clicked ${this.count} times`);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst btn = new Button();\\n// Way 3: A wrapper in an arrow function on the passing\\ndocument.addEventListener('click', () => btn.handleClick());\"],[1537,\"Asynchronicity lets a program not be blocked during the waiting for slow operations of input and output, such as the reading of files or network requests. Instead of idling, the thread can carry out other tasks, which raises the system's overall throughput. This especially matters for IO-bound tasks, in which the main time goes on the waiting for external resources rather than on computations. In this way one thread can handle a multitude of operations in parallel, using the resources efficiently.\",\"Asynchronous programming is an approach in which the program's execution is not blocked for the duration of the waiting for the completion of operations of input and output (IO), such as queries to a database, the reading of files or network calls. Instead of waiting for the response, the program can pass control to other tasks, and when the operation has finished, receive a notification and handle the result.\\n\\nHow this works\\n\\nAt the foundation of asynchronicity there often lies the mechanism of the event loop. The program registers the IO operations and the callbacks (or promises/asynchronous functions) that will be called when they are ready. While one operation waits for the data from the disk or the network, the event loop can handle other events or carry out code that is ready for work. This lets one thread (in Node.js or in Python with asyncio, for example) serve a multitude of simultaneous connections.\\n\\nWhy it is better for IO-bound tasks\\n\\n- The efficiency of the resources: IO-bound tasks spend most of their time on waiting rather than on computations. A blocking thread simply \\\"sleeps\\\", spending memory and the processor's time on the switching of the context. The asynchronous approach uses that time for useful work.\\n\\n- Scalability: One thread can handle thousands of network connections simultaneously, which is critical for web servers. The creation of a separate thread or process for every connection (as in the synchronous models) loads the system.\\n\\n- Responsiveness: Applications remain responsive, since they do not block the main thread for long IO operations.\\n\\nAn example in Python with asyncio\\n\\nIn this example three virtual network requests are carried out concurrently. The overall time of the execution will be around 2 seconds rather than 6, as in sequential blocking code.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\nAsynchronicity is widely used in web servers (Node.js, FastAPI, aiohttp), in clients for databases, in the parsing of data, in chatbots and in any applications with intensive IO. For CPU-bound tasks (heavy computations), however, it gives no advantages and may even worsen the performance because of the overheads.\\n\\nConclusion: Asynchronicity is worth applying when your application often waits for external resources (the network, the disk, an API). This lets you substantially increase the throughput and the responsiveness, especially in server-side and network applications, without an increase in the number of threads or processes.\",\"import asyncio\\n\\nasync def fetch_data(url: str):\\n    # The imitation of a network request\\n    print(f\\\"The beginning of the request to {url}\\\")\\n    await asyncio.sleep(2)  # A non-blocking wait\\n    print(f\\\"The data from {url} has been received\\\")\\n    return f\\\"the result from {url}\\\"\\n\\nasync def main():\\n    # We start several IO operations \\\"in parallel\\\"\\n    tasks = [\\n        fetch_data(\\\"https://api.example.com/data1\\\"),\\n        fetch_data(\\\"https://api.example.com/data2\\\"),\\n        fetch_data(\\\"https://api.example.com/data3\\\")\\n    ]\\n    results = await asyncio.gather(*tasks)\\n    print(f\\\"Every result: {results}\\\")\\n\\n# The running of the asynchronous application\\nasyncio.run(main())\"],[1538,\"The main way of protection is the use of parameterised queries (prepared statements), in which the data is passed separately from the SQL command. It also matters to carry out the validation and the sanitisation (escaping) of the user's input. The use of an ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) automatically protects against many injections, since it generates safe queries. In addition you can apply the principle of minimal privileges for the accounts of the database.\",\"An SQL injection is an attack in which an attacker inserts or \\\"injects\\\" malicious SQL code through the user's input, which is then carried out by the database. This can lead to the theft, the change or the deletion of data. The protection is built on the separation of the code (the SQL commands) and the data (the user's input).\\n1. Parameterised queries (prepared statements)\\nThis is the most effective method. The place for the data in the query is denoted by a parameter (`?` or `@name`, for example). The database's driver guarantees that the value passed will be handled only as data rather than as part of the SQL command.\\n\\n2. The use of an ORM\\nORM libraries (SQLAlchemy for Python, Hibernate for Java, Sequelize for Node.js, for example) automatically generate parameterised queries, which minimises the risk of a developer's mistake.\\n\\n3. The validation and the sanitisation of the input\\nAlthough this does not replace parameterised queries, validation is useful as an additional barrier.\\n\\n- Whitelists: Permit only particular, expected characters (only digits for an ID, for example).\\n- Escaping: Use the functions of escaping provided by the database's driver (`mysqli_real_escape_string()` in PHP, for example), but it is unsafe to rely on them alone.\\n4. The other measures at the level of the application and of the database\\n\\n- The principle of minimal privileges: The application's account should not have the rights to delete tables or to carry out administrative commands.\\n- Stored procedures: They can help, but only if they are correctly implemented with parameters.\\n- The regular updating of the software: Update the database's drivers and the frameworks in order to eliminate the known vulnerabilities.\\nConclusion: Parameterised queries (prepared statements) are the obligatory and main method of protection against SQL injections at the level of the code. An ORM provides a convenient and safe abstraction. The validation of the input and the configuration of the database's rights serve as important additional layers of security.\",\"// An example in Python with sqlite3\\nimport sqlite3\\nconn = sqlite3.connect('test.db')\\ncursor = conn.cursor()\\n# WRONG: a vulnerable query\\n# user_input = \\\"' OR '1'='1\\\"\\n# cursor.execute(f\\\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE login = '{user_input}'\\\")\\n# RIGHT: a parameterised query\\nuser_input = \\\"' OR '1'='1\\\"\\ncursor.execute(\\\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE login = ?\\\", (user_input,))\\n# The driver will substitute the value safely; the injection will not work.\\n\\n# An example with SQLAlchemy (Python)\\nfrom sqlalchemy import create_engine, text\\nengine = create_engine('sqlite:///test.db')\\nwith engine.connect() as conn:\\n    # A safe query through text() and parameters\\n    stmt = text(\\\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE login = :login\\\")\\n    result = conn.execute(stmt, {'login': user_input})\"],[1539,\"An SQL injection is an attack in which an attacker inserts malicious SQL code into an application's queries in order to obtain unauthorised access to a database or to manipulate it. This happens when the application incorrectly handles the user's input, joining it with the SQL query directly. To combat this you should use parameterised queries (prepared statements), which separate the data from the code of the query. It also matters to carry out the validation and the sanitisation of the input data, to apply the principle of minimal privileges for the database's accounts and to carry out an audit of the code's security regularly.\",\"An SQL injection is one of the most widespread and most dangerous vulnerabilities of web applications. It lets an attacker interfere with the queries that the application sends to the database. This can lead to the theft, the change or the deletion of data, to the bypassing of the authentication and even to complete control over the database's server.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\n\\nThe problem arises when the application concatenates (glues) the strings entered by the user with the SQL query without checking or escaping them. Let us look at an example of vulnerable code in Python with the use of SQLite:\\n\\nThe main methods of protection\\n\\n- Parameterised queries (prepared statements): The most effective way. The DBMS's mechanism itself distinguishes the code of the query and the data passed, which excludes the possibility of their interpretation as commands.\\n\\n- The validation and the sanitisation of the input: Check that the data entered corresponds to the format expected (an email, a number, for example).\\n\\n- The principle of minimal privileges: The application's account for work with the database should have only the necessary rights (only SELECT and INSERT but not DROP, for example).\\n\\n- The escaping of the special characters: Use the functions of escaping provided by the framework or the library (but this is less reliable than parameterised queries).\\n\\nAn example of safe code\\n\\nThe corrected variant with the use of a parameterised query:\\n\\nConclusion: Parameterised queries are the obligatory and most reliable practice for the prevention of SQL injections. They should always be applied in work with dynamic SQL queries, especially when data entered by the user takes part in them. This is a fundamental skill for any developer who works with databases.\",\"import sqlite3\\n\\n# A vulnerable function for the authentication of a user\\ndef unsafe_login(username, password):\\n    conn = sqlite3.connect('app.db')\\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\\n    # The user's input is substituted into the query directly!\\n    query = f\\\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name='{username}' AND password='{password}'\\\"\\n    cursor.execute(query)\\n    return cursor.fetchone()\\n\\n# If an attacker enters in the field of the password: ' OR '1'='1\\n# The resulting query will become:\\n# SELECT * FROM users WHERE name='admin' AND password='' OR '1'='1'\\n# The condition '1'='1' is always true, so the query will return every user, bypassing the check of the password.\\n\\ndef safe_login(username, password):\\n    conn = sqlite3.connect('app.db')\\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\\n    # We use a parameterised query. ? is a placeholder for the data.\\n    query = \\\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name=? AND password=?\\\"\\n    cursor.execute(query, (username, password))  # The data is passed as a separate argument\\n    return cursor.fetchone()\\n# Now, whatever characters the user enters, they will be handled as data rather than as part of the SQL code.\"],[1540,\"An asynchronous function can be run in several ways. The simplest is to call it directly, but since it returns a promise, the result has to be handled. You can use the await keyword inside another async function. You can also use the .then() method for the handling of the promise's result. It matters to remember that a call without an await or a .then() will carry the function out, but you will not obtain its result.\",\"An asynchronous function in JavaScript always returns a Promise object. This means that its call initiates an asynchronous operation whose result will be available in the future. An understanding of the ways of running it is critical for correct work with asynchronous code, for the avoidance of the blocking of the main thread and for the handling of the possible errors.\\nThe main ways of running it\\n\\n- A direct call: The function will be carried out, but the promise returned will be in the pending state until the operation has finished. To obtain the result you have to attach handlers.\\n- The use of .then() and .catch(): This is the classic approach to work with promises. The .then() method handles the successful execution and .catch() the errors.\\n- The use of await: The await keyword can be used only inside another async function. It suspends the execution of the current function until the promise is resolved, returning its result.\\nPractical examples\\nLet us look at an asynchronous function that imitates a delay.\\n\\nThe first way (.then()) is well suited for chains of promises or when the execution of the surrounding code does not have to be suspended. The second way (await) makes the code more linear and more readable, especially with several sequential asynchronous calls.\\nWhere it is applied\\nAsynchronous functions and their running are used everywhere in work with APIs (fetch), with the file system (in Node.js), with timers, with databases and with any other operations of input and output that may take time.\\nConclusion: Use await inside async functions for the writing of clean, sequential code. Use .then() for a more flexible composition of promises or in environments in which the async/await syntax is not available. Always handle the possible errors by means of a try...catch or a .catch().\",\"async function fetchData() {\\n  // The imitation of an asynchronous operation\\n  return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve('The data has been received'), 1000));\\n}\\n\\n// Way 1: The use of .then()\\nfetchData().then(result => {\\n  console.log(result); // 'The data has been received'\\n}).catch(error => {\\n  console.error(error);\\n});\\n\\n// Way 2: The use of await inside an async IIFE (an immediately invoked function)\\n(async () => {\\n  try {\\n    const result = await fetchData();\\n    console.log(result); // 'The data has been received'\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error(error);\\n  }\\n})();\\n\\n// Way 3: A simple call (not recommended, since the result is lost)\\n// fetchData(); // The promise has been created but not handled.\"],[1541,\"A palindrome is a word, a phrase, a number or another sequence of characters that reads the same from left to right and from right to left, if you ignore the spaces, the punctuation marks and the case. For example, the words \\\"radar\\\" and \\\"level\\\" are palindromes. In programming the task of the checking for a palindrome is often solved by means of two pointers, which move from the ends of the string towards the centre, comparing the characters. This is a basic task, which helps you understand the work with strings and simple algorithms.\",\"A palindrome is a fundamental concept in computer science and in linguistics, denoting a symmetrical sequence. In programming the task of the determination of a palindrome is often met with in interviews and in teaching courses, since it tests the ability to work with strings, indexes and basic algorithms without the use of complex data structures.\\nThe main concept\\nA palindrome is considered to be a string that remains unchanged on being read backwards. Formally, for a string `s` of length `n`, the condition `s[i] == s[n - 1 - i]` has to be met for every `i` from `0` to `⌊n/2⌋`. In practice the strings are often cleaned beforehand: they are converted to one case and the spaces and the non-alphanumeric characters are removed.\\nThe typical approaches to the solution\\n\\n- The method of two pointers: the most efficient in memory (O(1) additional memory) and in time (O(n)). The pointers start from the beginning and the end of the string, compare the characters and move towards the centre.\\n- The comparison with the reversed string: create a reversed copy of the string (by means of `reverse()`, for example) and compare it with the original. Simple, but it uses O(n) additional memory.\\nAn example of code in Python\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nBesides academic tasks, the checking of palindromes can be met with in systems of the checking of input (for the validation of serial numbers, for example), in simple games or puzzles, and also as part of more complex algorithms (the search for palindromic substrings, for example).\\nConclusion: The task of palindromes is an excellent way of assessing the basic understanding of string operations and of algorithmic thinking in beginning developers. It is worth applying as a warm-up or as the first task in a series on algorithms.\",\"def is_palindrome(s: str) -> bool:\\n    # The cleaning of the string: the lower case, only letters and digits\\n    cleaned = ''.join(ch.lower() for ch in s if ch.isalnum())\\n    # The method of two pointers\\n    left, right = 0, len(cleaned) - 1\\n    while left < right:\\n        if cleaned[left] != cleaned[right]:\\n            return False\\n        left += 1\\n        right -= 1\\n    return True\\n\\n# Examples of the use\\nprint(is_palindrome(\\\"A man, a plan, a canal: Panama\\\"))  # True\\nprint(is_palindrome(\\\"race a car\\\"))  # False\"],[1542,\"It is better to catch specific exceptions because that lets you determine the type of the error precisely and handle it correspondingly. If you catch a general exception (`Exception`, for example), you can hide unforeseen errors, which will complicate the debugging. Specific handling makes code more predictable and more robust, since different actions can be provided for different failures — logging or an attempt at recovery, for example.\",\"The handling of exceptions is a mechanism that lets a program react correctly to errors during the execution without terminating abnormally. The key principle is to intercept only those exceptions that you expect and know how to handle.\\nThe problem with a general interception\\nThe use of a general `except Exception` block or, which is even worse, of an `except:` (which catches absolutely everything, system events of the KeyboardInterrupt kind included) is an anti-pattern. It hides the true nature of the error. For example, if in a block of code an error of division by zero (ZeroDivisionError) or an error of a reference to a non-existent key (KeyError) takes place, the general handler will intercept them but will not let you understand what exactly went wrong, and it may carry out an incorrect logic of recovery.\\nThe advantages of specific exceptions\\n\\n- Clarity and debugging: You know exactly what error has taken place and can write it into the log with a comprehensible context.\\n- Pinpoint recovery: For different errors different strategies can be provided. For a `FileNotFoundError`, for example, create the file; for a `ConnectionError`, repeat the request.\\n- The prevention of hidden bugs: Unexpected errors (errors in the program's own logic, for example) will not be \\\"swallowed\\\" and will let the developer discover them fast.\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at code that tries to import a module and read a configuration file.\\n\\nIn this example we clearly separate the handling of the absence of the file and of an incorrect format of the data. An error of the rights of access (PermissionError) will not be intercepted and the program will fall over, which will point to a problem with the environment at once.\\nConclusion\\nCatching specific exceptions is worth it for the creation of robust and easily debuggable code. This approach lets a program recover correctly after expected failures without masking the more serious, unforeseen errors that require the developer's attention.\",\"import json\\n\\ndef load_config(config_path):\\n    config = {}\\n    try:\\n        # We try to open and read the file\\n        with open(config_path, 'r') as f:\\n            config = json.load(f)\\n    except FileNotFoundError:\\n        # We handle only the case of the absence of the file\\n        print(f\\\"Config file {config_path} not found. Using defaults.\\\")\\n        config = {\\\"default\\\": True}\\n    except json.JSONDecodeError as e:\\n        # We handle only the error of the parsing of the JSON\\n        print(f\\\"Invalid JSON in config: {e}. Using empty config.\\\")\\n    # The other exceptions (PermissionError, for example) will not be intercepted here\\n    # and will \\\"float\\\" upwards, which is correct, since those are critical failures.\\n    return config\\n\\n# An example with an ImportError\\ntry:\\n    import some_optional_module\\nexcept ImportError:\\n    print(\\\"Optional module not found. Running in limited mode.\\\")\\n    some_optional_module = None\"],[1543,\"Yes, threads can be started from synchronous code. Synchronous code is carried out in sequence, blocking the main thread. To carry tasks out in parallel you can create and start a new thread, which will work independently. This lets you, for example, carry out long computations or operations of input and output without blocking the main interface. After the start the main thread can carry on with its execution while the new thread works asynchronously.\",\"The concept of the starting of threads from synchronous code is fundamental for multithreaded programming. Synchronous code is carried out line by line, one operation after another, in one thread of execution. In many programming languages, however, there are APIs for the creation and the management of threads, which let code be carried out in parallel or concurrently.\\n\\nHow this works\\n\\nWhen you are inside a synchronous function or method, you can call a special function (`Thread.start()` in Java or `threading.Thread().start()` in Python, for example) in order to create a new thread of execution. That new thread receives its own area of execution and its own call stack but shares the process's memory with the main thread. After the start the control returns to the synchronous code, which carries on being carried out while the new thread works in parallel (if the number of the CPU's cores permits) or concurrently (through the switching of the context).\\n\\nThe practical application and an example\\n\\nThis approach is often used for:\\n\\n- The carrying out of lengthy computations without blocking the user's interface.\\n\\n- The handling of several network requests simultaneously.\\n\\n- The parallel handling of data.\\n\\nAn example in Python with the use of the `threading` module:\\n\\nThe important aspects\\n\\nIn the starting of threads from synchronous code you have to take into account:\\n\\n- Synchronisation: if several threads turn to shared data, mechanisms of locks (mutexes) are needed in order to avoid race conditions.\\n\\n- The global interpreter lock (GIL): in CPython the GIL prevents the parallel execution of Python bytecode in several threads, which makes them useful mainly for operations of input and output but not for CPU-intensive tasks.\\n\\n- The termination of the program: a program usually does not terminate while every non-daemon thread is working.\\n\\nConclusion: The starting of threads from synchronous code is the standard way of adding parallelism to an application. It is worth applying when you have to carry out a background task without blocking the main thread, especially for operations connected with waiting (I/O). For CPU-intensive tasks in Python, however, it is often preferable to use multiprocessing (`multiprocessing`) or asynchronous programming.\",\"import threading\\nimport time\\n\\ndef background_task():\\n    # A long operation - the loading of a file, for example\\n    print(\\\"The background thread: it has begun its work\\\")\\n    time.sleep(3)\\n    print(\\\"The background thread: it has finished its work\\\")\\n\\n# The synchronous code\\nprint(\\\"The main thread: I am beginning\\\")\\n\\n# We create and start a thread from the synchronous code\\nthread = threading.Thread(target=background_task)\\nthread.start()\\n\\n# The main thread carries on with its work at once, without waiting for the background one to finish\\nprint(\\\"The main thread: the thread has been started, I am carrying on...\\\")\\n\\n# You can wait for the thread to finish if that is needed\\nthread.join()\\nprint(\\\"The main thread: every thread has finished\\\")\"],[1544,\"The finally block is part of the try...catch construct in JavaScript, which is carried out always after the try and catch blocks, independently of whether an error took place or not. It is used for the freeing of resources, the closing of connections or the carrying out of obligatory concluding actions. For example, if you have opened a file in the try block, in the finally you can close it with a guarantee, even if an error arose in the reading.\",\"The `try...catch...finally` construct in JavaScript provides a mechanism for the handling of errors (exceptions) and for the carrying out of obligatory concluding code. The `finally` block follows the `try` and `catch` blocks (or only the `try`) and is carried out always, independently of whether an exception was thrown in the `try` block and of whether it was intercepted in the `catch`.\\n\\nHow this works\\n\\nThe flow of the execution:\\n\\n- The code inside the `try` block is carried out.\\n\\n- If an error arises in the `try`, the execution immediately moves into the `catch` block (if there is one).\\n\\n- After the completion of the `try` block (and of the `catch`, if there was an error), the `finally` block is carried out.\\n\\n- If in the `finally` block there is no `return` statement or throwing of a new exception, the execution continues after the whole construct.\\n\\nThe key particular feature: the `finally` block is carried out even if a `return`, `break` or `continue` statement is met with in the `try` or the `catch`.\\n\\nExamples of the use\\n\\nThe typical scenario is the freeing of resources — the closing of a file stream or of a connection to a database, for example, in order to avoid leaks.\\n\\nAnother example, demonstrating the behaviour with a `return`:\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- The cleanup of resources: The closing of file descriptors, of network connections, of the transactions of a database.\\n\\n- The resetting of state: The removal of a flag of loading in a UI after an attempt at a request, independently of its success, for example.\\n\\n- Logging: The recording of the fact of the completion of an operation in a log.\\n\\n- In asynchronous code with `async/await` the principle remains the same, but you have to remember that the `finally` will be carried out after the resolution or the rejection of the Promise.\\n\\nConclusion: The `finally` block is necessary when some code (most often a cleanup) has to be carried out with a guarantee after an attempt at the execution of the main operation, independently of its outcome. This is a key tool for the writing of reliable code that is robust to errors.\",\"let resource = acquireResource(); // We obtain the resource\\n\\ntry {\\n  // We work with the resource, which may raise an error\\n  useResource(resource);\\n  console.log('The operation is successful');\\n} catch (error) {\\n  console.error('An error has taken place:', error.message);\\n} finally {\\n  // This code will be carried out in any case\\n  releaseResource(resource); // We free the resource with a guarantee\\n  console.log('The resource has been freed');\\n}\\n// The execution will continue here\\n\\nfunction testFinally() {\\n  try {\\n    console.log('Inside the try');\\n    return 'the return from the try'; // The return statement\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    console.log('Inside the catch');\\n  } finally {\\n    console.log('Inside the finally'); // This console.log will be carried out BEFORE the return of the value\\n  }\\n  console.log('This code will never be carried out');\\n}\\n\\nconst result = testFinally();\\nconsole.log('The function\\\\'s result:', result);\\n// The output:\\n// Inside the try\\n// Inside the finally\\n// The function's result: the return from the try\"],[1545,\"The use of the double type for money leads to errors of rounding because of the binary representation of fractional numbers. For example, 0.1 in the binary system is an infinite fraction, so operations of addition can give an imprecise result. This is critical for finance, in which precision to the kopeck or the cent matters. For money you should use special decimal types, such as Decimal in Python or BigDecimal in Java.\",\"Floating-point types such as a double use a binary representation for the holding of fractional numbers. This is efficient for scientific computations, but it creates a fundamental problem for decimal monetary values, since many decimal fractions (0.1, for example) cannot be precisely represented in the binary system and are held as approximate values.\\n\\nThe main problems\\n\\n- Errors of rounding: The accumulation of microscopic inaccuracies in multiple operations leads to noticeable discrepancies.\\n\\n- The comparison of values: The direct comparison of two double numbers often gives a false result because of differences in the lowest bits.\\n\\n- The rounding for the output: In the display of a sum a \\\"superfluous\\\" kopeck may appear (19.999999 instead of 20.00, for example).\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nLet us look at simple code in Python demonstrating the problem:\\n\\nWhere this is applied\\n\\nFor financial operations (bank transactions, accounting, e-commerce) special types are used, which hold the value as a whole number of the minimal units (kopecks/cents) or use decimal arithmetic with a fixed precision (Decimal, BigDecimal, the monetary types in SQL).\\n\\nConclusion: The double type is not suitable for monetary calculations because of the imprecise representation of decimal fractions; for the guarantee of the precision you should apply decimal types or integer holding in the minimal units of the currency.\",\"# The use of a float (the analogue of a double)\\ntotal = 0.0\\nfor i in range(10):\\n    total += 0.1  # We add 10 kopecks\\nprint(f\\\"The total with a float: {total}\\\")  # Will print: 0.9999999999999999\\nprint(f\\\"Is it equal to 1.0? {total == 1.0}\\\")  # Will print: False\\n\\n# The correct approach with Decimal\\nfrom decimal import Decimal\\ndecimal_total = Decimal('0.0')\\nfor i in range(10):\\n    decimal_total += Decimal('0.1')\\nprint(f\\\"The total with a Decimal: {decimal_total}\\\")  # Will print: 1.0\\nprint(f\\\"Is it equal to 1.0? {decimal_total == Decimal('1.0')}\\\")  # Will print: True\"],[1546,\"Instead of long if-else chains for null checks you can use several approaches. Languages like Java 8+ have the Optional class, which wraps a value and provides `map` and `orElse` methods. Many languages have a null-coalescing operator (`??` in C#/JavaScript) for supplying a default value. Guard clauses also help — early returns on null, which makes the code more linear. The ternary operator sometimes replaces simple if-else.\",\"Handling null values is a common task, and direct if-else checks can clutter the code. Let's look at the main alternatives.\\n1. Optional (Java, other languages)\\nJava 8's Optional class encapsulates a value that may be null. It provides methods for handling it safely.\\n\\nThis removes the need for explicit checks and lets you build chains of transformations.\\n2. The null-coalescing operator\\nMany languages have a dedicated operator that returns the left operand if it is not null, and the right one otherwise.\\n\\nThis is a compact replacement for a simple if-else that assigns a default value.\\n3. Guard clauses\\nThis is a technique where checks for invalid values (including null) are performed at the start of a function, and an early exit happens on error.\\n\\nThis keeps the code flat and clear, freeing it from deep nesting.\\n4. The ternary operator\\nSuitable for simple cases of choosing between two values.\\n\\n5. The Null Object pattern\\nA special object is created to represent \\\"the absence of a value\\\", but with neutral behaviour, which lets you avoid checks altogether.\\n\\nConclusion: Use Optional for pipeline-style handling of possibly-null values in Java, the `??` operator for concisely supplying defaults in JS/C#, and guard clauses to simplify method logic and improve readability.\",\"Optional<String> maybeName = Optional.ofNullable(getName());\\nString result = maybeName\\n    .map(String::toUpperCase)\\n    .orElse(\\\"DEFAULT\\\");\\n// If getName() returns null, result will be \\\"DEFAULT\\\"\\n\\n// C#\\nstring name = potentialName ?? \\\"Unknown\\\";\\n// JavaScript/TypeScript\\nconst value = input ?? defaultValue;\\n\\npublic void process(User user) {\\n    if (user == null) {\\n        throw new IllegalArgumentException(\\\"User cannot be null\\\");\\n        // or return;\\n    }\\n    // Main logic, user is now guaranteed to be non-null\\n}\\n\\nString status = (value != null) ? value : \\\"pending\\\";\\n\\n// Instead of returning null, an empty implementation of the interface is returned\\npublic interface Listener {\\n    void onEvent();\\n}\\nclass NullListener implements Listener {\\n    public void onEvent() { /* do nothing */ }\\n}\"],[1547,\"Streams are an abstraction for working with data that can be read or written in pieces. They are related to concurrency because they let you process data asynchronously, without waiting for the whole volume to load. For example, in Node.js streams can read a file and immediately pass chunks to the next handler, which saves memory and time. This is especially useful when working with large files or network requests.\",\"Streams represent a sequence of data available over time. Their key link to concurrency is that they let you break data processing into stages and run those stages simultaneously rather than sequentially. This is called pipeline processing.\\nHow streams implement concurrency\\nInstead of waiting until all the data is loaded into memory, streams start processing it as soon as the first fragment arrives. This allows you to:\\n\\n- Save memory: there is no need to hold the entire volume of data at once.\\n- Increase throughput: while one stage processes one chunk, the next stage can start working on the previous chunk.\\n- Improve responsiveness: the application can start producing a result before the whole operation finishes.\\nExample in Node.js\\nConsider reading a large file, compressing it and writing it to another file using streams:\\n\\nIn this example data from `input.txt` starts flowing into `gzip` for compression, and the compressed chunks are written to the output file right away. All three operations (reading, compressing, writing) run in parallel at the system level, which significantly speeds up the process compared with sequentially loading, compressing in full, and then writing.\\nWhere parallel streams are used\\n\\n- Servers: handling HTTP requests and responses, especially for video or audio streaming.\\n- Big data processing: ETL pipelines, log analysis.\\n- Real time: chats, live broadcasts.\\nConclusion: Streams are worth using when you need to process large volumes of data efficiently or ensure low latency in real-time applications. They are a fundamental pattern for achieving I/O concurrency without blocking the main thread of execution.\",\"const fs = require('fs');\\nconst zlib = require('zlib');\\n\\n// Create a readable stream from a file\\nconst readStream = fs.createReadStream('input.txt');\\n// Create a stream for Gzip compression\\nconst gzip = zlib.createGzip();\\n// Create a stream for writing to a file\\nconst writeStream = fs.createWriteStream('input.txt.gz');\\n\\n// Connect the streams into a pipeline\\nreadStream.pipe(gzip).pipe(writeStream);\\n\\nconsole.log('Compressing the file...');\"],[1548,\"Writing tests after the code (Test-Last) lets you implement functionality faster, since it does not require designing through tests up front. However, this often leads to less testable code, because the architecture was not conceived with tests in mind. TDD, on the contrary, forces you to think through interfaces and makes refactoring easier, but takes more time at the start. The choice depends on the project: TDD suits complex logic, while Test-Last suits prototypes or simple tasks.\",\"Comparing the approaches: Test-Last and TDD\\nTest-Driven Development (TDD) is a methodology where the developer first writes a failing test, then the minimal code to make it pass, and finally refactors the code. Writing tests after the code (often called Test-Last) is a more traditional approach, where functionality is implemented and then covered with tests to verify correctness.\\nAdvantages of writing tests after the code\\n\\n- Fast initial implementation: you can focus on the logic without being distracted by writing tests.\\n- Flexibility while exploring: useful when prototyping or working with unclear requirements, where interfaces may change.\\n- Less overhead: suits simple tasks or scripts where the benefit of TDD is not obvious.\\nDisadvantages of Test-Last compared with TDD\\n\\n- Difficulty testing: the code may turn out to be tightly coupled or have side effects, which makes writing isolated tests hard.\\n- Risk of skipping tests: after the implementation there may be a temptation to save time and not cover all the cases.\\n- Less influence on design: TDD forces you to think about how the code will be used, which often leads to better APIs and architecture.\\nA practical example\\nConsider a function that adds two numbers. In Test-Last the code is written first, then the test:\\n\\nIn TDD the order is reversed: first the test, which fails, then the implementation.\\nWhen to use which\\nTDD is especially useful when developing complex business logic, libraries or components with clear contracts. It reduces the number of defects and makes maintenance easier. Test-Last can be justified in the early stages of a project, for integration testing, or when a team is only just getting acquainted with automated testing.\\nConclusion: TDD helps produce higher-quality, more testable code, but demands discipline and time. Test-Last is faster at the start but can lead to technical debt. Ideally you combine both approaches depending on the context of the task.\",\"// The code first\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n\\n// Then the test\\ndescribe('add', () => {\\n    it('should return sum of two numbers', () => {\\n        expect(add(2, 3)).toBe(5);\\n    });\\n});\"],[1549,\"Static analysis tools are integrated into a CI/CD pipeline as separate steps or jobs that run automatically on every commit or pull request. They usually execute at the build or test stage, before deployment. The analysis results (warnings, errors, vulnerabilities) either block merging the code if critical problems are found, or are sent as a report for review. This lets you find bugs and vulnerabilities at an early stage, keeping them out of the main branch.\",\"Integrating static analysis into CI/CD is the practice of automatically running tools that check source code for potential errors, security vulnerabilities, style violations and complexity, without executing it. The main goal is to raise code quality and application security, preventing defects from reaching production.\\nHow it works in a pipeline\\nA pipeline is usually configured in configuration files (for example, `.gitlab-ci.yml` for GitLab CI or `.github/workflows/` for GitHub Actions). The static analysis tool is added as a separate step (job/stage) that runs after the repository is cloned and dependencies installed, but before artifacts are built or deployed.\\n\\n- Trigger: Analysis runs on every push to particular branches or when a pull request is created.\\n- Execution: The pipeline script runs the analyzer (for example, SonarQube, ESLint, Bandit, Checkmarx) to scan the code base.\\n- Handling results: The analyzer generates a report. The pipeline can be configured to fail when problems above a certain severity level are found, which blocks merging the code.\\n- Reporting: Results are often published as comments on the pull request, sent to Slack, or stored in tools like the SonarQube Dashboard for further tracking.\\nExample configuration in GitHub Actions\\nBelow is a simplified example of a workflow for a Node.js project that runs ESLint and a security scan via npm audit.\\n\\nPractical aspects and use\\nThis kind of integration is used by teams of any size to enforce coding standards, detect vulnerabilities early (for example, with Snyk, or Trivy for containers) and reduce technical debt. The key point is configuring thresholds: you shouldn't fail the build over every style warning, or it will slow development down. Better to start with critical errors and security, gradually tightening the rules.\\nConclusion: Integrating static analysis into a CI/CD pipeline is an effective way to automate code quality control, making it an inseparable part of the development process. It is especially useful in large teams and projects with high security and maintainability requirements, preventing regressions and reducing the cost of fixing bugs at later stages.\",\"name: CI Static Analysis\\n\\non:\\n  push:\\n    branches: [ main ]\\n  pull_request:\\n    branches: [ main ]\\n\\njobs:\\n  static-analysis:\\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\\n    steps:\\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\\n      - name: Setup Node.js\\n        uses: actions/setup-node@v3\\n        with:\\n          node-version: '18'\\n      - name: Install dependencies\\n        run: npm ci\\n      - name: Run ESLint (Code Style & Potential Errors)\\n        run: npx eslint . --max-warnings=0\\n      - name: Run npm audit (Security Vulnerabilities)\\n        run: npm audit --audit-level=high\\n        # If high or critical vulnerabilities are found, the step fails.\"],[1550,\"Start by reproducing the bug exactly. Recreate the conditions under which it occurs: environment, data, sequence of actions. Then gather information: check error logs, messages in the browser console or the terminal. Isolate the problem by simplifying the code or the data to find a minimal reproducible example. Use a debugger to step through the code and check the state of the variables. A methodical approach saves time and helps you find the root cause.\",\"Hunting a bug is a systematic investigation process, not random guessing. The first and most important step is to understand exactly what is not working and under what conditions.\\n\\n1. Reproduction and localisation\\n\\nTry to reproduce the error reliably. If the bug is sporadic, gather as much information as you can about when it shows up. Determine the boundaries of the problem: does it occur only in a particular browser, with specific input data, or after a specific sequence of actions? Creating a Minimal Reproducible Example (MRE) often leads to an understanding of the root cause all by itself.\\n\\n2. Gathering information and analysis\\n\\nUse every available source of data:\\n\\n- Application and server logs: Look for stack traces, error messages, warnings.\\n\\n- Browser developer tools (for the web): The Console, the Network tab for inspecting requests/responses, the debugger (Sources/Debugger).\\n\\n- System logs and monitoring: If the problem is related to memory, CPU or the network.\\n\\nAnalyse the changes: did the bug appear after a recent update to the code, the libraries or the configuration? Use `git bisect` to find the offending commit.\\n\\n3. Debugging techniques\\n\\nOnce the problem is narrowed down to a module or a function, use active debugging:\\n\\n- Debugger: Set breakpoints and execute the code step by step (step into/over), watching the values of the variables.\\n\\n- Logging: Add logs at key points to trace the flow of execution and the state of the data. For complex asynchronous operations this is especially useful.\\n\\n- Rubber Duck Debugging: Try explaining the code and the problem in detail to a colleague or even to an inanimate object. Often the solution turns up in the course of explaining it.\\n\\n4. Example approach (JavaScript)\\n\\nIn this example logging will immediately show that the second element has its price as a string, which leads to incorrect addition. From there you can add validation or type conversion.\\n\\nConclusion: A systematic approach to hunting bugs — from reproduction and data gathering to targeted debugging — makes the process predictable and effective. It is especially useful in team work, when analysing legacy code, or in complex distributed systems where intuition is not enough.\",\"// Suppose the calculateTotal function sometimes returns NaN\\nfunction calculateTotal(items) {\\n    // 1. Log the input data\\n    console.log('Items received:', items);\\n\\n    let total = 0;\\n    for (let item of items) {\\n        // 2. Check every iteration\\n        console.log('Processing item:', item, 'Price:', item.price);\\n        // 3. We suspect price may be a string or undefined\\n        total += item.price; // Possible source of NaN if price is not a number\\n    }\\n    // 4. Use the debugger here to check the value of total\\n    debugger;\\n    return total;\\n}\\n\\n// 5. Build a minimal test case to reproduce it\\nconst testItems = [\\n    { name: 'A', price: 10 },\\n    { name: 'B', price: '20' }, // Bug: a string!\\n    { name: 'C', price: 30 }\\n];\\nconsole.log('Result:', calculateTotal(testItems)); // Prints NaN\"],[1551,\"To find the part of the code consuming the most memory, use profiling tools. In the browser that is Chrome DevTools (the Memory tab); in Node.js it is the built-in `v8` module or third-party tools like `clinic.js`. They let you take heap snapshots, track memory allocation and find objects that the garbage collector does not reclaim. By analysing the difference between snapshots you can uncover leaks and problematic functions.\",\"Working out which part of the code consumes the most memory requires specialised memory profiling tools. These tools let you look inside the application's execution and see which objects are created, how much memory they occupy and whether the garbage collector reclaims them.\\nMain approaches to memory profiling\\nThere are two main approaches: allocation tracking and heap snapshots. The first method shows where and when new objects are created, in real time. The second takes a static snapshot of all memory at a particular moment, letting you analyse the overall volume and the chains of references.\\nPractical tools and examples\\nFor JavaScript applications in the browser the most powerful tool is Chrome DevTools (the Memory tab). In Node.js you can use:\\n\\n- The built-in `v8` module to create heap snapshots.\\n- The `--inspect` flag to attach DevTools.\\n- Third-party packages such as `clinic.js` or `node-memwatch`.\\nAn example of using Chrome DevTools for a Node.js application:\\n\\nThe analysis will show which constructors (for example, Array, String, your own classes) created the most objects and how much memory they retain. Look for objects whose count keeps growing between snapshots.\\nWhere this is used\\nMemory profiling is critically important for long-lived applications (Node.js servers, single-page applications), where even a small leak eventually exhausts memory. It is also used to optimise functions that work with large volumes of data, for example file processing or heavy computations.\\nConclusion: Use memory profiling when you suspect leaks or unexplained growth in memory consumption, or to optimise resource-critical parts of the code in production applications.\",\"// Start the application with the inspect flag\\nnode --inspect your-script.js\\n\\n// 1. Open chrome://inspect in Chrome.\\n// 2. Click 'inspect' on your process.\\n// 3. Go to the 'Memory' tab.\\n// 4. Choose 'Heap snapshot' and click 'Take snapshot'.\\n// 5. Perform the actions that may be causing the leak.\\n// 6. Take a second snapshot and compare them using the 'Comparison' filter.\"],[1552,\"An exception is worth catching when you can handle it meaningfully at the current level: for example, retry the operation, return a default value or write to a log. You should let an exception propagate upwards when the current context does not know how to cope with it and the decision has to be made at a higher level of abstraction. For example, in the controller layer of a web application you can catch the exception and turn it into an HTTP response with an error code, while in a low-level file-handling function you should rethrow it, since that function does not know what to do about an I/O error in terms of business logic.\",\"Exception handling is a key part of writing reliable, maintainable code. The main principle is to catch an exception only when you have a concrete plan for resolving it or reacting to it meaningfully in the current context.\\nWhen to catch an exception\\nCatch an exception if you can:\\n\\n- Restore the normal flow of execution: for example, retry the operation with exponential backoff on a network error.\\n- Supply an alternative result: return a default value or an empty list, if that is acceptable for the business logic.\\n- Record detailed information for debugging and carry on, if the error is not critical.\\n- Translate a low-level error into something more comprehensible for the calling code, by wrapping it in an exception appropriate to the current level of abstraction.\\n\\nWhen to let an exception propagate\\nLet the exception bubble up if:\\n\\n- The current context does not know how to handle the error. For example, a function that reads a configuration file should not decide what to do when the file is missing — that is the calling code's job.\\n- The error is fatal for the current operation, and handling it at this level would create an inconsistent state.\\n- You want the error to be handled at a module or application-layer boundary, for example in a web framework's middleware that turns exceptions into HTTP responses.\\n\\nPractical application\\nIn a layered architecture (for example MVC) exceptions are often propagated from the data or service layer up to the controller or a middleware layer. At that top level the handling is centralised: logging, translation into a user-facing message or a specific HTTP status.\\nConclusion: Catch an exception when you can take a meaningful local action. Otherwise let it bubble up to a level that has enough context to make a decision, keeping the architecture clean and avoiding hidden errors.\",\"try {\\n    const data = JSON.parse(userInput);\\n} catch (error) {\\n    // We catch here because we can return a default value\\n    console.warn('Invalid JSON, using empty object');\\n    data = {};\\n}\\n\\nfunction readDatabaseConfig(path) {\\n    const content = fs.readFileSync(path, 'utf-8'); // May throw an exception\\n    return parseConfig(content); // May throw its own exception\\n    // We do not catch here; let the calling code decide how to react to a missing file.\\n}\"],[1553,\"Exceptions are considered expensive because handling them requires significant overhead. When an exception is thrown, the runtime has to gather information about the call stack in order to unwind it. That process involves searching for a suitable catch handler, which is slower than checking a return code. In addition, compilers often generate extra code to guarantee that resources are released correctly when exceptions occur, which increases the size of the binary and slows down normal execution.\",\"Exceptions provide a powerful error-handling mechanism that separates the normal flow of execution from failure situations. That flexibility, however, has its price in terms of performance and resource use.\\n\\nThe main reasons exceptions are expensive\\n\\n- Stack unwinding: When an exception is thrown, the runtime has to walk back along the chain of calls to find the nearest suitable `catch` block. That process requires analysing the exception tables generated by the compiler, which is significantly slower than a simple jump in the code.\\n\\n- Generating extra code: To guarantee that objects are destroyed and resources released correctly (for example, via destructors in C++ or `finally` in Java/C#) when an exception occurs, the compiler inserts additional management code. This increases the size of the executable and can slow execution down even in scenarios without errors.\\n\\n- Breaking branch prediction: Exceptions are rare, unpredictable events. Modern processors rely on branch prediction for efficient pipelined execution. An unexpected jump to an exception handler flushes the pipeline, which costs cycles.\\n\\n- Optimisation problems: Code inside `try` blocks is often harder for the compiler to optimise, since it has to account for the possibility of leaving that block at any point.\\n\\nA practical example and comparison\\n\\nConsider the difference between handling errors through exceptions and through return codes.\\n\\nIn the first case division by zero sets the whole exception machinery in motion. In the second a simple conditional branch and a value return are executed, which is orders of magnitude faster.\\n\\nWhere and how exceptions are used\\n\\nDespite the cost, exceptions are indispensable for handling genuinely exceptional, unforeseen situations (hardware failure, running out of memory, a critical I/O error), when restoring normal operation is impossible or requires complex actions across several levels of the call stack. They should not be used to control the ordinary flow of execution (for example, checking for end of file in a loop).\\n\\nConclusion: Use exceptions to handle rare, critical errors, when clarity of code and reliability matter more than micro-optimisations. For frequent, expected error conditions (for example, validating user input) it is preferable to use return codes or dedicated result types (for example, `std::optional`, or `Result` in Rust).\",\"// Example with an exception (the expensive path)\\ndouble divideWithException(double a, double b) {\\n    if (b == 0) {\\n        throw std::runtime_error(\\\"Division by zero\\\");\\n    }\\n    return a / b;\\n}\\n\\n// Example with a return code (the cheap path)\\nbool divideWithReturn(double a, double b, double &result) {\\n    if (b == 0) {\\n        return false; // Error\\n    }\\n    result = a / b;\\n    return true; // Success\\n}\"],[1554,\"Working with legacy code is a process of gradually improving and stabilising an outdated system without rewriting it completely. The key approach is to introduce tests around the existing code so that changes are safe. Then you can refactor in small steps, improving the structure of the code. It is important to understand the business logic and to have a clear migration plan so you don't break the system.\",\"Working with legacy code is not simply fixing bugs but a systematic approach to managing a complex, poorly documented and often unreliable code base. The main goal is to reduce the risks associated with changes and gradually raise the quality of the system without halting its operation.\\nKey principles of the work\\n\\n- Tests first: Since legacy code often has no automated tests, the first step is to create a \\\"protective belt\\\" of unit and integration tests around the critical modules. That lets you make changes with confidence.\\n- Refactoring in small steps: Instead of a large-scale rewrite, changes are made in small, isolated portions. Every step must be verified by the tests.\\n- Understanding through investigation: You need to spend time studying the code, its dependencies and the business logic, often with the help of debugging and log analysis.\\n- Isolation and encapsulation: Bad code is isolated where possible by wrapping it in cleaner interfaces, to minimise its influence on new logic.\\nPractical example: adding a test to an old function\\nImagine a Python function that calculates a discount, but whose code is tangled and has no tests.\\n\\nInstead of changing the code straight away, we first write a test that pins down the current behaviour:\\n\\nOnce we run the tests and confirm they pass, we have a safe foundation for refactoring. Now the code can be improved — for example, by moving the rules into a configuration dictionary — and the tests will immediately show whether we broke anything.\\nWhere it is used\\nThis approach is used when maintaining old enterprise systems (banking, insurance), inherited web applications and monoliths, where complete replacement is impossible or too risky. It is also used during a gradual migration from outdated technologies to a modern stack.\\nConclusion: The legacy-code approach is worth applying when you need to maintain and evolve a working system that cannot be switched off or quickly rewritten. It lets you reduce technical debt in a controlled, safe way, minimising disruption for users.\",\"# legacy_module.py\\n\\ndef calculate_discount(amount, customer_type):\\n    # Complex, tangled logic\\n    if customer_type == \\\"VIP\\\":\\n        if amount > 1000:\\n            return amount * 0.2\\n        else:\\n            return amount * 0.1\\n    elif customer_type == \\\"REGULAR\\\":\\n        return amount * 0.05\\n    else:\\n        return 0\\n\\n# test_legacy_module.py\\nimport pytest\\nfrom legacy_module import calculate_discount\\n\\ndef test_vip_large_order():\\n    # Pin down the expected behaviour\\n    assert calculate_discount(1500, \\\"VIP\\\") == 300  # 1500 * 0.2\\n\\ndef test_regular_customer():\\n    assert calculate_discount(500, \\\"REGULAR\\\") == 25  # 500 * 0.05\"],[1555,\"A memory leak occurs when objects stay in memory even though they are no longer needed. To debug one, use the browser developer tools (for example, Chrome DevTools) or Node.js profilers. Take several heap snapshots and compare them to find objects that the garbage collector does not remove. Check global variables, timers, event subscriptions and closures, which can hold on to references.\",\"A memory leak is a situation where an application keeps holding memory allocated for objects that are no longer in use, which over time can lead to slowdowns or a crash. Debugging it requires a systematic approach and specialised tools.\\nThe main debugging steps\\n\\n- Reproducing the scenario: Create a reliable way to reproduce the problem, for example by performing a particular action in the application repeatedly.\\n- Monitoring memory: Use the Performance or Memory tab in Chrome DevTools. Record memory allocation (Allocation instrumentation on timeline) or take heap snapshots.\\n- Comparing snapshots: Take a heap snapshot before the action, after the action and after forcing garbage collection. Compare them to find objects whose count grows without justification.\\n- Analysing retention chains: Study which root objects (for example the global window) hold references to the problematic objects through a chain of properties.\\nCommon causes and how to find them\\n\\n- Global variables: Accidental assignment onto `window` or `globalThis`.\\n- Forgotten timers and intervals: `setInterval` or `setTimeout` that are never cleared.\\n- Event subscriptions: Event listeners not removed when an element is destroyed.\\n- Closures: Functions capturing large objects from an outer scope.\\n- Caches and stores: Data structures that grow with no eviction mechanism.\\nExample code with a potential leak and its fix\\n\\nIn Node.js you can use the `--inspect` flag to attach DevTools, or modules like `heapdump` to create heap snapshots. For server applications it is also useful to monitor the process's memory use with utilities such as `top` or `process.memoryUsage()`.\\nConclusion: A method for debugging memory leaks is essential for building reliable long-lived applications, especially single-page applications (SPAs) and servers with long uptimes. Checking memory regularly during development helps prevent problems accumulating in production.\",\"// Potential leak: the listener is added but never removed.\\nconst button = document.getElementById('myButton');\\nconst hugeArray = new Array(1000000).fill('data');\\n\\nbutton.addEventListener('click', function onClick() {\\n    // The function captures hugeArray through a closure.\\n    console.log('Clicked', hugeArray.length);\\n});\\n// If the button is removed from the DOM, the listener and hugeArray may stay in memory.\\n\\n// The fix: keep a reference and remove the listener when needed.\\nfunction createListener() {\\n    const hugeArray = new Array(1000000).fill('data');\\n    const listener = () => console.log('Clicked', hugeArray.length);\\n    button.addEventListener('click', listener);\\n    // Return a function for cleanup.\\n    return () => button.removeEventListener('click', listener);\\n}\\nconst cleanup = createListener();\\n// When the button is no longer needed, call cleanup().\"],[1556,\"It is better to use both approaches, but in the right order. Validating input data is the first line of defence, which prevents incorrect data from entering the system. Exception handling is needed for situations that cannot or should not be predicted in advance, for example network or third-party service failures. The ideal approach is to validate everything that can be checked and handle exceptions for unforeseen failures.\",\"In software development, making a system reliable and predictable is a key task. The two main mechanisms for this are input validation and exception handling. They are not mutually exclusive but complement each other, solving different problems in the life cycle of data processing.\\nInput validation\\nThis is a preventive approach. The goal is to make sure that data entering the system (from a user, another service, a file) matches the expected format, type, range of values and business rules before processing begins. It is like checking a passport at the border.\\n\\n- Where it is used: At system boundaries (API endpoints, UI forms, public class methods), when deserialising data, before writing to a database.\\n- Advantages: Lets you give the user clear feedback quickly, prevents operations being performed on meaningless data, and improves security (protection against injections).\\nException handling\\nThis is a reactive approach. Exceptions are intended for handling exceptional situations — errors that arise while the program is running and disrupt the normal flow of execution. Such situations often cannot be foreseen during validation (for example, a database failing in the middle of a transaction, running out of memory, a network request timing out).\\n\\n- Where it is used: When working with external resources (databases, the file system, network calls), when performing operations that may fail for reasons outside the program's control.\\n- Advantages: Lets the program recover gracefully after a failure, log the error for analysis, and possibly retry the operation or provide a fallback.\\nPractical example\\nConsider a money-transfer function. First we validate the input data (account numbers, amount), and then in a try-catch block we handle the possible exceptions from interacting with the banking API.\\n\\nConclusion: Use input validation as the mandatory first step to cut off obviously incorrect requests and ensure data integrity. Use exception handling to manage runtime errors arising from external factors or internal system failures that cannot be checked in advance. The combination of these approaches creates a resilient, user-friendly system.\",\"function transferMoney(senderAccount, recipientAccount, amount) {\\n    // 1. INPUT VALIDATION\\n    if (!isValidAccountNumber(senderAccount)) {\\n        throw new ValidationError('Invalid sender account number');\\n    }\\n    if (!isValidAccountNumber(recipientAccount)) {\\n        throw new ValidationError('Invalid recipient account number');\\n    }\\n    if (amount <= 0 || amount > MAX_LIMIT) {\\n        throw new ValidationError('Amount must be positive and below limit');\\n    }\\n\\n    // 2. EXCEPTION HANDLING for unforeseen failures\\n    try {\\n        const transactionId = bankApi.processTransfer({\\n            from: senderAccount,\\n            to: recipientAccount,\\n            sum: amount\\n        }); // May throw NetworkError, InsufficientFundsError\\n        return transactionId;\\n    } catch (error) {\\n        // Log the technical error for the developers\\n        console.error('Transfer failed:', error);\\n        // Surface a user-facing message or apply a retry strategy\\n        throw new ApplicationError('Money transfer failed. Please try again later.');\\n    }\\n}\"],[1557,\"First you need to localise the problem using monitoring and profiling tools. For CPU, use a profiler (for example Chrome DevTools for the front end, or py-spy for Python) to find the \\\"hot\\\" functions. For memory, check for leaks via memory dumps or allocators. Then analyse the code: common causes are suboptimal algorithms, infinite loops or memory leaks from unclosed resources. Once the cause is identified, apply a fix: optimise the algorithm, fix the leak, add caching or limit concurrency.\",\"When an application unexpectedly starts consuming more CPU or RAM, the first step is not panic but systematic diagnosis. The goal is to determine the root cause: it may be new code, increased load, a resource leak or a change in the environment.\\n1. Monitoring and data collection\\nUse monitoring tools to collect metrics in real time (for example Prometheus, Grafana, the built-in monitors of cloud platforms). Compare the current figures with the baseline. Determine whether the problem is in the CPU (high core utilisation) or in memory (growth in consumption that is not returned after garbage collection).\\n2. CPU profiling\\nTo analyse the CPU, use a profiler that shows which functions spend the most time. An example for a Node.js application:\\n\\nFor web applications, use the Performance tab in Chrome DevTools to record and analyse activity.\\n3. Analysing memory use\\nMemory leaks often arise from keeping references to objects that are no longer needed. Take a heap snapshot and compare snapshots from before and after the operation presumed to cause the leak. The same Chrome DevTools has a Memory tab for this. For a Python back end you can use the `tracemalloc` module.\\n\\n4. Common causes and solutions\\n\\n- High CPU: inefficient algorithms (O(n²) instead of O(n log n)), synchronous blocking operations inside a loop, infinite or very frequent re-renders in the UI, a large number of concurrent goroutines/threads.\\n- Memory growth: leaks (global variables, caches with no limit, unclosed database or file connections), data accumulating in memory with no cleanup (for example, logs in an array).\\n5. Practical steps to fix it\\n\\n- Localise the problematic module or function with a profiler.\\n- Simplify reproducing the problem (write a test or a script).\\n- Optimise: replace the algorithm, add pagination, limit the cache size, use streaming data processing instead of loading everything into memory.\\n- For leaks: make sure resources are released (use `try...finally`, `using` in C#, `with` in Python).\\n- Test the fix under load and confirm the metrics are back to normal.\\nConclusion: A systematic approach to diagnosis is the key to solving performance problems. CPU profiling and memory analysis should become standard tools in a developer's arsenal. Apply these techniques not only when problems appear but also periodically as prevention, especially after making significant changes to the code.\",\"// Running a Node.js application with the built-in profiler\\nnode --prof app.js\\n// After the load, stop the application and convert the log\\nnode --prof-process isolate-0xnnnnnnnnnnnn-v8.log > processed.txt\\n// In the output look for the [Summary] section and functions with a high % of time.\\n\\nimport tracemalloc\\ntracemalloc.start()\\n# ... run the suspect code ...\\nsnapshot = tracemalloc.take_snapshot()\\ntop_stats = snapshot.statistics('lineno')\\nfor stat in top_stats[:10]:\\n    print(stat)  # Shows the files and lines allocating the most memory.\"],[1558,\"Input validation is the process of checking whether the data supplied matches the expected criteria, such as type, format, length and range of values. The main goal is to prevent the processing of incorrect or malicious data that could lead to errors, vulnerabilities or a breach of the business logic. The simplest approach is a client-side check to improve UX, but mandatory and stricter validation must be performed on the server side. Key principles include \\\"never trust user input\\\", using allowlists of permitted values and returning clear error messages.\",\"Input validation is a fundamental practice in software development, aimed at ensuring that any information entering the system (from users, APIs, files) matches defined rules before it is processed. Without it an application becomes vulnerable to a range of attacks, such as SQL injection or XSS, or may simply break on unexpected values.\\n\\nThe main types of validation\\n\\n- Syntax validation: Checking the format of the data (for example, whether a string matches an email or date pattern).\\n\\n- Semantic validation: Checking logical correctness (for example, an end date must be later than a start date).\\n\\n- Client-side validation: Performed in the browser via HTML5 attributes (required, pattern, type) or JavaScript. Convenient for the user, but unreliable, since it can be bypassed.\\n\\n- Server-side validation: The mandatory and final line of defence. Performed in the application's back-end logic.\\n\\nPractical implementation\\n\\nConsider an example of validating user registration data on the server using Node.js and the Joi library.\\n\\nIn this example the Joi library lets you describe the rules declaratively. The key points: checking the type, the length, the email format and a custom regular expression for the password. The `abortEarly: false` option guarantees that all errors are collected, not just the first one.\\n\\nWhere and how to apply it\\n\\nValidation must be applied at every entry point: HTTP requests (body, query, params), data from message queues (Kafka, RabbitMQ), files uploaded by users, and responses from external APIs. Web frameworks (Express, NestJS, Django, Spring) often have built-in or recommended validation libraries (for example, class-validator for NestJS, Pydantic for FastAPI).\\n\\nConclusion: Server-side input validation is a mandatory stage in building secure, reliable applications. It is worth applying whenever data comes from an untrusted source, using declarative schemas and libraries to minimise human error and centralise the validation rules.\",\"const Joi = require('joi');\\n\\n// Define the validation schema\\nconst userSchema = Joi.object({\\n    username: Joi.string().alphanum().min(3).max(30).required(),\\n    email: Joi.string().email().required(),\\n    password: Joi.string().pattern(new RegExp('^[a-zA-Z0-9]{8,30}$')).required(),\\n    birthYear: Joi.number().integer().min(1900).max(new Date().getFullYear()),\\n    role: Joi.string().valid('user', 'admin').default('user')\\n});\\n\\n// A function to handle the incoming data\\nfunction validateUser(inputData) {\\n    const { error, value } = userSchema.validate(inputData, { abortEarly: false });\\n    \\n    if (error) {\\n        // Collect every error into an array\\n        const errors = error.details.map(detail => detail.message);\\n        return { isValid: false, errors, value: null };\\n    }\\n    // Return the cleaned, validated data\\n    return { isValid: true, errors: null, value };\\n}\\n\\n// Example usage\\nconst rawInput = { username: 'john_doe1', email: 'john@example.com', password: 'Secret123' };\\nconst result = validateUser(rawInput);\\nconsole.log(result);\"],[1559,\"Using exceptions for business logic has both pros and cons. Pros: they separate the normal flow from errors, making the code cleaner, and they let you handle errors centrally at the upper levels. Cons: exceptions carry a performance overhead and can hide logic, making it less explicit. For expected errors (for example, invalid input) it is better to use return values or dedicated result objects.\",\"Exceptions are an error-handling mechanism that interrupts the normal flow of a program and transfers control to a special handler (a catch block). In the context of business logic, which implements the rules and operations of the domain, it is important to distinguish clearly when exceptions are appropriate and when they are not.\\nPros of using exceptions\\n\\n- Separation of concerns: The business logic code stays clean, since error handling is moved to a higher level (for example, into a controller or the presentation layer).\\n- Centralised handling: You can catch exceptions in one place, log them and turn them into responses for the user (for example, HTTP 400).\\n- Enforced handling: In languages with checked exceptions (such as Java) the compiler forces the developer to handle the possible errors explicitly, which improves reliability.\\nCons of using exceptions\\n\\n- Performance: Creating an exception and unwinding the stack is usually more expensive than returning a simple value. That can be critical in high-load loops.\\n- Obscuring control flow: Exceptions can be used to control the normal flow (for example, to break out of nested loops), which is considered an anti-pattern since it hides intent.\\n- Implicitness: It is not always obvious to the calling code which exceptions may be thrown, especially in languages without checked exceptions (such as JavaScript or Python).\\nPractical example\\nConsider a function that checks a user's balance before a withdrawal. Using an exception for an expected situation (insufficient funds) may be excessive.\\n\\nAn alternative approach is to return a result object that explicitly indicates success or failure, which can be more efficient and clearer for expected failures.\\n\\nConclusion: Exceptions are worth using to handle genuinely exceptional, unexpected situations (for example, a database failure, loss of network connection). For expected business-logic errors (invalid input, insufficient funds) it is preferable to use return values or dedicated result types (Result/Option), since this makes the control flow explicit and reduces overhead.\",\"// Example in TypeScript: using an exception for a business rule\\nclass InsufficientFundsError extends Error {\\n    constructor(public userId: number, public required: number) {\\n        super(`User ${userId} lacks ${required} credits.`);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nfunction withdraw(userId: number, amount: number): void {\\n    const balance = getBalance(userId); // get the balance from the database\\n    if (balance < amount) {\\n        // Business error: insufficient funds\\n        throw new InsufficientFundsError(userId, amount);\\n    }\\n    // ... withdrawal logic\\n}\\n\\n// Handling at the top level (for example, in an Express route)\\ntry {\\n    withdraw(123, 1000);\\n    res.status(200).json({ success: true });\\n} catch (error) {\\n    if (error instanceof InsufficientFundsError) {\\n        res.status(400).json({ error: error.message });\\n    } else {\\n        // An unexpected error (for example, a database problem)\\n        res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Alternative: returning a result without an exception\\ninterface WithdrawalResult {\\n    success: boolean;\\n    errorMessage?: string;\\n}\\n\\nfunction withdrawSafe(userId: number, amount: number): WithdrawalResult {\\n    const balance = getBalance(userId);\\n    if (balance < amount) {\\n        return { success: false, errorMessage: 'Insufficient funds' };\\n    }\\n    // ... withdrawal logic\\n    return { success: true };\\n}\\n\\nconst result = withdrawSafe(123, 1000);\\nif (!result.success) {\\n    // Handle the expected business error\\n    console.log(result.errorMessage);\\n}\"],[1560,\"Trying to access a property of the value undefined in JavaScript throws a TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined. This happens because undefined is a primitive type that has no properties. To avoid this error you can use optional chaining (`?.`) or check the value before accessing the property.\",\"In JavaScript `undefined` is a primitive value that signals the absence of a definition for a variable or property. Since it is a primitive, it has no internal object structure, and trying to access its property (for example, `undefined.someProperty`) or call a method results in an immediate error.\\n\\nWhy does the error occur?\\n\\nWhen the JavaScript engine encounters the expression `undefined.property`, it tries to convert `undefined` into an object in order to access the property. That conversion is impossible for the `undefined` primitive, so a `TypeError` is generated. This is a protective mechanism of the language, preventing further work with non-existent data.\\n\\nPractical examples and solutions\\n\\nConsider a typical scenario where a variable may be `undefined`:\\n\\nWhere does this matter?\\n\\n- When working with data from an API that may arrive incomplete.\\n\\n- When accessing deeply nested object properties (`obj.level1.level2.property`).\\n\\n- In functions where arguments may not be passed.\\n\\nConclusion: Always check or guard your code when accessing properties of variables that may be `undefined`. Use optional chaining `?.` for safe access and the nullish coalescing operator `??` to supply defaults. This is a key practice for writing resilient JavaScript code.\",\"let user;\\n// user is undefined by default\\n\\n// This will throw an error:\\n// console.log(user.name); // TypeError!\\n\\n// Solution 1: a check with if\\nif (user !== undefined) {\\n  console.log(user.name);\\n}\\n\\n// Solution 2: optional chaining (ES2020)\\nconsole.log(user?.name); // Prints undefined, but throws no error\\n\\n// Solution 3: using a default value\\nlet safeName = (user || {}).name; // undefined\\nlet safeName2 = user?.name ?? 'Guest'; // 'Guest'\"],[1561,\"A property added directly to a constructor function is stored on the function itself as an object and will not be accessible on its instances through `this`. It is only accessible through the constructor function itself. For the property to be available on instances, it has to be added to the function's `prototype`.\",\"In JavaScript functions are objects, so you can add properties to them directly, like to any other object. However, when a function is used as a constructor (with the `new` keyword), a new object (an instance) is created that inherits properties from the function's prototype but does not receive the function's own direct properties.\\n\\nA direct property of a function\\n\\nA property added directly to a function belongs to the function object itself. It is accessible only by referring to the function, not through the instances created with that function.\\n\\nA property on the prototype\\n\\nFor a property to be available on every instance, it must be added to the function's `prototype`. It will then be inherited through the prototype chain.\\n\\nPractical application\\n\\nDirect properties of functions are often used for:\\n\\n- Storing static data or methods common to all instances (for example, constants).\\n\\n- Implementing utility functions related to the constructor but not requiring access to instance data.\\n\\nFor example, in the `Math` object all methods are static and are called directly on `Math`, not through instances.\\n\\nConclusion: Direct properties of functions are useful for static members that do not depend on instance state, while properties on the `prototype` are needed for methods and data that must be available to every instance.\",\"function Car(model) {\\n  this.model = model;\\n}\\n\\n// Add a property directly to the function\\nCar.manufacturer = 'Toyota';\\n\\nconst myCar = new Car('Corolla');\\nconsole.log(Car.manufacturer); // 'Toyota'\\nconsole.log(myCar.manufacturer); // undefined\\nconsole.log(myCar.model); // 'Corolla'\\n\\nCar.prototype.getDetails = function() {\\n  return `${this.model} by ${Car.manufacturer}`;\\n};\\n\\nconsole.log(myCar.getDetails()); // 'Corolla by Toyota'\"],[1562,\"In JavaScript arrays are reference types. When you assign an array to a new variable, you copy not the array itself but a reference to it. Both variables point at the same array in memory. So a change made to the array through one variable (for example, adding an element) will be visible through the other variable, since they work with the same object.\",\"In JavaScript objects (including arrays, functions and plain objects) are reference types. That means a variable stores not the value itself but the address (reference) of the place in memory where the data lives. When an array is assigned to another variable, it is precisely that reference that is copied, not the contents of the array.\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\nConsider an example:\\n\\nBoth variables `arr1` and `arr2` point at the same array. Any operation that changes the array (a mutation), such as `push`, `pop`, `splice` or direct assignment by index, will be reflected when accessed through either variable.\\n\\nWhere it is used and what the pitfalls are\\n\\nThis behaviour is used to pass arrays into functions without creating copies, which can be memory-efficient. However, it often leads to bugs when a developer unknowingly modifies the original data. For example, when working with state in React or Redux, unexpected mutations can cause rendering problems.\\n\\nTo avoid such situations you need to create copies of the array:\\n\\n- Shallow copy: `let copy = [...arr1];` or `let copy = arr1.slice();`\\n\\n- Deep copy (for nested objects): `let deepCopy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(arr1));` or using `structuredClone`.\\n\\nAn example with copying:\\n\\nConclusion: Understanding the reference nature of arrays is critical for writing predictable code. Always bear in mind that assignment does not create a copy of the data. If you need to work with an independent version of an array, create an explicit copy with a suitable method.\",\"let arr1 = [1, 2, 3];\\nlet arr2 = arr1; // arr2 now references the same array\\narr2.push(4); // Change the array through arr2\\nconsole.log(arr1); // [1, 2, 3, 4] - arr1 also \\\"sees\\\" the change\\n\\nlet original = [1, 2, 3];\\nlet independentCopy = [...original]; // A new array is created\\nindependentCopy.push(4);\\nconsole.log(original); // [1, 2, 3] - the original array has not changed\"],[1563,\"When you access a property of an object, JavaScript first looks for it on the object itself. If the property is not found, the search continues on the object's prototype (the `__proto__` property), then on the prototype's prototype and so on along the prototype chain. The search stops when the property is found or when the chain ends (`null` is reached). This is the basis of inheritance in JavaScript.\",\"When you try to access a property of an object (for example, `obj.property`), JavaScript performs a search following a particular algorithm, known as the prototype chain lookup. This mechanism underpins prototypal inheritance in the language.\\nThe steps of a property lookup\\n\\n- Step 1: The engine checks whether the property exists directly on the object itself (among its own properties). If so, its value is returned.\\n- Step 2: If the property is not found on the object, the search moves to the object's prototype, referenced by the internal `[[Prototype]]` property (accessible as `__proto__` or via `Object.getPrototypeOf()`).\\n- Step 3: The process repeats: if the property is found on the prototype, it is returned. If not, the prototype's prototype is checked, and so on.\\n- Step 4: The search continues along the chain until an object is reached whose prototype is `null` (normally that is `Object.prototype.__proto__`). If the property is still not found, `undefined` is returned.\\nCode example\\nConsider a simple example that demonstrates the chain:\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nThe prototype chain is used everywhere in JavaScript: when creating classes with `class`, when inheriting the methods of built-in objects (for example, arrays inherit methods from `Array.prototype`), and in various frameworks to implement mixins or decorators. Understanding this order helps you avoid errors where properties get overridden accidentally, and lets you use inheritance effectively.\\nConclusion: Knowing the property lookup order is critical for debugging and for designing the architecture of JavaScript applications, especially when you work with inheritance or build your own prototype chains to reuse code.\",\"// Create a prototype object\\nconst animal = {\\n  eats: true\\n};\\n\\n// Create an object that inherits from animal\\nconst rabbit = {\\n  jumps: true\\n};\\n\\n// Set the prototype\\nObject.setPrototypeOf(rabbit, animal);\\n\\n// The property lookup:\\nconsole.log(rabbit.jumps); // 1. Its own property -> true\\nconsole.log(rabbit.eats);  // 2. Not its own, looks in animal -> true\\nconsole.log(rabbit.runs);  // 3. Not found anywhere -> undefined\"],[1564,\"Every function in JavaScript (except arrow functions) has a `prototype` property, which is an object. That object is created automatically when the function is declared and is used as the prototype for new objects created with that function in the role of a constructor (via the `new` operator). The `prototype` property holds the methods and properties that will be available to every instance created by that constructor. This is the basis of prototypal inheritance in the language.\",\"In JavaScript functions are first-class objects, and they have a special `prototype` property. That property exists only on functions (with the exception of arrow functions) and is created automatically by the engine when the function is declared. It plays a key role in the prototypal inheritance mechanism.\\n\\nWhat is a function's prototype?\\n\\nThe `prototype` property is an ordinary object, which initially has a single own property, `constructor`, referring back to the function itself. That object is used as a template, or a \\\"store of shared methods\\\", for all the objects that will be created with this function in the role of a constructor.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\n\\nWhen you create an object via `new Constructor()`, the internal `[[Prototype]]` reference of that new object (accessible through `__proto__` or `Object.getPrototypeOf()`) is set to the `Constructor.prototype` object. So if a property is not found on the object itself, JavaScript looks for it along the prototype chain, starting from `Constructor.prototype`.\\n\\nCode example\\n\\nWhere is it used?\\n\\n- To implement inheritance and code reuse (methods are stored on the prototype rather than copied into every instance).\\n\\n- When creating custom classes (before the ES6 `class` syntax appeared).\\n\\n- In libraries and frameworks to extend built-in objects (for example, adding methods to `Array.prototype`, which requires caution).\\n\\nConclusion: A function's `prototype` property is an object that becomes the prototype of every instance created via `new`. This mechanism is worth using when you need to share methods efficiently across many objects of the same type, avoiding code duplication.\",\"// Declare a constructor function\\nfunction Person(name) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n}\\n\\n// Add a method to Person's prototype\\nPerson.prototype.greet = function() {\\n    console.log(`Hello, my name is ${this.name}`);\\n};\\n\\n// Create an instance\\nconst alice = new Person('Alice');\\n\\n// The method is called from the prototype\\nalice.greet(); // Output: Hello, my name is Alice\\n\\n// Check the link\\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(alice) === Person.prototype); // true\\nconsole.log(alice.constructor === Person); // true (through the prototype chain)\"],[1565,\"When a function is called with the `new` operator, a new empty object is created. The `this` reference inside the function is bound to that new object. Then the object's internal `[[Prototype]]` property (accessible as `__proto__`) is set to the constructor function's `prototype` object. If the function does not return its own object, the `new` operator automatically returns the newly created object, which now has access to all the properties and methods defined on the constructor's `prototype`.\",\"Prototypal inheritance is JavaScript's fundamental mechanism for code reuse and for building chains of objects. Unlike classical class-based inheritance, in JavaScript objects inherit properties and methods directly from other objects via a prototype reference.\\nThe steps the new operator performs\\nWhen a function is called with the `new` operator, the following sequence of actions takes place:\\n\\n- A new empty object is created.\\n- That object is linked to the constructor function's `prototype` property. Technically, the new object's internal `[[Prototype]]` property (accessible through `__proto__`) is set equal to the `Constructor.prototype` object.\\n- The execution context `this` inside the constructor function is bound to that new object.\\n- The body of the constructor function runs (usually it adds properties to `this`).\\n- If the function does not explicitly return another object, the `new` operator automatically returns the newly created object.\\nCode example\\nConsider creating an object via a constructor and accessing methods through the prototype chain.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nThis mechanism underpinned the creation of custom data types in JavaScript before the ES6 class syntax appeared. It is used in many libraries and frameworks. Understanding prototypes is critically important for debugging (for example, when inspecting an inheritance chain), for efficient memory use (methods on a prototype are created once rather than in every instance), and for building complex patterns such as mixins or inheritance from built-in objects.\\nConclusion: The prototypal inheritance mechanism via `new` is the foundation of object-oriented programming in JavaScript. It is worth using when creating many objects of the same type where you need to save memory by moving methods onto the prototype, or when working with legacy code written before ES6.\",\"// A constructor function\\nfunction Animal(name) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n}\\n\\n// Add a method to the constructor's prototype\\nAnimal.prototype.sayName = function() {\\n    console.log('My name is ' + this.name);\\n};\\n\\n// Create an instance with new\\nconst cat = new Animal('Whiskers');\\n\\n// The method is available through the prototype chain\\ncat.sayName(); // Prints: My name is Whiskers\\n\\n// Check the prototype link\\nconsole.log(cat.__proto__ === Animal.prototype); // true\\nconsole.log(cat instanceof Animal); // true\"],[1566,\"The `new` operator creates a new empty object, binds `this` inside the constructor function being called to it, links that object's prototype to the function's `prototype` property, and returns the new object (unless the function itself returns another object). This is the standard way of creating many objects with the same structure and behaviour. For example, `new User('John')` creates an instance of a User object.\",\"The `new` operator in JavaScript is the key mechanism for creating object instances from constructor functions. It lets you create many objects with the same internal structure, inheriting properties and methods through the prototype chain.\\n\\nHow the new operator works: a step-by-step algorithm\\n\\nWhen you call a function with the `new` operator, the following sequence of actions takes place:\\n\\n- A new empty object is created.\\n\\n- The constructor function's `prototype` property is linked to that new object's internal `[[Prototype]]` property (or `__proto__`). This establishes the prototype chain.\\n\\n- The `this` keyword inside the function is bound to the object just created.\\n\\n- The body of the constructor function runs (usually it adds properties and methods to `this`).\\n\\n- If the function does not return its own object (that is, it returns a primitive or returns nothing), the `new` operator automatically returns the newly created object. If the function does explicitly return an object, that object is returned instead.\\n\\nPractical example\\n\\nConsider creating an object to represent a user:\\n\\nWhere and why it is used\\n\\nThe `new` operator was traditionally used in the \\\"Constructor\\\" pattern to create objects in an OOP style, before ES6 classes appeared. Today the `class` syntax is used more often, which internally also relies on `new` and prototypes. Understanding how `new` works is critically important for debugging, for writing libraries, for understanding inheritance and for working with legacy code.\\n\\nConclusion: Use the `new` operator (or the class syntax) when you need to create many objects with a shared structure and behaviour, especially if inheritance through the prototype chain is required. For simple one-off objects an object literal `{}` is often enough.\",\"function User(name, age) {\\n  // this automatically refers to the new object\\n  this.name = name;\\n  this.age = age;\\n  // A method could be added right here, but the prototype is more efficient\\n}\\n\\n// Add the method to the prototype so all instances share it\\nUser.prototype.greet = function() {\\n  return `Hello, I'm ${this.name}!`;\\n};\\n\\n// Using new\\nconst user1 = new User('Alice', 30);\\nconsole.log(user1.name); // 'Alice'\\nconsole.log(user1.greet()); // 'Hello, I'm Alice!'\\nconsole.log(user1 instanceof User); // true\"],[1567,\"Copying by value creates an independent copy of the data. Changes to the copy do not affect the original. Copying by reference creates a new reference to the same data in memory. Changes made through either reference are visible to all of them. Primitive types (numbers, strings in JS) are copied by value, while objects and arrays are copied by reference. Understanding this helps you avoid unexpected mutations.\",\"In programming, passing data between variables or into functions can happen in two fundamentally different ways, which affect the behaviour of the program and the management of memory.\\nCopying by value (Pass by Value)\\nWhen copying by value, a new, independent copy of the value itself is created. After the assignment the two variables hold identical data, but in different memory cells. Changing one variable has no effect at all on the other. This mechanism is normally used for primitive data types, such as numbers, booleans and strings (in some languages strings may be immutable objects, but the semantics of passing them often remain value-like).\\n\\nCopying by reference (Pass by Reference)\\nWhen copying by reference, what is copied is not the value itself but the address (reference) of the region of memory where the data is stored. As a result, several variables start pointing at the same object in memory. A change to the data through one variable will immediately be visible when accessed through another. This is characteristic of compound types: objects, arrays, dictionaries.\\n\\nPractical application and nuances\\nUnderstanding the difference is critical when working with functions that modify their arguments and when managing state in applications. For example, in React state must be updated immutably, creating new objects rather than mutating the old ones. To create a genuinely independent copy of an object you need to use methods such as `Object.assign()`, the spread operator `{...obj}` or `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj))` for a deep copy.\\nIt is worth noting that in some languages (for example, Python) the semantics of passing arguments into functions is described as \\\"call by object reference\\\", where the behaviour depends on the mutability of the object.\\nConclusion: Use your knowledge of the kinds of copying to stay in control of your data: copying by value is safe and predictable, while when working with references you must deliberately create deep copies when you need to isolate changes, or deliberately share data for efficiency.\",\"// Example in JavaScript (primitives are copied by value)\\nlet a = 10;\\nlet b = a; // The value 10 is copied\\nb = 20;    // Change the copy\\nconsole.log(a); // 10 - the original has not changed\\nconsole.log(b); // 20\\n\\n// Example in JavaScript (objects are copied by reference)\\nlet obj1 = { name: 'Alice' };\\nlet obj2 = obj1; // The reference is copied, not the object\\nobj2.name = 'Bob'; // Change the property through the second reference\\nconsole.log(obj1.name); // 'Bob' - the original changed!\\nconsole.log(obj2.name); // 'Bob'\"],[1568,\"When a constructor function is called with the `new` operator, a new empty object is created and the `this` keyword inside the function starts referring to that object. After the function's code runs, the `new` operator automatically returns that new object, unless the function itself returns another object. If the function returns a primitive value (for example a number or a string), it is ignored and the created object is returned.\",\"In JavaScript the `new` operator is used to create an object instance from a constructor function. Understanding this process matters for working with object-oriented programming in the language.\\n\\nHow the new operator works\\n\\nWhen `new Constructor()` is called, the following sequence of steps takes place:\\n\\n- A new empty object is created.\\n\\n- The constructor function's `prototype` property is linked to the new object's prototype (the object gains access to the prototype's methods and properties).\\n\\n- The `this` keyword inside the constructor is bound to the object just created.\\n\\n- The code of the constructor function runs (usually it adds properties to `this`).\\n\\n- If the function does not return its own object, the `new` operator automatically returns the new object referenced by `this`. If the function returns a primitive value (number, string, boolean, null, undefined), it is ignored and the object is returned. If the function explicitly returns another object, that object is returned instead.\\n\\nCode examples\\n\\nConsider the standard case:\\n\\nAn example with an explicitly returned primitive (ignored):\\n\\nAn example returning another object (that object is returned):\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\nConstructor functions and the `new` operator were historically used to create many objects with the same structure and behaviour, especially before ES6 classes appeared. Today this mechanism underpins the behaviour of built-in objects (for example, `new Date()`, `new Array()`) and many libraries. Understanding it is essential for debugging, for writing your own libraries and for understanding prototypal inheritance.\\n\\nConclusion: Use constructor functions with `new` when you need to create many objects of the same type with shared methods via a prototype, especially in a code base that does not use the ES6 class syntax.\",\"function User(name) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n  this.isAdmin = false;\\n  // Implicit return of this\\n}\\n\\nlet john = new User('John');\\nconsole.log(john.name); // John\\nconsole.log(john instanceof User); // true\\n\\nfunction Car(model) {\\n  this.model = model;\\n  return 42; // A primitive - it will be ignored\\n}\\n\\nlet myCar = new Car('Toyota');\\nconsole.log(myCar.model); // Toyota (the object was created)\\n\\nfunction Animal(type) {\\n  this.type = type;\\n  // Return a completely different object\\n  return { custom: 'object' };\\n}\\n\\nlet dog = new Animal('Dog');\\nconsole.log(dog.type); // undefined\\nconsole.log(dog.custom); // 'object'\\nconsole.log(dog instanceof Animal); // false\"],[1569,\"In JavaScript objects and arrays are assigned by reference, not by value. This means that with the operation `let b = a`, where `a` is an object, the variable `b` receives a reference to the same region of memory as `a`. Changes to properties through `b` will affect `a`, and vice versa. To create an independent copy you need to use cloning methods, for example `Object.assign()` or the spread operator `...`.\",\"In JavaScript primitive types (numbers, strings, booleans) are assigned by value, that is, a new copy of the data is created. Objects, however (including arrays and functions), are assigned by reference. That means a variable stores not the object itself but the address in memory where it is located.\\n\\nThe principle\\n\\nWhen you assign an object or array to another variable, you copy only that reference, not the contents. Both variables start pointing at the same object in memory. Any changes made through one variable are immediately visible through the other.\\n\\nAn example and its consequences\\n\\nHow do you create an independent copy?\\n\\nTo avoid unwanted sharing of data, you need to create a new object or array by copying the contents of the old one. This is called shallow cloning.\\n\\n- For objects: use the spread operator `{...obj}` or `Object.assign({}, obj)`.\\n\\n- For arrays: use the spread operator `[...arr]`, the `arr.slice()` method or `Array.from(arr)`.\\n\\nIt is important to remember that this is shallow copying. If an object's properties are themselves objects, they will still be copied by reference. For deep copying you will need more involved methods, for example `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj))` (with limitations) or library functions.\\n\\nWhere is this used?\\n\\nUnderstanding this mechanism is essential when working with state in React, Redux or Vue, where an unintended mutation of the state object can lead to bugs and debugging difficulties. It also matters when passing objects as function arguments, when working with APIs that return complex data structures, and in any operation that requires modifying data without affecting the original.\\n\\nConclusion: Assignment by reference is a fundamental feature of JavaScript for objects and arrays. It lets you work efficiently with large data structures, but demands caution. Always create explicit copies when you need to change data without touching the original object.\",\"const original = { name: 'Alice', age: 30 };\\nconst copy = original; // The reference is copied, not the object\\n\\ncopy.age = 31;\\nconsole.log(original.age); // 31 - the original object changed too!\\n\\n// The same with arrays\\nconst arr1 = [1, 2, 3];\\nconst arr2 = arr1;\\narr2.push(4);\\nconsole.log(arr1); // [1, 2, 3, 4]\\n\\nconst shallowCopy = { ...original };\\nshallowCopy.age = 40;\\nconsole.log(original.age); // 31 - the original stayed unchanged\"],[1570,\"Primitive types (numbers, strings, booleans) are copied by value. On assignment an independent copy of the value is created. Objects (including arrays and functions) are copied by reference. On assignment the variable receives a reference to the same object in memory, so changes through one variable are visible through the other. This fundamental difference affects the logic of how you work with data.\",\"In JavaScript data divides into primitive types (number, string, boolean, null, undefined, symbol, bigint) and objects (including arrays, functions, dates). On assignment, or when passed into a function, they behave fundamentally differently because of the way they are stored in memory.\\n\\nPrimitive types: copying by value\\n\\nWhen you assign a variable holding a primitive value to another variable, a new, independent copy of that value is created. The two variables are then unconnected in any way. Changing one will not affect the other.\\n\\nObjects: copying by reference\\n\\nObjects are stored in memory, and a variable holds only a reference (an address) to that object. On assignment it is precisely that reference that is copied, not the object itself. As a result several variables start pointing at the same object in memory.\\n\\nThe same happens when an object is passed into a function — the function receives the reference and can change the original object (a side effect).\\n\\nPractical consequences and creating copies\\n\\nUnderstanding this mechanism matters for managing state, especially in React, Redux or when working with arrays. To avoid unwanted mutations you often need to create real copies of objects.\\n\\n- Shallow copy of an object: `const copy = { ...original };` or `Object.assign({}, original)`.\\n\\n- Shallow copy of an array: `const copy = [...original];` or `original.slice()`.\\n\\n- Deep copying (for nested objects) can be done with `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj))` (with limitations) or libraries such as Lodash.\\n\\nConclusion: Primitives are copied by value, which makes working with them predictable and safe. Objects are copied by reference, which demands care when modifying them and often makes explicit copies necessary to isolate the data, especially in architectures sensitive to side effects.\",\"let a = 10;\\nlet b = a; // b receives a copy of the value 10\\nb = 20;\\nconsole.log(a); // 10 - a has not changed\\nconsole.log(b); // 20\\n\\nlet obj1 = { name: 'Alice' };\\nlet obj2 = obj1; // obj2 receives a reference to the same object\\nobj2.name = 'Bob';\\nconsole.log(obj1.name); // 'Bob' - the change is visible through obj1\\nconsole.log(obj2.name); // 'Bob'\\n\\n// An example of shallow copying\\nconst original = { x: 1, nested: { y: 2 } };\\nconst shallowCopy = { ...original };\\nshallowCopy.x = 99; // Does not affect original\\nshallowCopy.nested.y = 100; // Does affect original.nested!\\nconsole.log(original.x); // 1\\nconsole.log(original.nested.y); // 100\"],[1571,\"Division by zero is allowed in JavaScript because the language uses floating-point numbers per the IEEE 754 standard. Dividing a positive number by zero yields positive infinity (`Infinity`), and dividing a negative number by zero yields negative infinity (`-Infinity`). Dividing zero by zero gives the special value `NaN` (Not a Number), which indicates an undefined result. This behaviour lets computations continue rather than raising an immediate error, which gives the developer the chance to handle such cases explicitly.\",\"In JavaScript all numbers are represented as 64-bit double-precision floating-point values (the IEEE 754 standard). That standard defines special values to represent infinities and \\\"not a number\\\", which makes arithmetic operations more resilient.\\n\\nSpecial numeric values\\n\\nThe IEEE 754 standard introduces several special values:\\n\\n- `Infinity` and `-Infinity` — represent positive and negative infinity\\n\\n- `NaN` (Not a Number) — represents the result of an undefined or invalid operation\\n\\nBehaviour on division by zero\\n\\nUnlike some languages, which raise an exception on division by zero, JavaScript returns one of the special values:\\n\\nPractical application and handling\\n\\nThis behaviour lets programs carry on executing even when mathematically invalid operations occur. The developer can check the results explicitly:\\n\\nComparison specifics\\n\\nWhen working with these values it is important to remember a few peculiarities:\\n\\n- `Infinity === Infinity` returns `true`\\n\\n- `NaN === NaN` returns `false` (use `isNaN()` or `Number.isNaN()` to check)\\n\\n- `Infinity > Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` returns `true`\\n\\nThis behaviour is especially useful in scientific computations and algorithms, where division by zero may arise as an intermediate result but should be handled at a higher level of the application's logic.\\n\\nConclusion: Allowing division by zero in JavaScript is a deliberate design choice that makes the language more resilient to errors in numeric computations. It is useful in situations where boundary cases need to be handled programmatically rather than interrupting execution with an exception, especially in web applications where fault tolerance matters.\",\"console.log(5 / 0);      // Infinity\\nconsole.log(-5 / 0);     // -Infinity\\nconsole.log(0 / 0);      // NaN\\nconsole.log(-0 / 0);     // NaN\\n\\nfunction safeDivide(a, b) {\\n  const result = a / b;\\n  \\n  if (isNaN(result)) {\\n    return \\\"The result is undefined\\\";\\n  }\\n  \\n  if (!isFinite(result)) {\\n    return \\\"The result is infinite\\\";\\n  }\\n  \\n  return result;\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(safeDivide(10, 2));   // 5\\nconsole.log(safeDivide(10, 0));   // \\\"The result is infinite\\\"\\nconsole.log(safeDivide(0, 0));    // \\\"The result is undefined\\\"\"],[1572,\"Properties added directly to a constructor function are static and belong to the function itself as an object; they are not inherited by instances. Properties added to the function's `prototype` object are shared by all instances created via `new` and form the basis of the prototype chain. For example, `Array.isArray` is a static method, while `Array.prototype.push` is an instance method.\",\"In JavaScript functions are objects, and they have two important sets of properties: the function's own properties and the properties of its `prototype` object. Understanding this difference is critical for working with object-oriented programming and inheritance.\\n\\nProperties of the function (static properties)\\n\\nWhen you add a property or method directly to a function, it becomes that function's own property. Such properties are often called static. They belong to the constructor function itself as an object and are not passed on to instances created with the `new` operator. They are useful for utility methods that are logically related to the constructor but do not require an instance.\\n\\nProperties of the prototype object\\n\\nEvery function (except arrow functions) receives a `prototype` property on creation, which is an ordinary object. Properties and methods added to that object become available to every instance created via `new Constructor()`. When an instance is created, its internal `[[Prototype]]` reference (accessible through `__proto__` or `Object.getPrototypeOf()`) is set to the `Constructor.prototype` object. This is the basis of prototypal inheritance.\\n\\nPractical application and conclusion\\n\\nStatic properties of a function are used for functionality relating to the class as a whole (for example, utility methods like `Array.isArray`, `Math.max`, or constants). `prototype` properties are used to define methods and shared properties that every instance should have. When looking up a property on an object, JavaScript first checks the instance's own properties and then climbs the prototype chain.\\n\\nSummary: Use static properties for methods/data shared at the constructor level, and `prototype` properties for methods/data shared by all instances. This is a key pattern for organising code and implementing inheritance in JavaScript before the arrival of ES6 classes.\",\"function Car(model) {\\n  this.model = model;\\n}\\n// A static property of the function\\nCar.defaultModel = 'Unknown';\\n// A static method\\nCar.getDefault = function() {\\n  return Car.defaultModel;\\n};\\n\\nconst myCar = new Car('Tesla');\\nconsole.log(myCar.model); // 'Tesla'\\nconsole.log(myCar.defaultModel); // undefined - not inherited!\\nconsole.log(Car.getDefault()); // 'Unknown' - called on the constructor\\n\\nfunction Car(model) {\\n  this.model = model;\\n}\\n// The method is added to prototype\\nCar.prototype.getModel = function() {\\n  return this.model;\\n};\\n// The property is added to prototype\\nCar.prototype.wheels = 4;\\n\\nconst myCar = new Car('Tesla');\\nconsole.log(myCar.getModel()); // 'Tesla' - the method is found on the prototype\\nconsole.log(myCar.wheels); // 4 - the property is found on the prototype\\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(myCar) === Car.prototype); // true\"],[1573,\"When two variables that point at the same object are compared with the strict (`===`) or loose (`==`) equality operator, the result will be `true`. That happens because both variables hold a reference to the same region of memory where the object is stored. In JavaScript objects are compared by reference, not by content. Even if two objects have identical properties and values but were created separately, comparing them returns `false`.\",\"In JavaScript objects (including arrays and functions) are reference types. That means a variable assigned an object stores not the object itself but a reference (an address in memory) to it. When you assign that variable to another variable, it is precisely the reference that is copied, not the object. As a result both variables start pointing at the same object in memory.\\n\\nComparison by reference\\n\\nThe equality operators (`==` and `===`), when working with objects, compare those references, not the contents of the objects. If two variables hold a reference to the same object, the comparison returns `true`. Otherwise, even if the objects look identical, the result will be `false`.\\n\\nCode example\\n\\nWhere is this used?\\n\\nUnderstanding reference equality is critically important in several scenarios:\\n\\n- Performance optimisation: React and other libraries use comparison by reference to determine whether props or state have changed, so as to avoid unnecessary re-renders.\\n\\n- Working with state in Redux: Reducers must return a new state object rather than mutating the old one, so that comparing references reveals the change.\\n\\n- Cache management and memoisation: Functions like `React.memo` or libraries (Reselect) rely on comparing the references of their input arguments.\\n\\nHow do you compare the contents of objects?\\n\\nTo compare by content (a deep comparison) you need to use special methods:\\n\\n- Walking the properties manually (only for simple objects).\\n\\n- `JSON.stringify()` — if the object contains no functions, undefined values or circular references.\\n\\n- Using libraries, for example Lodash with the `_.isEqual()` function.\\n\\nConclusion: Comparing variables that point at the same object always gives true, since identical references are being compared. This is fundamental JavaScript behaviour that you need to account for when working with application state, optimising, and avoiding errors caused by unintended mutation of data.\",\"// Create an object\\nconst obj1 = { name: 'Alice' };\\n\\n// Assign a reference to that object to another variable\\nconst obj2 = obj1;\\n\\n// Compare the variables\\nconsole.log(obj1 === obj2); // true\\n\\n// Create a new object with the same content\\nconst obj3 = { name: 'Alice' };\\n\\n// Compare obj1 and obj3\\nconsole.log(obj1 === obj3); // false\\nconsole.log(obj1 == obj3);  // false (loose equality also gives false)\\n\\n// An example of deep comparison via JSON.stringify (with limitations)\\nconst objA = { a: 1, b: { c: 2 } };\\nconst objB = { a: 1, b: { c: 2 } };\\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(objA) === JSON.stringify(objB)); // true\"],[1574,\"JavaScript automatically converts values of different types to a single type before performing arithmetic operations. Different rules apply to the `+`, `-`, `*`, `/` and `%` operators. The `+` operator can perform both numeric addition and string concatenation, which often causes confusion. For the other arithmetic operators (`-`, `*`, `/`, `%`) the operands are coerced to numbers. The values null, undefined, true, false and empty strings are also converted by specific rules.\",\"Implicit type conversion (type coercion) is an automatic process in JavaScript that tries to bring operands to a compatible type when an operation is applied to values of different types. It matters especially in arithmetic operations, where the behaviour depends on the operator used and on the types of the operands.\\n\\nRules for the + operator (addition/concatenation)\\n\\nThe `+` operator is unique because it is overloaded: it works both as numeric addition and as string concatenation. If at least one operand is a string, concatenation happens and the other operand is implicitly converted to a string.\\n\\nIf neither operand is a string, JavaScript tries to convert both operands to numbers and perform addition.\\n\\nRules for the other arithmetic operators (-, *, /, %)\\n\\nFor subtraction, multiplication, division and remainder, both operands are always coerced to numbers where possible.\\n\\nHow it is used and where it matters\\n\\nUnderstanding implicit conversion is critical when handling user input (which often arrives as a string), in calculations in financial applications, and when debugging unexpected behaviour. For example, reading a value from an input field via `input.value` always returns a string, and using it directly in a formula can give an incorrect result.\\n\\nConclusion: Implicit type conversion is a powerful but dangerous mechanism. To write reliable code it is recommended to use explicit conversion (for example, `Number()`, `String()`, `parseInt()`) wherever the types may be non-obvious, especially when working with user data or external APIs.\",\"console.log(5 + '5'); // '55' (the number 5 converted to the string '5')\\nconsole.log('Hello, ' + 2024); // 'Hello, 2024'\\nconsole.log(true + ' is true'); // 'true is true' (the boolean true converted to the string 'true')\\n\\nconsole.log(true + false); // 1 (true -> 1, false -> 0)\\nconsole.log(null + 10); // 10 (null -> 0)\\nconsole.log(undefined + 10); // NaN (undefined -> NaN)\\n\\nconsole.log('10' - 5); // 5 (the string '10' converted to the number 10)\\nconsole.log('10' * '2'); // 20 (both strings converted to numbers)\\nconsole.log('abc' / 2); // NaN (the string 'abc' cannot be converted to a number)\\nconsole.log('5' % '2'); // 1 (both strings converted to numbers)\\n\\n// Bad: concatenation may happen instead of addition\\nconst price = document.getElementById('price').value; // a string, for example '100'\\nconst tax = 20;\\nconst total = price + tax; // '10020' - a bug!\\n\\n// Good: explicit conversion\\nconst totalCorrect = Number(price) + tax; // 120\"],[1575,\"In JavaScript the boolean values true and false can be implicitly converted to numbers. In such a conversion true becomes 1 and false becomes 0. This happens, for example, when arithmetic operators are used (addition, subtraction) or with functions that expect a numeric argument. Understanding this rule helps you avoid errors when mixing types in computations.\",\"In JavaScript, as in many languages, there is a mechanism of implicit type conversion (type coercion). When a boolean value takes part in an operation where a number is expected, it is converted automatically. This is a fundamental rule of the language.\\n\\nThe conversion rules\\n\\nThe conversion is strictly defined:\\n\\n- The value `true` is converted to the number `1`.\\n\\n- The value `false` is converted to the number `0`.\\n\\nWhere is this used?\\n\\nThis conversion is often encountered in the following scenarios:\\n\\n- Arithmetic operations: In addition, subtraction and multiplication a boolean value is coerced to a number.\\n\\n- Functions that expect numbers: For example, `Math.max(true, false)` returns 1.\\n\\n- Comparisons: In loose comparisons (using `==`) a conversion to a number can take place.\\n\\n- Increment/decrement: The `++` and `--` operators also trigger the conversion.\\n\\nCode examples\\n\\nLet's look at a few practical examples:\\n\\nImportant nuances\\n\\nTwo key points are worth remembering:\\n\\n- The addition operator (`+`) can also work as a string concatenator. If one of the operands is a string, the boolean is converted to a string (`'true'` or `'false'`), not to a number.\\n\\n- For explicit and predictable conversion it is better to use the `Number()` function or the unary plus (`+true`).\\n\\nConclusion: Converting booleans to numbers (true -> 1, false -> 0) is a basic JavaScript rule that is actively used in arithmetic operations and comparisons. Understanding this mechanism is critically important for writing correct code, especially when working with mixed data types. To avoid surprises in complex expressions, explicit conversion is recommended.\",\"// Arithmetic operations\\nconsole.log(true + 5);   // 6 (true -> 1, 1 + 5 = 6)\\nconsole.log(false * 10); // 0 (false -> 0, 0 * 10 = 0)\\n\\n// Use in functions\\nconsole.log(Math.abs(false)); // 0\\nconsole.log(Number(true));    // 1 (explicit conversion)\\n\\n// Comparisons\\nconsole.log(true == 1);  // true (loose comparison, true -> 1)\\nconsole.log(false == 0); // true (false -> 0)\\n\\n// Increment\\nlet flag = true;\\nflag++; // flag is now 2 (true -> 1, then incremented to 2)\\nconsole.log(flag);\\n\\n// The case with a string\\nconsole.log(true + '5'); // 'true5' (concatenation, not addition)\\n\\n// Explicit conversion\\nconsole.log(Number(false)); // 0\\nconsole.log(+true);         // 1\"],[1576,\"If a constructor function does not return an explicit value (or returns a primitive), the `new` operator creates a new object, binds it to `this` inside the function and returns that new object. This is the standard behaviour. If, however, the function explicitly returns another object, the `new` operator ignores the automatically created object and returns precisely that explicitly specified object.\",\"In JavaScript a function becomes a constructor when called with the `new` operator. Understanding what happens when such a function does not return a value explicitly is key to working with object-oriented patterns in the language.\\n\\nThe mechanics of the new operator\\n\\nWhen a function is called with `new`, the JavaScript engine performs the following steps:\\n\\n- A new empty object is created.\\n\\n- That function's `prototype` property is linked to the new object's internal `[[Prototype]]` property (or `__proto__`).\\n\\n- The newly created object is bound to the `this` keyword inside the function's execution context.\\n\\n- The body of the function runs (usually it modifies `this`, adding properties).\\n\\n- If the function does not return its own object (that is, it returns `undefined` or a primitive value), the `new` operator automatically returns the object just created and bound to `this`. If the function returns another object, that object is returned instead.\\n\\nCode examples\\n\\nConsider the standard case, where the function has no `return` statement:\\n\\nHere `new Person` creates an object, assigns it to `this`, runs `this.name = name`, and then, since there is no return value (effectively `undefined` is returned), the `new` operator returns the automatically created object.\\n\\nNow consider the case where the function returns a primitive:\\n\\nDespite the `return 42`, the `new` operator ignores the primitive value and still returns the object created in step 1.\\n\\nAnd finally the case of explicitly returning an object:\\n\\nHere the `new` operator sees that the function returns an object and returns precisely that one, completely ignoring the object created in step 1 and bound to `this`.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\nThis behaviour underpins classical inheritance through constructors and prototypes in ES5. Understanding this mechanism is critical for:\\n\\n- Writing your own libraries or frameworks.\\n\\n- Debugging unexpected behaviour when a constructor function accidentally returns a value.\\n\\n- Implementing patterns such as object caching or pools, where the constructor may return a previously created instance.\\n\\nConclusion: The standard and expected behaviour of a constructor is not to return a value explicitly, relying on the `new` operator's automatic object creation. Explicitly returning an object is useful in specific cases, for example to implement singletons or memoisation, but it demands care, as it breaks the standard semantics of `instanceof`.\",\"function Person(name) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n  // No return statement\\n}\\n\\nconst john = new Person('John');\\nconsole.log(john.name); // 'John'\\nconsole.log(john instanceof Person); // true\\n\\nfunction Car(model) {\\n  this.model = model;\\n  return 42; // Returns a primitive\\n}\\n\\nconst myCar = new Car('Tesla');\\nconsole.log(myCar.model); // 'Tesla'\\nconsole.log(myCar instanceof Car); // true\\n\\nfunction Animal(type) {\\n  this.type = type;\\n  return { custom: 'object' }; // Explicitly returns another object\\n}\\n\\nconst dog = new Animal('Mammal');\\nconsole.log(dog.custom); // 'object'\\nconsole.log(dog.type); // undefined\\nconsole.log(dog instanceof Animal); // false\"],[1577,\"In React functional components the equivalent of `componentWillUnmount` is the cleanup function returned from the `useEffect` hook. The `useEffect` hook lets you perform side effects. If you return a function from its callback, React will run it when the component unmounts. This is the ideal place to unsubscribe from events, cancel timers or release resources.\",\"In React class components the `componentWillUnmount` method is used to release resources before the component is removed from the DOM. In functional components that use hooks, this logic is implemented with the `useEffect` hook.\\n\\nHow useEffect works with cleanup\\n\\nThe `useEffect` hook takes two arguments: an effect function and an optional dependency array. If the effect function returns another function, that returned function is run by React for cleanup. The cleanup happens in two cases: immediately before the next run of the same effect (if the dependencies changed) and when the component unmounts.\\n\\nPractical example\\n\\nConsider a component that subscribes to an external event, for example a change in the window size. On unmount the subscription must be removed to avoid memory leaks.\\n\\nKey points\\n\\n- An empty dependency array (`[]`) guarantees that the effect (and the cleanup) runs only once, on mount/unmount.\\n\\n- An array with dependencies will make React run the cleanup before every new run of the effect, if the dependencies changed.\\n\\n- The cleanup function has access to the closure (the values of variables and props) at the moment the effect was created, which helps avoid \\\"stale\\\" values.\\n\\nConclusion: Use the function returned from `useEffect` for any cleanup logic (cancelling network requests, timers, subscriptions). This is the main and only way to imitate `componentWillUnmount` in React functional components.\",\"import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction WindowSizeTracker() {\\n  const [windowWidth, setWindowWidth] = useState(window.innerWidth);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // The effect: subscribe to the event\\n    const handleResize = () => setWindowWidth(window.innerWidth);\\n    window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);\\n\\n    // The cleanup function: the equivalent of componentWillUnmount\\n    return () => {\\n      window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);\\n      console.log('EventListener removed, the component is unmounting.');\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty dependency array = the effect runs only on mount\\n\\n  return <div>Window width: {windowWidth}px</div>;\\n}\"],[1578,\"The main approaches are: passing the user's timezone to the server via headers (for example `Accept-Language` or a custom header) or cookies, determining the zone from the IP address (less accurate), and storing the preference in the user's profile. On the server, dates are converted to UTC for storage and computation, and at render time into the user's local time. It is important to use libraries such as `date-fns-tz` or `Luxon`, which handle timezones correctly both on the server and on the client.\",\"With server-side rendering (SSR) dates are generated on the server but must be displayed according to the user's timezone. If that is not done, users in different regions will see incorrect times, which degrades the user experience, especially in calendars, booking systems or event feeds.\\nThe main approaches to determining the timezone\\n\\n- Via HTTP headers: The client can send the timezone in a custom header (for example, `X-User-Timezone: America/New_York`). Browsers also send the locale in `Accept-Language`, but it does not always accurately reflect the timezone.\\n- Via cookies: On the first visit you can determine or ask for the zone, save it in a cookie and send it with every request.\\n- By IP address: Server-side services (for example MaxMind) let you determine the region from the IP approximately, but this method is not accurate for VPNs or mobile networks.\\n- Via profile settings: The most reliable way is to let the user choose their timezone explicitly in their account settings and store it in the database.\\nHandling on the server and the client\\nAll dates in the database and in internal logic should be stored in UTC. With SSR the server receives information about the user's timezone, converts the UTC dates into that zone and inserts the formatted strings into the HTML. On the client, JavaScript can re-check and correct the display if needed.\\nCode example (Node.js with Express)\\n\\nOn the client you can use the Intl API to check or reformat if the zone has changed.\\nConclusion\\nUse a combined approach: determine the zone via headers or IP on the first visit, invite the user to confirm it in their settings, and store it in the database. Always work with dates in UTC on the server, converting only at the rendering stage. This will ensure consistency of the data and correct display for users all over the world.\",\"// The server side (Express + date-fns-tz)\\nconst express = require('express');\\nconst { format, utcToZonedTime } = require('date-fns-tz');\\n\\nconst app = express();\\n\\napp.get('/api/events', (req, res) => {\\n    // 1. Get the timezone from a header, a cookie or the database\\n    const userTimeZone = req.headers['x-user-timezone'] || 'UTC';\\n    \\n    // 2. The date from the database, in UTC\\n    const eventDateUtc = new Date('2023-12-25T10:00:00Z');\\n    \\n    // 3. Convert it into the user's zone\\n    const userDate = utcToZonedTime(eventDateUtc, userTimeZone);\\n    \\n    // 4. Format it for display\\n    const formattedDate = format(userDate, 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm', { timeZone: userTimeZone });\\n    \\n    // 5. Pass it to the template (for example, for SSR)\\n    res.render('event', { date: formattedDate, timeZone: userTimeZone });\\n});\"],[1579,\"Calling alert blocks the main thread of execution and the browser's Event Loop. While the alert window is open, setInterval callbacks cannot run, because the Event Loop is suspended. The timers (intervals) keep \\\"ticking\\\" in the background, but their callbacks are only placed in the task queue after the alert is closed and the thread is freed. Once the alert is closed, all the accumulated callbacks run immediately, one after another, without honouring the original interval.\",\"In JavaScript running in a browser, the `setInterval` and `setTimeout` functions are asynchronous and managed by the Event Loop mechanism. The Event Loop is responsible for running code, handling events and callbacks from the task queue. Some operations, however, such as `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`, are synchronous and blocking.\\n\\nHow the blocking works\\n\\nWhen `alert('Message')` is called, the browser shows a modal window and completely halts the main script execution thread and the Event Loop on the current tab. That means no other code, including event handlers, animations and timer callbacks, can run until the user closes the window.\\n\\nThe behaviour of setInterval\\n\\nThe timer created by `setInterval` keeps \\\"ticking\\\" in the background (in the sense of internal timekeeping). Every time the specified interval elapses, the corresponding task (the callback invocation) should be placed in the macrotask queue (task queue). However, since the Event Loop is blocked, these tasks cannot be processed and accumulate while waiting.\\n\\nAs soon as the user closes the `alert`, the Event Loop resumes work. All the accumulated `setInterval` callbacks (which should have run during the blockage) are immediately and sequentially taken from the queue and executed. The original interval will not be honoured between them — they will run practically back to back.\\n\\nCode example\\n\\nIn this example, if you leave the alert window open for about 5 seconds, roughly 5 callbacks will accumulate during the blockage (one per second). After the alert is closed, the messages \\\"Interval fired: 3\\\", \\\"Interval fired: 4\\\" and so on will appear in the console immediately one after another, with no 1-second delays between them.\\n\\nPractical significance\\n\\nThis behaviour is important to bear in mind when developing interactive applications where precise timing is critical (for example, timers, animations, real time). Blocking calls such as `alert` disrupt every asynchronous process. In modern applications, instead of `alert` you should use non-blocking UI components (modal windows, notifications) that do not halt the Event Loop.\\n\\nConclusion: `setInterval` does not run during a blocking `alert`; its callbacks accumulate in the queue and run as a batch immediately after the thread is unblocked, which breaks the expected timing logic. This vividly demonstrates the single-threaded nature of JavaScript in the browser and the importance of avoiding blocking operations on the main thread.\",\"let counter = 0;\\nconst intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n    console.log(`Interval fired: ${++counter}`);\\n}, 1000);\\n\\n// After 2.5 seconds, launch the blocking alert\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n    alert('Blocking the thread for 5 seconds (close the window)');\\n}, 2500);\\n\\n// Stop the interval 10 seconds from the start\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n    clearInterval(intervalId);\\n    console.log('Interval stopped');\\n}, 10000);\"],[1580,\"The main front-end frameworks are React, Vue and Angular. React is a library for building UIs with a declarative approach and a virtual DOM. Vue is a progressive framework that integrates easily and has a simple syntax. Angular is a full-featured framework with TypeScript, built-in routing and DI. They differ in architecture, complexity and ecosystem.\",\"Front-end frameworks are tools that help developers build complex, interactive web applications by managing state, rendering and the structure of the code. Their main purpose is to increase productivity and the maintainability of code compared with using plain JavaScript.\\nKey differences between the frameworks\\nLet's look at the three most popular solutions.\\n\\n- React (a library from Meta): Focused on building the user interface. It uses a component-based approach, JSX (a syntax mixing HTML and JavaScript) and a virtual DOM to update the real DOM efficiently. React gives you a lot of freedom, but requires you to choose additional libraries for routing or state management.\\n- Vue (a progressive framework): Created by Evan You. It combines the approaches of React and Angular. It has a simple, intuitive template syntax, a reactive data system and a gentle learning curve. Vue can be used as a library to enhance individual parts of a page, or as a full framework with Vue Router and Vuex.\\n- Angular (a platform from Google): This is a full-featured framework for enterprise applications. It uses TypeScript by default and has built-in solutions for routing, an HTTP client, forms and dependency injection (DI). Its architecture is based on modules, components and services, which makes it strict and well suited to large teams.\\nCode example: a simple component\\nLet's compare what a simple counter component looks like in each of the frameworks.\\n\\nAs the examples show, React and Vue use a more imperative approach to updating state (calling a function), while Vue allows direct modification in the template. Angular requires a stricter class structure and decorators.\\nWhere they are used\\n\\n- React: Ideal for dynamic SPAs, mobile applications (React Native), and projects that need flexibility and a rich ecosystem. Widely used at Facebook, Instagram, Airbnb.\\n- Vue: Excellent for rapid prototyping, the gradual migration of legacy projects, and applications of medium complexity. Popular among startups and in the Chinese IT community.\\n- Angular: The choice for large enterprise applications where type safety, strict architecture and a complete out-of-the-box toolset matter. Used at Google, Microsoft, IBM.\\nConclusion: The choice of framework depends on the scale of the project, the team's experience and the performance requirements. React offers flexibility, Vue simplicity and progressiveness, and Angular a comprehensive solution for large, complex applications.\",\"// React (using hooks)\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>\\n        Click me\\n      </button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Vue (Composition API)\\nimport { ref } from 'vue';\\nexport default {\\n  setup() {\\n    const count = ref(0);\\n    return { count };\\n  },\\n  template: `\\n    <div>\\n      <p>You clicked {{ count }} times</p>\\n      <button @click=\\\"count++\\\">Click me</button>\\n    </div>\\n  `\\n}\\n\\n// Angular (TypeScript)\\nimport { Component } from '@angular/core';\\n@Component({\\n  selector: 'app-counter',\\n  template: `\\n    <div>\\n      <p>You clicked {{ count }} times</p>\\n      <button (click)=\\\"increment()\\\">Click me</button>\\n    </div>\\n  `\\n})\\nexport class CounterComponent {\\n  count = 0;\\n  increment() { this.count++; }\\n}\"],[1581,\"Yes, tasks will accumulate in their respective queues. The JavaScript event loop is single-threaded. While the main thread is blocked running synchronous code, the event loop cannot move to the next phase and process the tasks from the queues. However, as soon as the blockage ends, the event loop starts processing the accumulated tasks: first all the microtasks (for example, promises), and then one macrotask at a time (for example, timers, events).\",\"The Event Loop in JavaScript manages the execution of code, the collection of events and the processing of task queues. It works on a single main thread, which is also responsible for painting the UI. If that thread is blocked by a long synchronous computation (for example, a large loop), the browser cannot update the interface or handle user input — and that is exactly what UI blocking is.\\n\\nHow the queues work during a blockage\\n\\nWhile synchronous code is running, the event loop is \\\"frozen\\\" on the current task. Asynchronous operations (for example `setTimeout`, event handlers, resolved promises), however, continue to be scheduled and placed in the corresponding queues:\\n\\n- The Microtask Queue: This receives promise callbacks (`.then`/`.catch`/`.finally`), `queueMicrotask()`, and DOM mutations (in some implementations).\\n\\n- The Macrotask Queue (or Task Queue): This receives `setTimeout` and `setInterval` callbacks, DOM events (clicks, key presses) and I/O operations.\\n\\nWhile the main thread is busy, these tasks do not run, but they accumulate in their queues.\\n\\nAn example of the code and the behaviour\\n\\nConsider an example that demonstrates the accumulation:\\n\\nThe console output will be:\\n\\nExplanation:\\n\\n- The synchronous code runs: 'Start' is logged.\\n\\n- The `setTimeout` callback is placed in the macrotask queue, and the `Promise.resolve().then` callback in the microtask queue.\\n\\n- The 2-second blockage begins. During that time the event loop is not working, but the tasks are already in the queues.\\n\\n- After the blockage, 'End after blocking' is logged.\\n\\n- The next microtask (`Promise 2`) is placed in the microtask queue.\\n\\n- Now the event loop can carry on working. First it runs all the microtasks from the queue: 'Promise 1', then 'Promise 2'.\\n\\n- After emptying the microtask queue it takes one macrotask from the queue and runs it: 'Timeout 1'.\\n\\nPractical application and conclusions\\n\\nThis knowledge is critical for writing responsive applications. Long synchronous operations (complex computations, synchronous AJAX requests) block the handling of user events and animations, which makes the interface appear to \\\"freeze\\\". To avoid that, heavy tasks should be moved into Web Workers or split into parts using `setTimeout`/`setImmediate` or `async`/`await`, which lets the event loop periodically process other tasks and update the UI.\\n\\nSummary: Tasks really do accumulate in the queues while the UI is blocked. Understanding this mechanism helps you prevent performance problems by using asynchronous patterns correctly and avoiding blocking the main thread.\",\"console.log('Start');\\n\\n// A macrotask: a timer\\nsetTimeout(() => console.log('Timeout 1'), 0);\\n\\n// A microtask: a promise\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log('Promise 1'));\\n\\n// Simulating a long UI blockage (a synchronous loop)\\nconst start = Date.now();\\nwhile (Date.now() - start < 2000) { /* Blocking for 2 seconds */ }\\n\\nconsole.log('End after blocking');\\n\\n// One more microtask after the blockage\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log('Promise 2'));\\n\\nStart\\nEnd after blocking\\nPromise 1\\nPromise 2\\nTimeout 1\"],[1582,\"A hydration mismatch is an error that occurs when the HTML generated on the server (SSR) does not match what React tries to render on the client on the first load. React expects the DOM to be identical so that it can \\\"hydrate\\\" it by attaching event handlers. The main causes are: different times or data on the server and the client, and the use of browser APIs (for example `window`) on the server. To avoid it you need to synchronise the data, use `useEffect` for client-specific code, and libraries like `next/dynamic` for lazily loading components.\",\"A hydration mismatch is a critical error in React applications that use server-side rendering (SSR). It arises at the moment of hydration, when React on the client tries to bring the static HTML received from the server to life by attaching event handlers and state to it. If the DOM tree created by the client does not match the server HTML bit for bit, React cannot map the nodes correctly, which leads to an error and a complete re-render of the whole subtree on the client, negating the benefits of SSR.\\nThe main causes of a mismatch\\n\\n- Different data: The server and the client may use different sets of data for rendering (for example, because of caching or asynchronous requests).\\n- Client-specific logic: Accessing the `window`, `document` or `localStorage` objects directly while the component renders. On the server those objects do not exist, so the result will differ.\\n- Different times: Generating content that depends on the current time or on random values, without synchronisation.\\n- Third-party libraries: Some UI libraries may behave differently in a Node.js environment and in a browser.\\nHow to avoid a mismatch\\nThe key principle is to guarantee that the component's initial render gives absolutely the same result both on the server and on the client.\\n\\n- Use `useEffect` for client-specific code: Any operation requiring a browser API should run only after the component has mounted, inside `useEffect`. That excludes it from influencing the server render.\\n- Synchronise the data: Pass all the data needed for the initial render from the server to the client (for example, via props or global state). In Next.js `getServerSideProps` is often used for this.\\n- Control rendering with state: For components that must look different on the client, use an `isClient` flag that is set to `true` only after mounting.\\n- Use lazy loading for dynamic components: In Next.js components that must not render on the server can be wrapped in `next/dynamic` with the `ssr: false` option.\\nExample code with a solution\\nConsider a component that shows the current time but has to avoid a mismatch.\\n\\nIn this example, on the server and during the very first render on the client (during hydration) the component prints \\\"Loading...\\\". Only after mounting does `useEffect` update the state and show the real time. Since the initial HTML is identical, no mismatch occurs.\\nConclusion\\nA hydration mismatch is a problem of data and render-logic integrity between the server and the client. Avoiding it is essential to preserve the benefits of SSR: first-load speed and SEO. The main approach is a clear separation of code: everything that must be in the initial HTML has to render identically, while any client-specific logic must be isolated in `useEffect` or initiated only after hydration.\",\"import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TimeComponent() {\\n  // 1. Initialise the state identically for the server and the client\\n  const [currentTime, setCurrentTime] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // 2. Run browser-dependent code ONLY on the client\\n    setCurrentTime(new Date().toLocaleTimeString());\\n  }, []); // An empty dependency array = runs only after mounting\\n\\n  // 3. On the server and during the first render on the client, show a placeholder\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Current time: {currentTime || 'Loading...'}</p>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nexport default TimeComponent;\"],[1583,\"With SSR (server-side rendering) the main problem is the load on the server and a slower Time to First Byte (TTFB), since the server generates HTML for every request. With CSR (client-side rendering) the main problem is poor SEO and a long initial load, because the browser has to download all the JavaScript before any content is painted. CSR can also suffer from problems with indexing by search engines and with previews on social networks. A hybrid approach (for example, static generation or partial hydration) is often used to strike a balance.\",\"Server-side rendering (SSR) and client-side rendering (CSR) are the two main strategies for delivering web content, each with its own particular problems. Understanding those problems helps architects and developers choose the right approach for their project.\\nProblems of server-side rendering (SSR)\\nWith SSR the HTML page is generated on the server and sent to the client ready to display. That creates several difficulties:\\n\\n- High load on the server: Every page request requires the server's computing resources for rendering. Under heavy traffic that can demand scaling of the server infrastructure.\\n- A slower Time to First Byte (TTFB): The user waits longer for the first chunk of data from the server, since the server must complete the rendering before sending a response.\\n- Difficulty with dynamic content: Pages that depend heavily on user state or on real time may require constant re-rendering on the server, which is inefficient.\\n- Hydration problems in an SPA: If after SSR the application becomes a single-page application (SPA), the hydration process (binding JavaScript to the static HTML) can be complex and lead to a mismatch between the server and client DOM trees.\\nProblems of client-side rendering (CSR)\\nWith CSR the server sends an almost empty HTML file and a large JavaScript bundle, which then renders the interface in the user's browser. That brings other problems:\\n\\n- Poor SEO: Search bots may not execute JavaScript, or may do so with a delay, which leads to poor indexing of the content.\\n- A slow initial load: The user sees a blank page until all the JavaScript has downloaded and run. That is especially critical on slow connections.\\n- Problems with social sharing: Social networks (Facebook, Twitter), when someone tries to share a link, may not receive meta tags that are inserted dynamically by JavaScript.\\n- Dependence on browser capabilities: If the user has JavaScript disabled, the application will not work.\\nCode example and comparison\\nConsider a simple React component that displays a list of posts. With CSR it might look like this:\\n\\nTo implement SSR with Next.js, the same component can be pre-rendered on the server:\\n\\nConclusion: SSR is worth using for content-oriented sites where SEO and the speed of the first paint are critical, while CSR suits complex web applications with rich interactivity after loading, where SEO is not a priority. Modern frameworks often offer hybrid solutions (for example, static generation, incremental static regeneration, partial hydration) to minimise the drawbacks of both approaches.\",\"// A pure CSR approach (React)\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [posts, setPosts] = useState([]);\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch('/api/posts')\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => setPosts(data));\\n  }, []);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>Blog</h1>\\n      {posts.length === 0 ? (\\n        <p>Loading...</p>\\n      ) : (\\n        <ul>{posts.map(post => <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>)}</ul>\\n      )}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n// On the first request the server will send almost empty HTML.\\n// The content appears only after the fetch completes in the browser.\\n\\n// An SSR approach with Next.js (getServerSideProps)\\nexport async function getServerSideProps() {\\n  const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/posts');\\n  const posts = await res.json();\\n  return { props: { posts } };\\n}\\nfunction Blog({ posts }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>Blog</h1>\\n      <ul>{posts.map(post => <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>)}</ul>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n// The server renders the HTML with the posts and sends it to the client.\"],[1584,\"TypeScript is a programming language that extends JavaScript by adding static typing. It helps you find errors at compile time rather than while the program is running. This makes code more predictable and easier to maintain in large projects. TypeScript compiles to ordinary JavaScript, so it works everywhere JS works.\",\"TypeScript is an open-source language developed by Microsoft that is positioned as a superset of JavaScript. That means any valid JavaScript code is also valid TypeScript code. The main addition is a system of static types, which lets you describe which data types (strings, numbers, objects of a particular shape and so on) are expected in various parts of the code.\\n\\nMain concepts and benefits\\n\\nTypeScript's type system is not rigid — it is optional. You can add types to existing JS code gradually. The TypeScript compiler (tsc) analyses the code and checks that the types match before it runs. If the types do not match, it reports a compilation error, which lets you catch many common bugs (for example, trying to call a method on undefined, or passing an argument of the wrong type) while the code is still being written.\\n\\nWhere and how it is used\\n\\nTypeScript is widely used in modern front-end frameworks (React, Angular, Vue) and in back-end development on Node.js. It is especially useful in:\\n\\n- Large projects with a big code base and a team of developers, where consistency matters.\\n\\n- Libraries and APIs, where the types serve as excellent documentation and improve the experience of developers using your code.\\n\\n- Projects where reliability and predictability are critically important.\\n\\nPractical example\\n\\nConsider a simple addition function. In JavaScript it is easy to make a mistake by passing a string instead of a number.\\n\\nTypeScript lets you state the parameter types explicitly:\\n\\nConclusion: TypeScript is worth using in practically any serious JavaScript project, especially where long-term maintenance, scalability and a reduction in runtime errors matter. It adds discipline and clarity to the code, acting as a smart assistant rather than a strict overseer.\",\"// JavaScript (a potential runtime error)\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\nconsole.log(add(5, \\\"3\\\")); // Prints \\\"53\\\", not 8\\n\\n// TypeScript (the error is caught at compile time)\\nfunction add(a: number, b: number): number {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\nconsole.log(add(5, 3)); // OK, prints 8\\n// console.log(add(5, \\\"3\\\")); // Compilation error: Argument of type 'string' is not assignable to parameter of type 'number'.\"],[1585,\"For code quality control, ESLint is often used for static analysis and Husky to automate checks via Git hooks. ESLint checks the code against a set of style rules and for potential errors. Husky lets you run those checks (for example `npm run lint`) automatically before a commit, preventing problematic code from reaching the repository. This forms a protective barrier and instils discipline in the team.\",\"Code quality control is a set of practices aimed at keeping a code base clean, consistent and reliable. Two key technologies for this in the JavaScript/TypeScript ecosystem are the ESLint linter and Husky, a tool for managing Git hooks.\\nESLint: static code analysis\\nESLint analyses your JavaScript or TypeScript code, without executing it, looking for errors, style problems and discouraged constructs. It works from a set of rules, which can be configured. Popular configurations, such as `eslint:recommended` or the Airbnb style, provide ready-made rule sets.\\nESLint is applied during development: it can be run manually, integrated into a code editor (for example VS Code) to highlight problems in real time, or run as part of a CI/CD pipeline.\\n\\nHusky: automation via Git hooks\\nHusky simplifies working with Git hooks — scripts that Git runs at particular moments (for example, before a commit or before a push). Husky lets you configure those hooks easily inside the project, which makes the process standardised and mandatory for every developer.\\nThe typical use is a pre-commit hook that runs linting and possibly formatting (Prettier) or unit tests. If the script exits with an error, the commit is aborted.\\n\\nTogether these tools create an automated quality-control pipeline. A developer attempting a commit automatically triggers a code check. That prevents code which breaks the team's conventions or contains obvious syntax errors from reaching the shared branch.\\nConclusion: The practice of using ESLint with Husky is especially useful in team development, to keep the code consistent and to implement the \\\"don't break master\\\" principle. It is worth applying in any project where more than one person works on the code, or which is intended to be maintained for a long time.\",\"// An example .eslintrc.js configuration\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  env: {\\n    browser: true,\\n    es2021: true,\\n  },\\n  extends: ['eslint:recommended', 'plugin:@typescript-eslint/recommended'],\\n  parser: '@typescript-eslint/parser',\\n  plugins: ['@typescript-eslint'],\\n  rules: {\\n    'no-console': 'warn', // Warn when console.log is used\\n    'semi': ['error', 'always'], // Require a semicolon\\n  },\\n};\\n\\n// An example of the setup in package.json after installing Husky\\n{\\n  \\\"scripts\\\": {\\n    \\\"lint\\\": \\\"eslint src/**/*.{js,ts}\\\",\\n    \\\"prepare\\\": \\\"husky install\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"devDependencies\\\": {\\n    \\\"eslint\\\": \\\"^8.0.0\\\",\\n    \\\"husky\\\": \\\"^8.0.0\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n// Then the hook is created in the terminal:\\n// npx husky add .husky/pre-commit \\\"npm run lint\\\"\"],[1586,\"Figma Tokens is a plugin and a methodology for managing design tokens in Figma. Tokens are abstract variables holding the values of colours, typography, spacing and other styles. They are needed to create a single source of truth for the design, which can be synchronised with the code. This ensures consistency between the mock-ups and the implementation, speeds up updates and supports themes (for example, light and dark).\",\"Figma Tokens is a powerful Figma plugin that lets you introduce and manage a system of design tokens directly in the interface design process. Design tokens are abstract named entities that hold visual attributes of the design, such as colours, font sizes, shadow values, spacing and corner radii. Instead of using hard-coded values (for example, a hex colour code), designers and developers refer to tokens (for example, `color.primary.500`).\\n\\nHow it works and where it is used\\n\\nThe Figma Tokens plugin integrates into Figma and lets you create collections of tokens in JSON format. Those tokens can be applied to layers and styles in Figma. The key advantage is the ability to export those tokens into formats that development understands (CSS, SCSS, JavaScript, Android, iOS and others) with tools such as Style Dictionary. That creates a bridge between design and code, keeping them in sync.\\n\\nThe main usage scenarios:\\n\\n- Creating and maintaining design systems: Tokens become the foundation for every component, guaranteeing visual consistency.\\n\\n- Multi-branding and themes: You can easily create different themes (light/dark, different brands) by defining sets of values for the same tokens.\\n\\n- Workflow automation: Changing a token's value in one place automatically updates every linked element in the mock-ups and, after export, in the code base.\\n\\nPractical example\\n\\nHere is what a simple JSON file of tokens created in the plugin might look like:\\n\\nIn Figma you set a button's fill colour not as `#3b82f6` but as a reference to the token `{color.primary.500}`. If you later decide to change the brand's primary colour, you change the token's value and all the buttons in every Figma file update automatically.\\n\\nConclusion: Figma Tokens is worth using in projects where the scalability of the design matters, where consistency between the design and development teams is important, and where support for several themes or brands is planned. It is a tool for professional teams building complex, long-lived design systems.\",\"{\\n  \\\"color\\\": {\\n    \\\"primary\\\": {\\n      \\\"500\\\": { \\\"value\\\": \\\"#3b82f6\\\" },\\n      \\\"600\\\": { \\\"value\\\": \\\"#2563eb\\\" }\\n    },\\n    \\\"background\\\": {\\n      \\\"base\\\": { \\\"value\\\": \\\"#ffffff\\\" }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"spacing\\\": {\\n    \\\"md\\\": { \\\"value\\\": \\\"16px\\\" },\\n    \\\"lg\\\": { \\\"value\\\": \\\"24px\\\" }\\n  }\\n}\"],[1587,\"To embed maps in web applications, three main libraries are most often used: Leaflet, OpenLayers and the Google Maps API. Leaflet is a lightweight, easy-to-use open-source library, ideal for basic maps. OpenLayers is a more powerful, comprehensive solution for complex GIS tasks, also open source. The Google Maps API is a proprietary service from Google providing rich functionality, but requiring licensing for commercial use.\",\"In web development there are several key libraries for creating interactive maps, each with its own philosophy and area of application. The choice depends on the project's requirements: whether you need simplicity and speed, advanced GIS functionality, or integration with a popular service such as Google.\\nLeaflet\\nLeaflet is a lightweight (about 42 KB) open-source library that has become the standard for many projects thanks to its simplicity, good documentation and extensive community. It works excellently with tile maps (for example OpenStreetMap) and is easily extended with plugins.\\n\\nOpenLayers\\nOpenLayers is a powerful open-source library oriented towards complex cartographic tasks and GIS (geographic information systems). It supports a huge number of data formats (vector tiles, KML, GeoJSON) and provides fine-grained control over the display.\\n\\nGoogle Maps API\\nThe Google Maps API is a proprietary service from Google providing access to maps, streets, satellite imagery and a rich set of services (place search, routes). It requires registering an API key and, for intensive use, payment. The integration is simple, but imposes licence restrictions.\\n\\nConclusion: Leaflet is worth choosing to roll out simple maps with open data quickly. OpenLayers suits complex, customisable GIS applications. The Google Maps API is optimal when you need integration with the Google ecosystem (streets, business data) and a ready-made UI, and the project is prepared for the possible costs.\",\"// An example of creating a simple map with Leaflet\\nconst map = L.map('map').setView([51.505, -0.09], 13);\\nL.tileLayer('https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {\\n    attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'\\n}).addTo(map);\\n// Adding a marker\\nL.marker([51.5, -0.09]).addTo(map)\\n    .bindPopup('An example marker.')\\n    .openPopup();\\n\\n// An example of creating a map with OpenLayers\\nimport Map from 'ol/Map';\\nimport View from 'ol/View';\\nimport TileLayer from 'ol/layer/Tile';\\nimport OSM from 'ol/source/OSM';\\n\\nconst map = new Map({\\n    target: 'map',\\n    layers: [\\n        new TileLayer({\\n            source: new OSM()\\n        })\\n    ],\\n    view: new View({\\n        center: [0, 0],\\n        zoom: 2\\n    })\\n});\\n\\n// An example of initialising a Google Maps map\\nfunction initMap() {\\n    const map = new google.maps.Map(document.getElementById(\\\"map\\\"), {\\n        center: { lat: 51.505, lng: -0.09 },\\n        zoom: 13,\\n    });\\n    new google.maps.Marker({\\n        position: { lat: 51.5, lng: -0.09 },\\n        map: map,\\n        title: \\\"An example marker\\\"\\n    });\\n}\"],[1588,\"Canvas is a raster drawing surface. The main limitation when working with a large number of objects is performance. Every object has to be redrawn on every frame, which creates a heavy CPU load. There is no built-in system for culling invisible areas or optimising redraws. For complex scenes it is better to use WebGL or SVG.\",\"HTML5 Canvas provides a low-level API for drawing graphics on the fly with JavaScript. Being essentially a raster canvas, however, it has a number of fundamental limitations when you try to display hundreds or thousands of dynamic objects.\\nThe main performance limitations\\n\\n- No built-in DOM: Unlike SVG, objects on a Canvas are not separate DOM elements. That means there is no automatic tracking of which object is where, and no object-level events without additional logic.\\n- A full redraw: On any change to the scene (objects moving), you usually have to clear the whole canvas or part of it and redraw every object from scratch. That can become a bottleneck.\\n- No hardware acceleration by default: Standard 2D drawing (context2d) mostly uses the CPU. The canvas itself may be accelerated, but the drawing operations (fill, stroke) are not.\\nPractical problems and an example\\nImagine you are building a game with 10,000 particles. On every frame you need to update each particle's position and draw it. The code might look like this:\\n\\nThis approach quickly leads to a drop in frame rate, because the browser performs thousands of drawing calls (arc, fill) on the CPU.\\nWays to optimise, and alternatives\\n\\n- Using WebGL: For complex 2D/3D graphics, WebGL (via libraries like Pixi.js or Three.js) uses the GPU, which radically improves performance.\\n- Clipping: Draw only the part of the scene that is visible in the viewport.\\n- Combining objects: If objects are static, draw them once onto an off-screen canvas and then copy the finished image.\\n- Using sprite sheets to reduce the number of draw calls.\\nConclusion: Plain Canvas 2D is poorly suited to displaying thousands of interactive, dynamically changing objects. For such tasks it is worth considering WebGL or a hybrid approach with optimised drawing. Canvas is ideal for drawing images, simple animation or video processing, where the whole scene is redrawn infrequently.\",\"// A simplified example of an animation loop with particles\\nconst particles = []; // An array of 10000 objects {x, y, vx, vy}\\n\\nfunction animate() {\\n    // 1. Clear the whole canvas\\n    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);\\n\\n    // 2. Update and draw EVERY particle\\n    for (let p of particles) {\\n        p.x += p.vx;\\n        p.y += p.vy;\\n        ctx.beginPath();\\n        ctx.arc(p.x, p.y, 2, 0, Math.PI * 2);\\n        ctx.fill();\\n    }\\n    requestAnimationFrame(animate);\\n}\"],[1589,\"Jest is a framework for unit and integration testing, mainly of JavaScript logic (React, Node.js). Cypress and Playwright are tools for end-to-end (E2E) testing of web applications in a browser. Cypress is convenient for writing stable tests for Chrome quickly, but works only within the browser. Playwright supports several browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Safari) and lets you test more complex scenarios, including mobile emulation and working with several pages.\",\"The choice of testing tool depends on the type of functionality being checked and the level of isolation required. Jest, Cypress and Playwright solve different problems in the quality assurance process.\\nJest: a framework for unit and integration testing\\nJest is a library for testing JavaScript code. It is ideal for checking individual functions, modules, React components or server-side logic on Node.js. Jest runs fast and provides powerful facilities for mocking (substituting dependencies) and snapshot testing (comparing snapshots of data). It runs in a Node.js environment, without a real browser.\\n\\nCypress and Playwright: tools for end-to-end (E2E) testing\\nThese tools automate interaction with a real browser, imitating the user's actions: clicks, typing text, navigation. They verify that the whole application (front end + back end) works correctly together.\\n\\n- Cypress has a simple syntax and an excellent debugger, which shows snapshots of the state at every step. It runs in the same environment as the application under test, which gives direct access to the DOM but limits working with several tabs or domains.\\n- Playwright, from Microsoft, is a more versatile tool. It supports not only Chromium but also Firefox and WebKit (Safari's engine), which is critical for cross-browser testing. Playwright can emulate mobile devices, work with several pages and even intercept network requests.\\n\\nWhere and what to use?\\n\\n- Use Jest to test business logic, utilities, React hooks, Redux reducers. This is the base of the testing pyramid.\\n- Use Cypress when you need fast, stable E2E tests for an application on a single domain, with an emphasis on test-driven development (TDD) and debugging.\\n- Use Playwright when cross-browser coverage is required, for testing PWAs, for complex scenarios with files or requests, or if the application uses several windows.\\nConclusion: Jest is for testing code; Cypress and Playwright are for testing the application's behaviour in a browser. For full coverage of a project, a combination of Jest (unit/integration) and one of the E2E tools (Cypress or Playwright) is often used.\",\"// An example of a simple Jest test\\nfunction sum(a, b) {\\n  return a + b;\\n}\\n\\ntest('adds 1 + 2 to equal 3', () => {\\n  expect(sum(1, 2)).toBe(3);\\n});\\n\\n// An example of a Playwright test (TypeScript)\\nimport { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';\\n\\ntest('has title', async ({ page }) => {\\n  await page.goto('https://example.com');\\n  await expect(page).toHaveTitle('Example Domain');\\n});\"],[1590,\"Vite is a modern build tool that uses native ES modules in the browser during development, which gives an instant server start and fast hot module replacement (HMR). Its advantages include exceptionally fast development thanks to not pre-bundling all the code, built-in support for TypeScript, JSX, CSS and other assets, and an optimised production build via Rollup. Vite's limitations stem from its ES-module-oriented architecture: there may be problems with legacy browsers in development, difficulties with some Webpack-specific plugins, and a need to adapt non-standard workflows.\",\"Vite is a build tool and development server created by Evan You (the author of Vue.js) that rethinks the approach to front-end development. Instead of the traditional bundling of all the code during development, Vite uses native ES modules in the browser. That lets the development server start instantly, since it simply serves the source files and the browser loads the modules itself as needed.\\n\\nKey advantages of Vite\\n\\n- Instant server start: No build of the whole application is required; the server starts immediately, regardless of the size of the project.\\n\\n- Fast Hot Module Replacement (HMR): Module updates happen almost instantly, without reloading the page, which speeds up the development cycle.\\n\\n- An optimised production build: For the final build Vite uses Rollup, which provides an efficient dependency tree, code splitting and other optimisations.\\n\\n- Built-in support for modern technologies: TypeScript, JSX, CSS modules, PostCSS, WebAssembly and other assets work out of the box without complex configuration.\\n\\n- A Rollup-based plugin system: Lets you extend the functionality easily using the Rollup ecosystem.\\n\\nAn example of configuring a simple project\\n\\nLimitations and trade-offs\\n\\n- Browser requirements: In development mode support for native ES modules is required, which rules out old browsers (for example IE11). For production the code is transpiled.\\n\\n- The plugin ecosystem: Not every Webpack plugin is available for Vite, which may require finding alternatives or writing your own adapters.\\n\\n- Complex configurations: For non-standard scenarios (for example, custom asset transformations) the configuration may be less flexible than Webpack's.\\n\\n- Dependence on Node.js: Like most modern tools, it requires a Node.js environment.\\n\\nConclusion: Vite is ideally suited to modern front-end projects where development speed matters and an ES-module-based stack is used. It is worth choosing for new applications on React, Vue, Svelte or other frameworks where support for legacy browsers in development mode is not required. For projects with heavily customised build processes or a dependence on specific Webpack plugins, additional evaluation may be needed.\",\"// vite.config.js\\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite';\\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react';\\n\\nexport default defineConfig({\\n  plugins: [react()],\\n  server: {\\n    port: 3000,\\n    open: true\\n  },\\n  build: {\\n    outDir: 'dist',\\n    sourcemap: true\\n  }\\n});\"],[1591,\"For front-end profiling, browser tools such as Chrome DevTools and specialised utilities are used. Chrome DevTools provides the Performance and Memory tabs for recording and analysing rendering, JavaScript execution and memory leaks. Lighthouse assesses performance, accessibility and SEO, generating a report with recommendations. React DevTools profiles React components, showing their render times and unnecessary re-renders. These tools help you find bottlenecks and improve the user experience.\",\"Front-end profiling is the process of measuring and analysing a web application's performance in the browser in order to identify bottlenecks, such as slow JavaScript, unnecessary DOM repaints or memory leaks. It is essential for providing smooth user interaction and meeting the Core Web Vitals metrics.\\nThe main tools\\n\\n- Chrome DevTools: The built-in set of tools in the Chrome browser. The key tabs:\\n- Performance: Records a timeline of the page load, JS execution, styles, layout and painting. Lets you find long tasks.\\n- Memory: Helps detect memory leaks by taking heap snapshots and tracking object allocation.\\n- Network: Analyses resource load times, the request waterfall, and can simulate slow networks.\\n\\n- Lighthouse: An automated audit tool, built into DevTools or available as a CLI/Node module. It runs a series of tests and generates a report on performance, accessibility, best practices and SEO, giving concrete recommendations for improvement.\\n- React DevTools: A browser extension. The Profiler tab lets you record renders of React components to see which components re-render, how long they take, and to identify unnecessary re-renders.\\nPractical application and an example\\nSuppose you have a React application where the interface \\\"hangs\\\" while typing into a search field. You can use a combination of tools to diagnose it.\\nFirst, start a recording in the Performance tab of Chrome DevTools, type into the field and stop the recording. Look for long yellow bars (JavaScript execution) or purple ones (Layout/Reflow). Then, if the problem is in React, go to the Profiler in React DevTools, start a recording and repeat the action. You will see which components render and how long they take.\\n\\nIt is also useful to run Lighthouse periodically to get an overall report on the application's health and to track the Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), First Input Delay (FID) and Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) metrics.\\nConclusion: Front-end profiling tools (Chrome DevTools, Lighthouse, React DevTools) are essential for systematically finding and eliminating performance problems. They are worth using during development, in regression testing and when preparing for a release, to ensure the application works quickly and responsively for users.\",\"// An example of code that can cause a performance problem\\nfunction SearchComponent() {\\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');\\n  const filteredItems = hugeList.filter(item => \\n    item.name.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase())\\n  ); // An expensive operation on every character typed\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />\\n      <ul>\\n        {filteredItems.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}\\n      </ul>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n// Profiling will reveal that filtering hugeList on every onChange is the bottleneck.\\n// The solution: use useMemo to memoise filteredItems, or debounce the input.\"],[1592,\"A custom bundler configuration, for example of Webpack, often leads to problems with build performance, the difficulty of maintaining the configuration, and plugin incompatibilities. Developers may face slow builds because of suboptimal settings or duplicated code. Errors in the configuration can also cause problems with tree shaking or with loading assets. Maintaining such a configuration gets harder over time, especially when upgrading the bundler's version or its plugins.\",\"Customising a module bundler such as Webpack lets you tune the build process finely to the project's needs, but carries a number of risks and difficulties. The main problems arise from the complexity of the configuration, the need for a deep understanding of how the bundler works, and the constant need to keep the settings current as the ecosystem is updated.\\nTypical problems\\n\\n- Difficulty of maintenance and comprehension: Large custom configurations become a \\\"black box\\\" for new team members. A lack of documentation makes the problem worse.\\n- Build performance: Suboptimal settings (for example, incorrect caching, redundant plugins) can sharply increase build time, especially in large projects.\\n- Fragility and incompatibility: Plugins and loaders may conflict with each other or stop working after a major Webpack version upgrade.\\n- Tree-shaking problems: An incorrect configuration can prevent unused code from being removed effectively, inflating the final bundle.\\n- Errors in asset handling: Wrong settings for handling CSS, images or fonts can lead to runtime errors or to assets being missing in production.\\nAn example of a problematic configuration\\nConsider a fragment of a Webpack configuration where problems may be lurking:\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\nTo minimise the risks:\\n\\n- Start from ready-made configurations (for example, `create-react-app`, `@vue/cli`) and eject only when absolutely necessary.\\n- Document the non-obvious parts of the configuration and the reasons for choosing particular plugins.\\n- Update Webpack and the plugins regularly, checking compatibility.\\n- Use bundle analysis tools (for example, `webpack-bundle-analyzer`) to keep the size under control.\\n- Write tests for critical parts of the build process where possible.\\nConclusion: A custom bundler configuration is a powerful but risky tool. It is worth using when the standard facilities do not cover the project's specific requirements (for example, special asset handling, custom code splitting). In most cases it is better to rely on proven templates and the minimum necessary changes.\",\"module.exports = {\\n  // ...\\n  optimization: {\\n    // Problem: splitChunks is configured too aggressively,\\n    // which can create a lot of tiny chunks and worsen loading.\\n    splitChunks: {\\n      chunks: 'all',\\n      minSize: 0 // A dangerous setting - chunks may be too small.\\n    },\\n    // Problem: minification is disabled for production.\\n    minimize: false\\n  },\\n  module: {\\n    rules: [\\n      {\\n        test: /\\\\.js$/,\\n        // Problem: excluding node_modules can break tree shaking\\n        // for some libraries.\\n        exclude: /node_modules/,\\n        use: 'babel-loader'\\n      }\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  plugins: [\\n    // Problem: several plugins may duplicate functionality\\n    // or conflict with each other.\\n    new SomeCustomPlugin(),\\n    new SomeOtherPlugin()\\n  ]\\n};\"],[1593,\"Interaction between several SPAs can be organised through a few approaches. The simplest is using iframes, where each application runs in an isolated context and communicates via the postMessage API. A more modern way is a micro-frontend architecture, where the applications are loaded as independent modules and communicate through a global event bus or shared state. You can also use Web Components to create reusable widgets embedded into different SPAs. The choice of method depends on the requirements for isolation, performance and the complexity of the integration.\",\"When several SPA applications have to work together, for example in a portal or a complex control panel, the key task is to ensure they communicate and coordinate without a full page reload. The main approaches differ in their degree of coupling and the complexity of the implementation.\\nThe main approaches to integration\\n\\n- Iframe + postMessage: The classic method, providing complete isolation. Each SPA is loaded in a separate iframe. The `window.postMessage` API is used to exchange data.\\n- Micro frontends: An architectural style where each SPA is an independent application, deployed separately. They can be loaded dynamically and communicate through a shared global object or an event bus.\\n- Web Components: Let you encapsulate functionality in reusable custom HTML elements that can be embedded into any SPA.\\n- Module Federation (Webpack 5): Lets one application load modules from another dynamically at runtime, creating the feel of a single application.\\nExample: communicating through an event bus\\nLet's create a simple global event bus to be used by all the SPAs on the page. This lets the applications publish and subscribe to events without knowing about each other directly.\\n\\nExample: integration via an iframe\\nThis approach is good when you need to run a completely independent third-party application with maximum isolation.\\n\\nConclusion: For a quick integration with maximum isolation, use an iframe. To create a single user experience out of independently developed parts, choose a micro-frontend architecture with a shared event bus, or Module Federation. Web Components suit the creation of reusable UI components that have to work in different frameworks.\",\"// shared-event-bus.js\\n// This file is loaded onto the page before the SPAs load\\nclass EventBus {\\n  constructor() {\\n    this.listeners = {};\\n  }\\n\\n  on(event, callback) {\\n    if (!this.listeners[event]) this.listeners[event] = [];\\n    this.listeners[event].push(callback);\\n  }\\n\\n  emit(event, data) {\\n    if (this.listeners[event]) {\\n      this.listeners[event].forEach(cb => cb(data));\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Create a global instance\\nwindow.globalEventBus = new EventBus();\\n\\n// In SPA App 1 (for example, the notifications panel)\\nwindow.globalEventBus.emit('userLoggedIn', { userId: 123 });\\n\\n// In SPA App 2 (for example, the chat)\\nwindow.globalEventBus.on('userLoggedIn', (data) => {\\n  console.log('Initialising the chat for the user:', data.userId);\\n  // Chat initialisation...\\n});\\n\\n// host-app.html (the host application)\\n// <iframe id=\\\"childApp\\\" src=\\\"https://child.example.com\\\"></iframe>\\n// <script>\\n//   const frame = document.getElementById('childApp');\\n//   // Send data into the embedded SPA\\n//   frame.onload = () => frame.contentWindow.postMessage(\\n//     { type: 'userLoggedIn', userId: 123 },\\n//     'https://child.example.com'\\n//   );\\n//   // Receive messages back from it\\n//   window.addEventListener('message', (event) => {\\n//     if (event.origin !== 'https://child.example.com') return;\\n//     console.log('Message from the embedded SPA:', event.data);\\n//   });\\n// <\/script>\"],[1594,\"A monolithic front end is a single application where all the code is connected and deployed as one whole. Its main advantages are the simplicity of development, debugging and deployment in the early stages. The main disadvantages are the difficulty of scaling for large teams and slow deployments, because the whole application has to be rebuilt for any change. Micro frontends solve those problems by breaking the application into independent parts, but add complexity in coordination and infrastructure setup.\",\"A front-end application's architecture determines how the code is organised, how teams work on it and how it is deployed. The two main approaches — monolithic and micro frontends — offer different trade-offs.\\nThe monolithic front end\\nIn a monolithic architecture the whole application (every page, component, piece of logic and style) lives in one repository, is produced by one build and is deployed as a single whole. This is the classic approach, used in web development for decades.\\n\\n- Advantages: Simplicity of initial setup and development. A single code base makes navigation, refactoring and keeping styles and behaviour consistent easier. Debugging and testing are also simpler, since everything runs in one context.\\n- Disadvantages: As the application and the team grow, the monolith becomes a bottleneck. Any change, even a small one, requires rebuilding and redeploying the whole application, which slows the delivery process. It is harder for teams to work independently, since their changes can conflict. The technology stack is rigidly fixed for the entire application.\\nMicro frontends\\nMicro frontends are an architectural style in which a large application is broken into independent, autonomous parts (micro frontends) that can be developed, built and deployed by separate teams. Those parts are then integrated at the browser or server level.\\n\\n- Advantages: Independence of the teams and faster development. The ability to use different technologies and frameworks in different parts of the application. Safer, faster deployment — updating one micro frontend does not require rebuilding the whole application.\\n- Disadvantages: A significant increase in infrastructure complexity: you need mechanisms for building, integrating, routing and communicating between the micro frontends. The size of the final bundle grows because of duplicated dependencies. It is harder to keep the UI/UX uniform and to debug the application as a whole.\\nAn example of integrating micro frontends\\nConsider a simple example of using Module Federation in Webpack to load a remote micro frontend:\\n\\nIn this example the host application dynamically loads the \\\"products\\\" micro frontend from another domain. That lets the \\\"products\\\" team develop and deploy their part completely independently.\\nConclusion: A monolithic front end is worth choosing for small projects or startups, where speed to market and ease of maintenance matter. Micro frontends become justified for large, long-lived projects with several autonomous teams, where independence of development and the frequency of updates are critically important.\",\"// The host app's configuration\\n// webpack.config.js\\nconst ModuleFederationPlugin = require('webpack/lib/container/ModuleFederationPlugin');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  plugins: [\\n    new ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n      name: 'host',\\n      remotes: {\\n        mf_products: 'products@http://localhost:3001/remoteEntry.js',\\n      },\\n      shared: ['react', 'react-dom'], // Shared dependencies\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n};\\n\\n// Code in the host application for dynamic loading\\nconst ProductsApp = React.lazy(() => import('mf_products/ProductsPage'));\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>The main application</h1>\\n      <React.Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n        <ProductsApp />\\n      </React.Suspense>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[1595,\"Without Module Federation, micro frontends are integrated at build time or on the server side. The main approaches are: bundling all the modules into one bundle (build-time), using iframes for isolation, composition on the server (SSI, Edge-Side Includes), or client-side routing that loads separate applications. These methods often require rebuilding the whole application in order to update it, and create tight coupling or problems with performance and sharing state.\",\"Micro frontends are an architectural style in which a large web application is broken into independent, autonomous parts developed by different teams. Before tools such as Webpack Module Federation appeared, integrating those parts was more difficult and often created points of friction.\\nThe main approaches without Module Federation\\n\\n- Build-time integration: The separate applications are built as packages (npm packages) and imported into the main \\\"container\\\" application as dependencies. Updating any micro frontend requires rebuilding and redeploying the whole application. That negates the independence of deployment.\\n- Server-side composition: The server (for example, using Nginx, or a back-end application) assembles HTML fragments from different services. Technologies such as Server-Side Includes (SSI) or Edge-Side Includes (ESI) are used. The user receives an already assembled page. This gives good performance, but complicates the server logic and can create a dependence on the network.\\n- Integration via iframes: Each micro frontend is loaded in a separate iframe. That provides complete isolation of CSS and JavaScript, but severely limits interaction between the parts (complicated data exchange, problems with navigation and SEO) and creates overhead.\\n- Client-side integration via routing: In this approach the \\\"container\\\" application is responsible for routing. Depending on the URL, it dynamically loads and mounts the JavaScript bundle of the required micro frontend. This can be done with systematic script loading.\\n\\nThis method preserves the independence of deployment, but requires careful management of the global namespace, of library versions (the duplicate dependencies problem) and complex coordination of loading.\\nProblems and limitations\\n\\n- Duplicated dependencies: Each micro frontend may include its own copies of React, Vue and so on, which increases the amount of code downloaded.\\n- No shared runtime: It is hard to share state or components between micro frontends without hacks (for example, via the global window object).\\n- The difficulty of updates: In the build-time approach, updating a button in one micro frontend means rebuilding the whole monolith.\\n- Problems with style isolation: One micro frontend's CSS can affect the others unless strict methodologies or iframes are used.\\nConclusion: The classic approaches to micro frontends let you achieve organisational decomposition and team independence, but often sacrifice technical efficiency, performance or ease of integration. They are useful in scenarios where server-side assembly is critical for SEO, or where teams work on completely isolated parts of the application with minimal interaction.\",\"// A simplified example: the container loads a micro frontend's script\\nconst loadMicroFrontend = (name, scriptUrl) => {\\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n    const script = document.createElement('script');\\n    script.src = scriptUrl;\\n    script.onload = () => {\\n      // The script is assumed to export a global render function\\n      window[`render${name}`](`${name}-container`);\\n      resolve();\\n    };\\n    script.onerror = reject;\\n    document.head.appendChild(script);\\n  });\\n};\\n// On navigating to the '/app1' route\\nloadMicroFrontend('App1', 'http://cdn.example.com/app1.bundle.js');\"],[1596,\"Pre-commit hooks are scripts that Git runs automatically before a commit is created. They are used to check the code, for example by running linters, formatters or tests. You can set them up by hand, creating files in `.git/hooks/`, or with tools like Husky for convenience. This helps prevent code with errors or code that does not match the style from reaching the repository.\",\"Pre-commit hooks are a Git mechanism that lets you automatically perform certain actions (scripts) immediately before changes are recorded in the repository. Their main purpose is to ensure code quality control by running checks such as linting, formatting or unit tests before the changes are committed. This prevents code with obvious errors, or code that breaks the team's conventions, from entering the project's history.\\nHow to set one up by hand\\nEvery Git repository has a hidden `.git/hooks/` folder containing example scripts for various events. To create a pre-commit hook you need to:\\n\\n- Go into the `.git/hooks/` folder.\\n- Create, or rename `pre-commit.sample` to, `pre-commit` (removing the `.sample` extension).\\n- Make the file executable (`chmod +x pre-commit` on Unix systems).\\n- Write a script inside it in Bash, Python, Node.js or another language that performs the checks you need. If the script exits with a non-zero return code, the commit is aborted.\\n\\nUsing tools (Husky and lint-staged)\\nSetting them up by hand is inconvenient for team work, since hooks are not copied when the repository is cloned. That is why the combination of Husky and lint-staged is popular in modern JavaScript/Node.js projects.\\n\\n- Husky simplifies managing Git hooks, letting you configure them through the project's configuration file (for example, `package.json`).\\n- lint-staged runs particular commands (for example, ESLint, Prettier) only on the files added to the staging area (the index), which speeds the checks up.\\nAn example of the setup in a Node.js project:\\n\\nAfter installing the dependencies, initialise Husky with the `npm run prepare` command, then create a pre-commit hook that runs lint-staged:\\n\\nNow, on every attempt to commit, ESLint and Prettier will run automatically on all the changed JavaScript files. If the checks do not pass, the commit is aborted.\\nWhere it is used\\nPre-commit hooks are widely used in team development to:\\n\\n- Check code syntax and style (ESLint, Pylint, RuboCop).\\n- Format automatically (Prettier, Black).\\n- Run unit tests for the changed files.\\n- Check for secrets (keys, passwords) in the code.\\n- Validate commit messages (for example, with commitlint).\\nConclusion: Pre-commit hooks are an effective way to automate routine checks and keep a code base at a high quality. They are worth using in any project, especially when working in a team, to minimise human error and ensure a single code standard.\",\"#!/bin/bash\\n# An example of a simple pre-commit hook checking for console.log in JavaScript files\\necho \\\"Running pre-commit checks...\\\"\\nif git diff --cached --name-only | xargs grep -l \\\"console.log\\\"; then\\n    echo \\\"Error: Found console.log statements in staged files.\\\"\\n    exit 1\\nfi\\necho \\\"Checks passed.\\\"\\n\\n// package.json\\n{\\n  \\\"scripts\\\": {\\n    \\\"prepare\\\": \\\"husky install\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"devDependencies\\\": {\\n    \\\"husky\\\": \\\"^8.0.0\\\",\\n    \\\"lint-staged\\\": \\\"^13.0.0\\\",\\n    \\\"eslint\\\": \\\"^8.0.0\\\",\\n    \\\"prettier\\\": \\\"^2.0.0\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"lint-staged\\\": {\\n    \\\"*.js\\\": [\\\"eslint --fix\\\", \\\"prettier --write\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nnpx husky add .husky/pre-commit \\\"npx lint-staged\\\"\"],[1597,\"Micro frontends are an architectural style in which a web interface is broken into independent, autonomous parts that can be developed, deployed and scaled by separate teams. The main goal is to overcome the limitations of a monolithic front end in large projects by letting teams work independently with different technologies. The key approaches include build-time composition, server-side integration and client-side integration using tools such as Webpack Module Federation or frameworks like single-spa. This increases development speed and makes large applications easier to maintain.\",\"Micro frontends are an approach to front-end development inspired by microservice architecture. Instead of one large monolithic application, the interface is broken into small, loosely coupled parts — \\\"micro frontends\\\". Each part is responsible for a particular business capability (for example, the product catalogue, the basket, the user profile) and can be developed by a separate team with its own technology stack, build process and deployment cycle.\\n\\nThe main approaches to implementation\\n\\nThere are several strategies for integrating micro frontends into a single application:\\n\\n- Build-time composition: The individual micro frontends are published as packages (for example, on npm), and the main application includes them as dependencies and builds everything together. This is simple, but requires rebuilding the whole application when any micro frontend is updated.\\n\\n- Server-side integration: The server (for example, using Nginx or a dedicated composer) assembles HTML fragments from the different micro frontends into a single page. This improves performance, but adds complexity on the server.\\n\\n- Client-side integration: This is the most popular approach. The main application (the shell) dynamically loads and renders the micro frontends at runtime in the browser. This provides maximum independence of deployment.\\n\\nPopular tools and technologies\\n\\nFor client-side integration the following are often used:\\n\\n- Webpack Module Federation: Lets one application dynamically load code from another at runtime. It has become the de facto standard.\\n\\n- single-spa: A meta-framework that coordinates the loading and rendering of micro frontends written in different technologies (React, Vue, Angular).\\n\\n- Web Components: Using native browser standards to create encapsulated components that can be embedded in any application.\\n\\nA code example with Webpack Module Federation\\n\\nThe configuration of the main application (host) and of the remote micro frontend (remote).\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\nMicro frontends are especially useful in large companies with several front-end development teams working on a single product (for example, marketplaces, banking applications, corporate portals). They let you speed up development, isolate risks and modernise legacy code gradually.\\n\\nConclusion: A micro-frontend architecture is worth applying to large, long-lived web applications where the independence of teams and the frequency of deployments are critically important factors. For small projects or applications with a single team, the complexity of this architecture may outweigh its benefits.\",\"// webpack.config.js of the main application (host)\\nconst ModuleFederationPlugin = require('webpack/lib/container/ModuleFederationPlugin');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  plugins: [\\n    new ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n      name: 'hostApp',\\n      remotes: {\\n        // State where to look for the remote module\\n        mfCart: 'cartApp@http://localhost:3001/remoteEntry.js',\\n      },\\n      shared: ['react', 'react-dom'], // Shared dependencies\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n};\\n\\n// In the main application's code the React component is loaded dynamically\\nconst RemoteCart = React.lazy(() => import('mfCart/CartComponent'));\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>The shop</h1>\\n      <React.Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n        <RemoteCart />\\n      </React.Suspense>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[1598,\"For working with maps on the front end, specialised JavaScript libraries are most often used. Leaflet is a lightweight, simple library for basic maps with tile support. Mapbox GL JS provides modern vector maps with high performance and customisation. The Google Maps API is a popular commercial solution with extensive functionality, including search and routes. OpenLayers is a powerful library for complex GIS tasks with support for many data formats.\",\"Integrating maps into web interfaces has become standard for a great many applications — from delivery services to analytics dashboards. The main task is to display geographic data, provide interactivity (zoom, panning, markers) and, often, overlay your own layers of information. Ready-made libraries are used for this, which abstract away the complexities of working with tile servers, projections and rendering performance.\\nPopular libraries\\n\\n- Leaflet: Lightweight (about 42 KB), easy to learn. Ideal for quickly rolling out a basic map with markers, popups and vector layers (polygons, lines). Works with raster tiles from OpenStreetMap and other providers.\\n- Mapbox GL JS: A modern library for vector maps. The maps are drawn on the client side using WebGL, which lets you style every element (roads, buildings) dynamically and animate transitions smoothly. Requires an access token.\\n- Google Maps JavaScript API: A commercial but very popular solution. It provides not only cartography but also ready-made services: Places (place search), Directions (route building), Street View. The integration is simple, but it has limits and a cost.\\n- OpenLayers: A powerful library for professional GIS applications. It supports a huge number of data sources (WMS, GeoJSON, KML) and complex map operations. It has a higher barrier to entry.\\nCode example: a basic map with Leaflet\\n\\nThis code creates a map, adds an OpenStreetMap tile layer, a marker in the centre of Moscow and a simple polygon. Leaflet automatically handles mouse and touch events for zooming and panning.\\nCriteria for choosing an approach\\n\\n- Leaflet: Choose it for simple projects, when you need lightness, open data (OSM) and minimal dependencies.\\n- Mapbox GL JS: Suits projects where custom map design, high performance with a large amount of dynamic data and a 3D view matter.\\n- Google Maps API: Optimal if you need maps that are maximally recognisable to users, as well as integration with other Google services (search, routes, reviews).\\n- OpenLayers: Use it in complex analytical or enterprise systems, where working with professional geodata and standards is necessary.\\nConclusion: The choice of approach depends on the project's requirements: for a quick prototype or a simple map with markers, Leaflet will do; for creating a unique, high-performance cartographic interface, Mapbox GL JS; for using maps familiar to users with built-in services, the Google Maps API; and for complex GIS tasks, OpenLayers.\",\"// 1. Include Leaflet's CSS and JS in the HTML\\n// 2. Initialise the map\\nconst map = L.map('map-container').setView([55.751244, 37.618423], 10); // Moscow, zoom 10\\n\\n// 3. Add a tile layer (we use OpenStreetMap)\\nL.tileLayer('https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {\\n    attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'\\n}).addTo(map);\\n\\n// 4. Add a marker with a popup\\nconst marker = L.marker([55.751244, 37.618423]).addTo(map);\\nmarker.bindPopup(\\\"<b>The Kremlin</b><br>Moscow\\\").openPopup();\\n\\n// 5. Add a simple polygon (a triangle)\\nconst polygon = L.polygon([\\n    [55.75, 37.60],\\n    [55.75, 37.65],\\n    [55.70, 37.62]\\n]).addTo(map);\\npolygon.bindPopup(\\\"I am a polygon.\\\");\"],[1599,\"RTK Query is worth using directly for most operations that fetch, cache and invalidate data from the server, since it manages loading, errors and the cache automatically. Normalising data in Redux should be applied when you need to perform complex, cross-cutting updates between different entities, or to implement optimistic updates affecting several queries. RTK Query is ideal for typical CRUD, while normalisation is for complex domain models with tight relationships.\",\"RTK Query is a powerful tool for managing server state, built into Redux Toolkit. It abstracts away the logic of requests, caching, synchronisation and data updates, significantly reducing boilerplate code. Using RTK Query directly is recommended for most API scenarios, especially for standard CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations.\\nWhen to use RTK Query directly\\n\\n- Simple fetching and caching of data: RTK Query automatically caches responses, manages the subscription life cycle and prevents duplicate requests.\\n- Automatic cache invalidation: You can configure data to refresh automatically after mutations (for example, after a post is created, the list of posts is marked as stale).\\n- Standard UI patterns: It provides hooks (useQuery, useMutation) that give you isLoading and isError flags, which is ideal for displaying loading state in components.\\n\\nWhen to normalise data in Redux\\nNormalisation is the process of organising data in Redux state as dictionaries (objects) keyed by ID, with separate arrays of IDs to preserve order. It is useful when the data has complex interrelationships.\\n\\n- Complex updates across entities: For example, when a user is deleted, all their comments also need to be removed from the state.\\n- Optimistic updates: When you want to update the UI instantly and then synchronise with the server, and that update affects several types of data.\\n- Efficient UI updates: Normalisation lets selectors and React components memoise effectively, since references to unchanged objects stay the same.\\n\\nConclusion: Start by using RTK Query directly for all server interactions — that covers 90 % of cases. Move to manual data normalisation in Redux only when your application requires complex, cross-cutting state updates between different entities that are hard to express through RTK Query's automatic tag invalidation, or when maximum performance in updating related data is critically important.\",\"// An example of using RTK Query directly\\nimport { createApi, fetchBaseQuery } from '@reduxjs/toolkit/query/react';\\n\\nexport const api = createApi({\\n  baseQuery: fetchBaseQuery({ baseUrl: '/api' }),\\n  endpoints: (builder) => ({\\n    getPosts: builder.query({ query: () => '/posts' }),\\n    addPost: builder.mutation({\\n      query: (newPost) => ({\\n        url: '/posts',\\n        method: 'POST',\\n        body: newPost,\\n      }),\\n      // Automatically refreshes the cache for getPosts\\n      invalidatesTags: ['Post'],\\n    }),\\n  }),\\n});\\n\\n// In a React component\\nconst { data: posts, isLoading } = api.useGetPostsQuery();\\nconst [addPost] = api.useAddPostMutation();\\n\\n// An example of a normalised structure in Redux\\n{\\n  posts: {\\n    ids: [1, 2, 3],\\n    entities: {\\n      1: { id: 1, title: 'Post A', authorId: 101 },\\n      2: { id: 2, title: 'Post B', authorId: 102 },\\n      3: { id: 3, title: 'Post C', authorId: 101 },\\n    }\\n  },\\n  users: {\\n    ids: [101, 102],\\n    entities: {\\n      101: { id: 101, name: 'Alice' },\\n      102: { id: 102, name: 'Bob' },\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// A selector that gets a post together with its author's details\\nconst selectPostWithAuthor = (state, postId) => {\\n  const post = state.posts.entities[postId];\\n  const author = state.users.entities[post.authorId];\\n  return { ...post, author };\\n};\"],[1600,\"The main problems are poor rendering performance and heavy memory use. The browser or application can freeze trying to process thousands or millions of points, lines or polygons. There are also difficulties with managing the level of detail (zoom levels) and filtering the data. The solutions used are clustering, geometry simplification, lazy loading and WebGL acceleration.\",\"Rendering a large amount of geodata is a typical task for mapping services, GIS applications or analytics dashboards. The problems begin when the number of objects (points, routes, outlines) exceeds the platform's capacity for smooth rendering.\\n\\nKey performance problems\\n\\n- Slow rendering: Every graphical element requires drawing calls in Canvas, SVG or WebGL. Thousands of elements lead to blocking of the main thread and a drop in FPS.\\n\\n- High memory consumption: Holding detailed geometries (for example, polygons with thousands of vertices) in the browser's or the client application's memory can exhaust the limits.\\n\\n- Difficulties with navigation: When zooming you need to show/hide objects dynamically depending on the level of detail, which requires additional computation.\\n\\n- Transferring data over the network: Downloading the full dataset can be slow and traffic-heavy.\\n\\nApproaches to optimisation\\n\\nSeveral strategies are used to fight these problems:\\n\\n- Clustering: Grouping closely located points into a single visual cluster at low zoom levels.\\n\\n- Simplification: Reducing the number of vertices in lines and polygons using algorithms (for example, the Douglas-Peucker algorithm), especially at distant scales.\\n\\n- Lazy loading and pagination: Loading and rendering only the data visible in the map's current viewport.\\n\\n- Using WebGL: Libraries such as Mapbox GL JS or Deck.gl use the GPU to render millions of points, which is significantly more efficient than SVG/Canvas.\\n\\n- Pre-aggregating the data on the server: The server can compute simplified tiles or aggregated statistics for different zoom levels in advance.\\n\\nCode example: simple clustering on the client\\n\\nThis code demonstrates the basic idea, although in production more efficient spatial indexes are used (for example, a k-d tree).\\n\\nConclusion: Optimising the rendering of large geodata is critical for creating responsive cartographic interfaces. The approach depends on the task: for static data, geometry simplification will do; for interactive real-time maps, clustering and WebGL. It is always worth considering offloading the computation to the server and using specialised libraries (Leaflet with plugins, Mapbox GL, Deck.gl).\",\"// A simplified example of clustering points\\nfunction clusterPoints(points, zoom) {\\n    const clusterDistance = 100 / zoom; // The distance depends on the zoom level\\n    const clusters = [];\\n    const used = new Set();\\n\\n    for (let i = 0; i < points.length; i++) {\\n        if (used.has(i)) continue;\\n        const cluster = { points: [points[i]], x: points[i].x, y: points[i].y };\\n        used.add(i);\\n\\n        // Look for nearby points\\n        for (let j = i + 1; j < points.length; j++) {\\n            if (used.has(j)) continue;\\n            const dx = points[j].x - points[i].x;\\n            const dy = points[j].y - points[i].y;\\n            if (Math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy) < clusterDistance) {\\n                cluster.points.push(points[j]);\\n                used.add(j);\\n            }\\n        }\\n        clusters.push(cluster);\\n    }\\n    // Return the cluster centres and the number of points in each\\n    return clusters.map(c => ({\\n        x: c.x,\\n        y: c.y,\\n        count: c.points.length\\n    }));\\n}\\n\\n// Usage: instead of 10000 points we render a few hundred clusters\\nconst originalPoints = [...]; // an array of {x, y}\\nconst zoomLevel = 5;\\nconst clustersToRender = clusterPoints(originalPoints, zoomLevel);\"]]");export{e as default};